        <p>THE RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL OF RUNCORN. GRAPPENHALL, LATCHFORD WITHOUT, AND THELWALL SEWERAGE. CONTRACT No. 1. The Runcorn Rural District Council intite TENDERS for the above works, consisting of Steel Tube, Cast Iron and Earthenware Pipe Sewers, together with Flushing Tanks, Storm Overflows, Manholes, Ventilators, Sedimenta- tion Tanks, Percolating Filter (including Rein- forced Concrete Construction on the Kahn System), Engine and Pump House, Pump Well, *id other appurtenant works in connection rewith. !lane and Specifications may be seen and Bill Quantities, Schedule of Prices and Form of ;ender may be obtained at the Offices of the Engineers, Messrs. James Diggle and Son, 14, Victoria-street, Westminster, S.W., and Hind Hill-street, Heywood, Lancashire, on and after Monday, the 13th day of July, 1914, on pay- ment of a deposit of Five Pounds (.0 Os. O(1.), which will be returned on receipt of a bona-fide tender. Sealed tenders, endorsed "Grappenhall, &amp;c., Sewerage, Contract No. 1," addressed to the undersigned, are to be delivered not later than Friday. the 7th day of August, 1914. The Council do not bind themserges to libirlpt the lowest or any tender. GEO. F. ASHTON, Clerk to the Council. Council Offices, 71, High-street, Runcorn, 2nd .Tuly, 1914.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON UNION. TO BUILDERS, CONTRACTORS, &amp;c. • The Guardians of the Warrington Union are prepared to receive TENDERS for Building Repairs required to be done at the Union Offices, 67, Market-street, Earlestown. The Standard Rate of Wages must be paid for all labour engaged on the work. Specification of the work required to be done and Form of Tender may be obtained from the undersigned, and applications for same must be made not later than Wednesday, the 22nd inst. Tenders endorsed "Tender for Building Re- pairs," must be received by.me not later than 5 p.m. on Tuesday, the 28th instant. The Guardians do not bind themselves to accept the lowest or any Tender. By order, • ARTHUR BOTTOMLEY, Clerk. Bewsey Chambers, Warrington. 11th July, 1914.</p>
