      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <p>lettrrs to the OhUlm</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. TELE EDITOR of "The Examiner" invites corre- spondence on all subjects of public interest. LETTERS should be brief and pointed. Their object must be to impart or seek information. not abuse. LErrsus must in all eases be accompanied by the name and address of the writer. COMMUNICATIONS received early in the week— except urgent matters—can always be given the best attention.</p>
        <p>SALVATION ARMY WALKING DAY TREAT. Sir,—Will you please permit me, through the medium of your valuable paper, to thank the many friends who have again made it possible for us to entertain just upon 1,100 children in the Bank Park on Walking Day? After the rain ceased we had a real good time with the children, and everything passed off very satisfactory. JOSEPH BEAUMONT, - Adjutant, Salvation Army. „2.5, Froghall-lane, July Bth, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>MILITARISM AND CONSCIENCE.</head>
        <p>MILITARISM AND CONSCIENCE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Whilst dissociating pr•litical significance from the incidents at the Cui..agh Camp—l pre- fer to leave this an open question—attention ought to be drawn to the revolutionary concep- tion of the officers, viz., the right to question the Government to compel them to fight in a quarrel which they consider unjust. I welcome this attitude, which was endorsed by Lord Roberts in the House of Lords last Monday. His Lordship's speech plainly indicates the anom- alies of militarism when exposed to the light of conscience. .</p>
        <p>The situation is fundamentally revolutionary. The issues involved are more far-reaching than can be measured or seen. It cuts at the base of the whole fabric of militarism. It is gener- ally assumed that a soldier does not legally possess this power of denial. He is not sup- nosed to know of the rights, or the wrongs, of the quarrel. Legally, when a man becomes a soldier, he allows himself to be converted into a fighting machine. So when a soldier begins to examine his position in the light of con- science, he finds himself in a painful and con- tradictory situation. If the officers at the Curragh are justified— and I believe them to be justified—it becomes necessary to carry this new and unprecedented adoption of a sacred principle to its logical con- clusion. Militarism stands self-condemned. Resignation from an impossible position would inevitably and morally follow. I have much respect for the soldier. Many of them are men of high principle. Chivalry and sacrifice are not the least of their virtues. But I believe them to be misguided. The great qualities of character are wanted on other fields than the battlefield. To refuse to fight at the behest of politicians, diplomatists and financiers, because they have failed to adjust their differences, is to perform the act not of a coward, but of a man. of principle. In closing, these officers should remember that life, not only in Ulster, but elsewhere, is equally sacred. God gives life, and to Him only must its withdrawal be committed.</p>
        <p>Ti. GRAY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>SURGERY IN A TWO-INCH BOX.</head>
        <p>A SURGERY IN A TWO-INCH BOX.</p>
        <p>The Usefulness &amp; 'Reliability of Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk has been aptly described as " a surgery in a two-inch box." Its wide range of usefulness and its unvarying reliability are being daily demonstrated. Every home in the British Isles needs Zam- Buk, as scarcely a day passes without some minor accident, or else some skin trouble occur- ring which can be safely, swiftly and effectively treated by this unique preparation. Zam-Buk is a rare and valuable herbal balm, skilfully refined, balanced and concentrated. It is manufactured in a beautifully-equipped labor- atory by certain complicated and highly- specialised processes which are known alone to the proprietors, and which take some three months to-finally complete. Zam-Buk contains no lard or other animal fat, and in this and every other respect is vastly superior to all common ointments. Both as a " First-Aid " for superficial wounds and as a sure cure for eczema, ringworm, and summer skin disorders, Zam-Buk is absolutely un- equalled. Sold only in sealed boxes bearing the regis- tered name "Zam-Buk."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>JEW CATHOLIC cmum4 FOR</head>
        <p>JEW CATHOLIC cmum4 FOR</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
