        <p>,yi—iremony. place at three p.m. ue building will \he ,ths. The church . will and -ost is</p>
        <p>PRESIDENT THANKED. Dr. W. Evans Hoyle, Cardiff, proposed a hearty vote of thanks to the President, whom it was a source of great pleasure to see in the presidential chair that year. Mr. H. M. Platnauer (ex-president) seconded, and said that the President, in his address, had asked a question and answered it as fully, systematically, and comprehensively as any man could do.</p>
        <p>The resolution was carried with acclamation. The Mayor entertained the delegates to luncheon, and at this his Worship proposed the health of the Museums' Association and its President (Mr. Madeley). Mr. Madeley replied, and proposed the health of the Mayor and Corporation, which was drunk with enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>In connection with the visit of a woman (Mrs. Ratcliffe, of Australia) with a revolver to Buck- tingham Palace Mr. Joshua Ratcliff, recently arris=ed from Australia, explains that she was not his wife. •</p>
        <p>The strike of 1,800 Mannesmann tube workers in the Swansea district, which had lasted a fort- night, has been settled, the four firemen who were the bone of contention having joined the' G a s workers' tnion..</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, etc.</p>
        <p>The Speed of Trams. There is one point which the Glasgow city authorities should take into consideration, and it is with reference to the speed of the tramcars on the roads leading out from the centre of the city. I came across a case recently in which a motor-cyclist was summoned for driving at 28 miles an hour through a trap, and in his evidence he brought the information that a tramcar had passed him when he was being timed. I can quite believe this, for I know that one has to travel at least 25 miles an hour to keep level with some of the trams on the out- lying roads, and a speed of 30 miles is not uncommon. A motor-car is more easily con- trolled than a tram that is running on lines, and therefore if there is to be any lenience shown it should be in favour of the motorist. If for the public convenience the speed limit is to be ignored for trams I can see no reason why motorists should be hauled up for travelling a little above the legal speed. lam not suggest- ing that trams should be penalised, but only that we should receive fair play and be treated in a similar spirit.</p>
