      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>LATE MR. JOSEPH TWISS.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. JOSEPH TWISS.</p>
        <p>Interred at Hill Cliffe Cemetery.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place on Monday of Mr. Joseph Twiss, who passed away at his residence —Stafford-road—on Thursday wk, as an- nounced in " The Examiner." Mr. Twiss was tbe third and youngest son of the late Mr. Edward Twiss, and had almost completed his 63rd year. The interment took place at the Hill Cliffe burial ground, and the service in the chapel and at the graveside were conducted by Mr. William Twiss, brother of the deceased. A large number of friends assembled, whose pre- sence was eloquent of the regard and high esteem in which Mr. Twiss was held. Among those present were: Mrs. Twiss (widow), the Misses Maggie, Ruth and Rhoda Twiss (daugh- ters), Mr. William Twiss (brother), Mr. and Mrs. Shepherd, Mr. Caleb Twiss, Mrs. Edward Twiss, Mr. and Mrs. Walter Twiss, Mr. Ernest Clark, Mrs. W. J. Twiss, Mr. Summerfield, Mr. Jack Gibson, Mr. F. W. Wright, Mr. and Mrs. Albert Wright, Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Wright, Mr. and Mrs. J. Longshaw, Mr. and Mrs. Henry Eyes, Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Hewitt, Mr. and Mrs. R. Storey, Messrs. George Smith (repre- senting Messrs. Henry Greenall and Co.), Joseph Knowles, Goodall, Eli James, Henry Roberts, John Lunt, H. Osborne; E. England, William' Mercer (Lymm), James Hudson, John Hudson, and W. Fairhurst. Mr. A. Mounfield had charge of the funeral arrangements. In a brief address during the service in the chapel, Mr. William Twiss said that the cir- cumstances under which they met were of a very affecting nature. Deceased was very dear not only to himself, but also to those who knew how his friendship endeared him to all with whom he came in contact. They had to submit to these losses, as friend after friend departed; but if that fact made them serious, many had been beneficially affected by it. His brother had departed with the sure and blessed hope of a resurrection. Right to the last there had been no change in that trust, and thus he had been prepared for a peaceful de- parture.</p>
        <p>• By an action in the Scottish courts a Glasgow boile3•maker is to test the legality of a is. levy on all members of the Boilermakers' Society for " The Daily Citizen" and the investment of society funds in the same paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAYS JULY 11, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAYS JULY 11, 1914.</p>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>RULES FOR FARMERS.</p>
        <p>The Milk Certification Committee of the PUlt Food and Health Society of Great Britain have drawn up a list of rule; to which farmers and dairymen willing to sell milk under the seal of the society are asked to agree :- 1. No milk to be sold from any cow that is for any reason not in good health and under physic, or with 'sore teats of affected udder. 2. Cows to be tuberculin tested. In accepting</p>
        <p>the evidence of a tuberculin test it will be neces- sary to state that no earlier tuberculin test has been made within six weeks. 3. No new cow's milk to be sold until it has been bacteriologically examined. 4. Veterinary supervision to be made periodic- ally at a fixed rate if not already made by the local authority, or the farmer, or the dairy, and a copy of the exact report whenever and however made to be sent to the society. 5. All milk to be strained at the farm, and at least once through clean absorbent cotton cheese-cloth.</p>
        <p>_ . 6. Provision to be made, which shall be ap- proved by the society, for cooling all milk to at least 45 deg. to 55 deg. P. immediately after milking unless the milk is to be " pasteurised" or separated.</p>
        <p>7. Provision to be made for cleaning and sterilising with live steam all utensils imme- diately after use, and such utensils to be used for no other purpose than for milk vessels. 8. Provision to be made for each milker to wash his or her hands with carbolic soap or other disinfectant in clean water previous to milking, also clean towels for drying the hands to be provided.</p>
        <p>9. The drainage of all cowsheds to be carefully , supervised. All cow-sheds to be lime-washed at least once every half-year. Cowsheds to be made and kept well lighted, well ventilated, and clean without noticeable odour. 10. To cleanse cows whenever necessary and to scrape hindquarters and brush down generally at least half an hour before milking. Each cow's udder shall be wiped with a clean cloth kept for that purpose before each milking. 11. To wear clean overalls when milking and also linen caps, which are to be changed at least twice a week. All milkers to be periodically medically inspected and to give an undertaking to report sickness at home. 12. The milking can shall be covered imme- diately the cows are milked. It is strongly recommended that it shall not have a larger opening than Bin. in diameter and should be covered with a hood, and that the churn should have a flush or convex top, and should be rimmed outside. 13. The natural standard of milk to• be left• unaffected. Absolute prohibition of any addi- tion or abstraction from milk, whether colour- ing or any substance or liquid. 14. For Dairies.—A certificate as to proper drainage. Rooms for storing utensils uncon- nected with living or sleeping rooms. Plentiful supply of fresh, clean water from an approved source. Concrete floors or floors easily cleaned. A sterilising or provision of live steam apparatus and also a cleansing apparatus shall be in- stalled. Milk only to be sold in glass bottles, which shall be sealed before leaving the dairy and not plugged only with pulp covers. Pro- vision against leaving receptacles for milk standing in the street. Thorough, cleansing of bottles, etc., and supervision of roundsmen. Distinct labelling of "sterilised," " pasteur- ised," or " separated " milk.</p>
        <p>QUALITY OF MILK. When many years ago some of us agitated for a milk standard, our desire was to increase the quality of milk; but while breeders have im- proved the quality materially, I doubt if the milk supplied by the great majority of retailers in some parts of the country has improved at all, for the standard, as a standard, has not achieved the object in view. Many leading and other traders, however, have, quite apart from the 3 per cent. figaie, built up their businesses- by supplying a richer article, and now we find that sound milk can be provided on a large scale, as witness the latest record, 7611 b., given by the winning cow at Portsmouth in one day, this milk containing 3.7 per cent, of fat, a figure which numerous competitors at the Lon- don Dairy Show have exceeded.—James Long in "Farm and Home."</p>
        <p>esses</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>MILK RECORDS.</head>
        <p>MILK RECORDS.</p>
        <p>_ The first and greatest advantage in the use of milk records is the increase of herd yields by weeding out poor milkers and substituting• bet- ter ones, costing no more to maintain than their predecessors, but largely increasing the return of milk from the herd. I have known a case from personal experience where, in a herd of 120 milking Ayrshires, the increase in the herd average which attended careful weeding out and selection was put down at .2150 per annum with- in eight years of taking the first record.— "Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>This is about the time when Potato disease attracts notice. It spreads quickest during a wet season, but in dry seasons sufficient mis- chief can be done as to entail some loss.' Downy yellow patches betoken diseased plants. The best remedy is to spray with Bordeaux Mixture. Sawdust soaked in paraffin oil and scattered on the ground will keep all flies of all ;plants. A good wrinkle for holiday-going people whose plants require water during their absence, is to fill a big jam jar or other vessel with water. Range the pots round the jar, and place a piece of thick wool from the jar to each pot, sinking the end of the wool in the soil. This will keep the plants uniformly moist for a fortnight or so. Before finally earthing up Potatoes scatter a little nitrate of soda of guano along the rows. Here is a remedy for "blight": Soak two pieces of batlibrick in paraffin, dry and rub into a fine powder, which distributed over the plants will be found a useful remedy. Thin out fruits gradually where they are too crowded. Put a good mulch of manure around the roots, after opening the soil with fork or hoe. A good watering afterwards will be help- ful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>VEGETABLE PLOT.</head>
        <p>THE VEGETABLE PLOT.</p>
        <p>Thin Carrots, Spinach, Beet, —Turnips, etc. Soot water is a good stimulant. Hoe between Potatoes and continue to earth up.</p>
        <p>When the soil is moist plant Brussels Sprouts, autumn Cauliflowers, and autumn protecting Broccoli, allowing two feet between ,each plant and 21 feet between the rows. Sow more French or Dwarf Beans. Growth will be rapid and only a short length of stem has to be made before they commence flowering. Tie up the main stems of Tomatoes, and re- move all side growths and suckers from the base. Give moisture, but do not feed until a few bunches of fruit have set.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>IDEAL SWEET PEA.</head>
        <p>THE IDEAL SWEET PEA.</p>
        <p>The standard of excellence, as set up in our Premier shows, would appear to be size. The standard of excellence, however, for a dinner- table is artistic merit, balance, quality, per- fume. Which is it to be? Are we always to aim at growing blooms with stems over two feet in length and the thickness of a pencil, with Cabbage-sized leaves, and with the blooms dis- tributed about half-way down the stem, as 'seen at our shows? Or shall we grow more 'natur- ally, on delicate stems, with-well arranged frag- rant .bloorini`at one end? Look at the 'question how we will, it resolves itself into one of coarse- ness versus refinement, and it is to be sincerely hoped that the latter will win the Clay..--" Agr- icultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
