      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>coming their increase, admiring their enter- prise, and making many suggestions for their development, in detail, the association has made no attempt to define the essential ele- ments or the reasonable limits of a municipal museum. .</p>
        <p>As to the conditions attaching to varibus museums, no two museums developed along exactly similar lines, and apart from that, many museums in their early stages moved under the accidental impulse of special circum- stances that did not occur elsewhere; local con- , ditions, the transfer of the collections of a local, society; the kindly thought of a generous bene- factor, may combine to start and direct the young institution with any plan in the midst of the local authorities.</p>
        <p>A Miniature Universe.</p>
        <p>The object woulti not be to dictate to the local authorities or the Curator what must neces- sarily be attempted, or to suppress or in any way limit local enthusiasm or individual orig- inality, but to provide a body of theory so com- plete as to cover all possible variations on the general theme. What was the definition of a museum?</p>
        <p>The answer must be wide enough to cover all the departments of any known museum, and even any which may arise in the future. Mr. Platnauer, in his presidential address, three years ago, gave a concise definition in the words: "A museum is a collection of speci- mens arranged on educational lines and with an educational purpose." At first sight it ap- peared to be a very broad statement, but he (the President) submitted that it was not broad enough. Why drag in education? He gave his own definition of the ideal museum, which, he said, was a miniature or synopsis of the universe—the true microcosm. But the great and essential difference was that in the museum things were classified, and therefore intelligible, whilst in the world out- side they were not.</p>
        <p>Functions of a Museum. He then dealt with the functions of a museum and alluded to the definition of Dr. Bather, "instruction, investigation, and inspir- ation," and to the dictum of Mr. Cameron that "museums are intended to stimulate the in- stinctive desire for knowledge in man." But that, whilst recognising one of the main reasons for the museum's existence, stops short of its complete function, which required that some effort be made to satisfy the craving which it excited.</p>
        <p>The -President dwelt on the importance of collections of economic products, and said that now that degrees'in applied science are being granted by the universities, they may hope that technology and eventually even commerce may meet with adehuate recognition in the museum. i It must be so if the museum was to assist n the advance of every branch of human know- ledge, of every laudable human interest. At present, with some important exceptions in Scotland, technology was hardly recognised; in their municipal museums, and commerce not at all. The President concluded by dealing with questions of classification.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>The twelve months' test which is being carried out at the Harper Adams Agricultural College, Newport, Salop, has reached the end of the eighth period of four weeks. The test com- prises fifty pens of six birds each, and the breeds which up to the present have made the best records have been the White Wyandottes and White Leghorns. The 300 birds during the 28 days have averaged 16.63 eggs each, which gives a total of 4,991 eggs. Owing to the season and weather conditions 124 birds have been broody, so that this has lessened the total some- what. The leghorns during the month have done exceptionally well; there are 17 pens of them which have, averaged 118.4 per pen, and 19.7 eggs per bird. Mr. J. N. Leigh, the manager of the competi- tion at Sedlescombe, in his report for the eighth period of four weeks states that for the first time since the commencement of the competi- tion the month's results show a falling off in the output of eggs when compared with the pre- vious month—the figures being 4,501 eggs as against 4,807 for the previous month—a de- crease of 306 eggs. The decline is entirely on the part of the heavy breeds and is attributable to excessive broodiness) no less than 106 birds out of a total of 152 having been affected, and also to moulting, which, although very early in the season, has become somewhat prevalent. The moulting is more noticeable in Section 11. The month's results show that the leading pen in each section has maintained its position, namely, in Section I. pen No. 7, White Leg- horns with a score of 592 eggs, value .X 3 Bs. 6d. for the 32 weeks, and in Section 11, pen No. 21 White Wyandottes, with a score of 587 eggs value .63 4s. 111. for the same period. There have been many changes amongst the other pens, White Leghorns generally taking higher places.</p>
        <p>• Tt-JE BEST SUMMER FOODS. The best summer meals are middlings,-ground oats, and bran. Middlings is an excellent food, possessing a good feeding value, yet not being heating, as some of the other meals are. It is really the half-way stage between flour and bran; it may be called very coarse flour or very fine bran. Ground oats, while it possesses a splendid feeding value, is rather too expensive for or- dinary use. Its chief value is for fattening. A good ration for use during the summer months consists of two parts middlings, one part ground oats, and one part bran. Wheat and oats are the beat grains for fowls during warm weather. In the case of the former "tail" wheat answers the purposecapitally, and it is very much cheaper. A good sample of oats, however, must be procured, for poor-quality grain consists largely of husk.</p>
        <p>FEEDING GEESE. Geese are such extremely large eaters that it is necessary if all the profit is not to be con- sumed to exercise the utmost care in feeding them, and expensive grains and meals should be avoided. Owing, too, to the fact that prices are not so high for the finished specimens as was the-case a few years ago, it is necessary to keep down the cost of feeding to the lowest limit. As far as possible natural foods should be employed, as not only are they more econo- mical but the birds appear to thrive better thereupon. Geese are grazing birds, and when they have access to a good meadow or rich arable land they are able to almost support themselves. When they have not access to a good pasture it is necessary to provide them with two meals a day. First thing in the morn- ing a supply of warm mash should be pro- vided, and an excellent mixture consists of two parts barley meal, one middlings, and half a part bean meal. The same mixture is quite suitable feeding in the afternoon, though some breeders prefer to give grain. Personally, I prefer the former, unless the grain has pre- viously been soaked for several hours, in which case it will be well swollen out and more easily digested by the birds. In arable districts where good pasture is at a premium, many goose farmers are in the habit of growing green crops merely for the geese, one of the best being oats. When the blades are about ten inches above the ground the geese are turned in, and thrive excellently upon the tender and succulent green food. In East Anglia, where so many geese are reared, it is customary to feed off roots by geese in the same manner as by sheep. I was recently assured by a large Suffolk farmer that the birds ate the roots as close to the ground as did the sheep. anrd, what was equally important, man- ured the land as well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>Speed Limits and Some Tram Car Examples.</p>
        <p>THE COURTESY OF ROAD USERS</p>
        <p>I have referred from time to time in this column to that class of motorists whom we , designate as road-hogs, but I am now. convinced 'that even that section of the motoring com- munity should not have all the blame attached to them. For some time past I have been gathering information on this point, and it has been impressed on me that times out of number there are cyclists who are guilty of something very similar to road-hogism. We as cyclists know that we have the right of the roads, but all the same there are certain obligations which we must fulfil if we expect proper treatment from motorists. Human nature being what it is, this is bound to bring reprisals in its train, as I have noted in a large number of cases cyclists deliberately obstruct motorists. Again, groups of riders have no business to ride two, three, or four abreast on a much frequented road; lastly, itds not their prerogative to mono- polise the centre of the road, for their place is on the near side of the road. Of all classes of road users perhaps the motor cyclist is the worst , offender, since he has a 'speedy machine, and, moreover, one that can be controlled with ease. The Swedish Cyclists' Union. _ _</p>
        <p>The international road race from Stockholm to Goeteburg and back is being held by the Swedish Cyclists' Union, and they have issued invitations to amateurs belonging to all coun- tries affiliated with the Union Cycliste Inter- nationale. The race is to begin at 4 a.m. on July 24. The prizes are the International Chal- lenge Cup, to be presented to the representatives of the country that scores best point; gold, silver, and bronze medals, and certificates. A special point to notice is that according to the regulations of the Swedish Cyclists' Union every competitor must submit to a medical test the day before the race. This invitation gives our road-racing men an opportunity of spending a most enjoyable time in Sweden, and also of adding to the laurels they have already won. The capital of the country is amongst the most interesting cities in the world, and as the Swedish people are noted for their hospitality a good trip is assured. I have toured through the country and I can, therefore, say from my own experience that anyone going will have a right royal time.</p>
        <p>Motor Sledges for Polar Exploration. A very interesting series of tests has just been completed by Sir Ernest Shackleton to deter- mine the value of the outfit which he proposes to take with him when he sets out this summer for his great journey across the Antarctic. A special tent has been designed, and this proved highly successful. Food trials have also been made, and much valuable information has been collected in this connection. I am more in- terested, however, in the news of the motor sledges that have been tried. These are driven by an aerial propellor, and although one showed signs of constructional weakness the other was found capable of carrying heavy loads through a conside7.able amount of snow at an average rate of 15 miles an hour. No better place could have been selected for the trials than Finse, Norway, and the weather was ex- ceptionally inclement for this time of the year. Every motorist realises that the difficulties to be surmounted with motor traction over vast fields of ice and snow are enormous, but it appears as though a machine has been designed that will facilitate the work of 'conveying the heavy loads that are indispensable to the well- being of the Polar parties.</p>
        <p>The Austrian Alpine Trial. A surprise has been sprung on the competitors in the Austrian trial, and one that will not be to their liking. It has been decided at the last moment to include a hill called the Turracher- hoehe, in one of the last days run as well as at Katschberg. The latter is bad enough, but the former will render it almost impossible for a number of the cars to finish. It is not only that the road is narrow—so much so that if one car sticks the remainder will be held up—but the surface is bad, the gradient is so great that a correspondent of the " Abendsblat " found that his gradometer was of no use, as it only recorded up to 30 per cent., and, finally, half-way up, there is a wooden bridge that has to be nego- tiated which is of doubtful strength. The last days of the trial should be interesting—for those who are not competing—as the number of cars that will be weeded out on the Katschberg will be considerable, and the proportion of those remaining that will assuredly come to grief on the dreaded Turracherhoehe will be so great as to leave very few to complete the course. In face of this, however, it must be conceded that although the trial will be severe the majority of the cars that are entered cannot be termed normal, in that they are constructed for Alpine work. The rules governing the test are, how- ever, sensible, and therefore the result should be of great interest to all motorists.</p>
        <p>Glasgow and the Speed Limit. The City of Glasgow authorities are up against a hard proposition with regard to the congestion of traffic in the main thoroughfares. In a city like Glasgow, with its huge network of trams all converging on one or two principal streets and the congested condition of the main roads and the streets leading thereto, it will be no light task to formulate regulations that over- come the grave difficulties that are apparent at present. The suggestion before the authorities, however, of fixing a ten-mile speed limit for motor-cars is certainly not a solution that is likely to assist matters. If proper precautions are taken the only way in which the difficulty can be met is by speeding up the traffic, not in- sisting on a low rate such as is proposed. The many regrettable accidents that have happened recently have no doubt caused this question to be raised again, and with a certain amount of reason; but what is really required is to have more police supervision in those roads leading up to the main arteries. I have nothing to say against the way in which the Glasgow police perform their duties, but a larger number is wanted, and this would rapidly stop any reck- lessness on the part of that section of the motor- ing community that is known for its careless driving.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>CYCLISTS' NOTEBOOK.</head>
        <p>THE CYCLISTS' NOTEBOOK.</p>
        <p>Fine Weather and Splendid</p>
      </div>
