        <head>Pi SUNDAY WALKING DAY.</head>
        <p>Pi SUNDAY WALKING DAY.</p>
        <p>Fine Weather.</p>
        <p>MINIMISING FRIDAY'S DISAPPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>- The Catholic children and adults, who were t.'h grivol-181Y disappointed on Walking Day, übg'i the miserable weather conditions ikLe' .Prevailed in the morning and made it dar-issible for them to take their share in that (128 Procession, were filled with joy on &amp;Un- euf' when they were able to carry through a Cittthai,ll Programme. Three out of the five Churches in the borough "proces- Rea,;`: on Sunday afternoon. The Sacred fld Our Lady's held a brief procession '-'rtua3r, traversing a few streets in their</p>
        <p>I)botogra</p>
        <p>parishes, and therefore did not take the function of Sunday. ital4g to the inconvenience of holding up the 4-11 c °ll Sunday, the usual route through the Iv Was not followed ; each church confined lit4-1k to its own parish, and through the h ng UP of thle procession into three a A IZahle amount of the spectacular value tra4,7.d. Each church, however, presented an 'lye spectacle, and the route taken by Waa lined with interested spectators. Si. MARY'S, BUTTERMARKET. Procession, which began at St. Mary's and ended at St. Mary's Church, when eltediction was pronounced, was of impos- Icaensions, though not so ornate as Usual. 'sett 1,100 and 1,200 children and adults took the Sunday School children being wig- :4 by the Catholic Boys' Brigade, St. 8 Eiranch of the Catholic Young Men's and the members of the League of the</p>
        <p>The Catholic Schools Parade Their Parishes in</p>
        <p>boys dressed in white, with pink sashes, whilst a number of other boys similarly attired acted as a bodyguard. The Children of Mary with the banner and statue of Our Lady slowly marched behind. The statue stood under an arrangement of ferns and was carried by four girls who wer,e accompanied by others, all dressed in white. The rear of the procession came with the mixed department conveying the statue of the Sacred Heart.</p>
        <p>The 600 scholars and 15 teachers were all dressed appropriately for the occasion, and</p>
