      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>ST. ALBAN'S.</head>
        <p>ST. ALBAN'S.</p>
        <p>t4-1* A li, , the ,eolo,l4stetions were uniformly dressed, and il)eih'hlldt treatment made a n 'effect. litt,:l pe,:,rl of Mary carrying lillielseaaslg and smilax, A 4re. -atularly noticeable in their chaste qLtltclecl tVle League of the Cross prize Band 1 Ild was° Procession, and the Boys' Brigade Viiieerefficwieuntnoauasttielimaryp h matter t to traverse the („rtz'sets of the town, the idea being .to t. -4:8 I,learly as possible, round the parish , r 37 8. Preceded by Father Wilson, who</p>
        <p>VI by "The Daily Sketch," London and Manchester. CHILDREN OF MARY. St. Benedices.</p>
        <p>coloured costumes, which added to the pic- turesqueness of the scene. A cross-bearer and acotyles headed the pro- , cession, and they were immediately followed by the band and the banner of St. Benedict. Then came- a large cross—composed of ferns from which a number of lilies hung down their heads —carried by a young lady in white, whilst sur- rounding her were a dozen mites in long white gowns and equipped with wings. A statue of the Divine Infant was carried by eight little</p>
        <p>There was awful slum property in Warrington. Ile could take them to places where there were 60 or 70 houses to the acre, and where houses were being built 40 or 50 to the acre. Yet for a person to live his allotted span in life no more than 20 or 30 houses should be built to the acre. If they really knew all that the Liberal party had in hand for the benefit of the working classes they would be only too glad to vote Liberal. TAey should return Mr. Crosfield, the true democratic candidate, to further their interests in Parliament and to help to build up a better, more prosperous and brighter Eng- land. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>St. Alban's School made a splendid muster of lbout 400, in charge of eleven teachers, and the Youngsters passed merrily along to the strains of the Borough Band. The arrangements: were superintended by the Very Rev. J. P. Whittle, the Revs. P. L. Buggins and R. J. Lane Fox. The crucifix, which headed the procession, was followed by the choir boys in surplices and caps. The clergy preceded the banner of St. Alban, and next came a shield, prettily decorated with red roses, and followed by the infants, all in white, with vine-leaf girdles and long purple ribbons, hanging from the shoulders. The boys wore striped purple and yellow caps. The banner of the Virgin headed the senior</p>
        <p>girls who were prettily adorned in uniform colours, consisting of smilax sprays, intertwined with helio ribbon and dainty buttercup wreaths. The girls carried bunches of lilies, and sur- rounded the statue of the Virgin, decorated with lilies and ferns. The boys had white shirts and red sashes, and the senior lads followed the banner of the Blessed Sacrament. The adult members of the church brought up the rear of a very fine procession. A Service was later held in the church.</p>
