      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>lERALS AND PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>lERALS AND PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>35es Fulfilled and Programme to be Carried Through.</p>
        <p>PROGRESSIVE LABOUR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>.411 et 41°4 olwag another good attendance at Bridge lionday evening, when the open-air j 4 1,41! held under the auspices of the Progres- crofqtlll".League were resumed. The meetings " ceommence at 7.30 instead of eight j- and finish before nine. On Monday Skit ohnson presided, and Mr. A. H. Ainley, °l'd. was the speaker.</p>
        <p>A FALSE PROPHET. I.Afte Free Trade Triumphant. i..'ktt•Clii...lllaking a touching reference to the late, '"itil . utuberlain, the CHAIRMAN said that he lino! ~p„„14 the the great Papers a speech which rot.s,e of Tariff Reform"_ made years ago. The tOa li 411, ad now shelved Tariff Reform, but . 1,4'i54;1,,8 '3,i Air. Chamberlain, itflou,roisghit part t 1/44 ,11 he said: 'lf we want t our colonies we must put a tax on tZ ' )5111',:l '4el'el The murder was Out. Sugar going, is li 11'001 isiron has gone, and steel a t4t°stt." Athough Mr. Chamberlain was alt' tl • - eqoan he (the speaker) could not help °14411.1t that in another respect he was a false years had gone b since that 1;Nli, oolte y Qnstead of ag oila•lid now with Free Trade, 4140 s i a the decline, trade had prospered by t INv " bounds. (Hear, hear.) They must k1,.i.,1110tt.,i,ek t-o- Free Trade, which was so hew- IltP4Ys B " trade of the country. They should kr!) at 111Vort the Liberal party, which did not '4 aae reform and did not break .its tI4 pro- to th e' ,e People. (Applause) I them to vote Liberal and support 144.'3' which was going to abolish slums. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>LIBERAL REFORMS CARRIED.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL REFORMS CARRIED.</p>
        <p>MY. AINLEY associated himself with the re- marks of the Chairman in regard to Mr. Cham- berlain„and said he admired him for his fight- ing powers, and the way he fought hard for the causes which he conscientiously believed to be right. After giving a brief survey of what the Liberal party had done in the past, the speaker said they were there to justify their position as a party. A distinguished American had said that no Government in any country at any period in the world's history had carried so many reforms as had the present administration. There was a greatideal of truth in that, and a 'great deal of force. They could justify their position to the full by what they had done. They could go into the various questions which they had tackled from time to time and form a splendid defence, if they wanted to defend. They were not only on the defensive, but were also on the attack. They must have some line of attack, and although ho claimed that the work done by the present administratinn entitled it to their full support, he would say that ast a pro- gressive force in the country they must have something more in the future, and let the people know what they were likely to achieve when they were sent back to Parliament. Liberals had got something good to put before the people, and a full programme to get through. Tackling the Land Problem. They were tackling the land question. During three decades they had had a very large decrease in the number of people employed on the land; whilst that decrease had been taking place they</p>
        <p>had an increased number of people employed in keeping the land and preserving it for sport. They were going to prevent that decrease going on, and so stop the agriculturists from leaving the country districts and either entering the towns to get better remuneration or going to the Colonies or places abroad. The present condition of things did not appeal to them, and they were going to alter the system which was responsible for it. There was plenty of room for the reformer to take into consideration what was the cause of that extraordinary position in which they found themselves. - Other countries had developed their land system, seeing that it was the source of all wealth, and they had brought about a different condition of things to what had existed previously. (Applause.) After enumerating the evils which would be swept away by the Liberal land proposals in the direction of better wages, decent houses, a better rating system, security of 'tenure, and making land easier of acquisition by local authorities, the speaker said that land reform was one of the best put forward for many years for it had so many branches and affected so many interests. They: were now in a position to carry such reforms, because they had got the other party clown. (Hear, hear.) They must do their best to encourage the Liberal party to do what they could to improve the conditions of the working classes throughout the country. (Applause.) • "JOE" AND HOME RULE.</p>
        <p>There were no„questions asked, and Mr. J. A. HEYWOOD proposed a hearty vote of thanks. He said tllat he was one who could not say that Mr. Chamberlain. was honest in all . his intentions. He remembered him speaking in the Public Hall at Warringtorkot a time when he was one of the greatest agitators for the amelioration of the Irish question. They could, however, all afford to speak respectfully of those who had departed. The Liberal party were going to alter the cumbersome measures now in evidence if land were required by public authorities. They were going to achieve what .they had set out, to do. just as they had given all they had promised in the past. (Applause.) Mr. AINLEY and the CHAIRMAN briefly re- sponded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 11, 1914. CHILDREN AT CINEMAS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 11, 1914. CHILDREN AT CINEMAS.</p>
        <p>Council Discuss the Proposed Restrictions. ONE ITEM REFERRED BACK.</p>
        <p>The question of school children and cinema shows, which has been discussed in the Educa- tion Committee and in other committees for some months, as well as being dealt with in detail in the report of the School Medical Officer, came for a final settlement before the War- rington Town Council on Tuesday night. It, however, did not reach a final stage, as a por- tion of the minutes of the Watch Committee dealing with the question was referred back by a majority of the Council. The Watch Com- mittee resolved :</p>
        <p>" That licences authorising the use of build- ings for the purpose of an exhibition of pictures by means of a cinematograph be issued, subject to the following regulations : (I).—Children apparently under 14 years of age shall not be allowed to enter into or be in the licensed premises after the hour of 9 p.m., unless accompanied by one of their parents or an adult person.</p>
        <p>(2).—ln the event of an epidemic of infectious disease arising, and the Medical Officer of Health ' advising that it is in the interests of public health, the Watch Committee may, for such , period as they think fit, direct that all children apparently under the age of 14 shall be excluded, and after such direction has been notified to the licensees, no child apparently under 14 years shall be permitted to enter into or he in the licensed premises at any time during the period named!' The recommendation that children under 14 shall be excluded from such licensed premises during the hours when Public, Elementary Schools are open was not approved, and the con- sideration of the fourth recommendation with regard to the space between the seats and the screens was deferred.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved adoption and Dr. JOSEPH seconded.</p>
        <p>A DOCTOR'S VIEWS. Dr. Fox moved that the question of excluding school children under 14 years of age during school hours be referred back for further con- sideration of the Watch Committee. The Medical Inspection Sub-committee of the Education Committee had the question many times under consideration and found that disease amongst school children had been very much on the in- crease of late. A number of children suffering from infectious disease attended these picture palaces, and it was recommended that they should be excluded during school hours. The recommendation had been approved by the Health Committee, and it was a matter of regret that it had not been approved by the Watch Committee. •He moved that the minute be re- ferred back so that the addition might be made to the Committee's proposals. Councillor ARCHER seconded, and said the Medical Inspection Sub-Committee had dis- covered cases where children were excluded from school because of infectious disease imme- diately attended picture shows, thus placing the health of other children in jeopardy. Dr. JOSEPH (Deputy Mayor) said although he seconded the adoption of the minutes he was thoroughly in accord with the amendment. He voted on the Watch Committee against the omission of the clause, but he was in a minority, therefore he thought he was now quite con sistent in su - pporting the amendment.</p>
        <p>"BECOMING REALLY INTOLERABLE."</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON expressed the hope that the Council" would not send the matter back. The Watch Committee had considered the questiontime, and the majority of the thought such a clause would be unfair iildthloobe interfering too much with the an .o members a liberty Of people. It seemed if they were to go on as was proposed People would have to come to this or that Committee and ask for permission as to what they should eat or wear. It was becoming .really intolerable. No reason was given for the course proposed, although private statements had been made. It was said that children who were away from school through illness should be excluded from places of amusement. He could not see what good that would do. If they passed the proposed resolution it only applied to the hours the elementary schools were open, but the same children could attend the shows after 4 p.m. Then the danger of spreading the disease would be quite as great. (Hear, hear.) All schools did not have holidays at the same time, and a child might come to Warrington, from Manchester, Liverpool, or even Stockton Heath, and would not be allowed to attend the pictures. That was placing a severe restriction on public entertainment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>"NOT FAIR ENOUGH."</head>
        <p>"NOT FAIR ENOUGH."</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS thought the whole matter should be reconsidered as it was not fair enough all round. He did not consider the proposal went far enough, and thought it ought to apply to all places of amusement as well as to picture- palaces. It was intended for the public good, and he would be prepared to support it. What on earth was the recommendation but for the good of the children. (Hear, hear.) There was a strong opinion that the congre- gating of children in such places spread infec- tious disease, and therefore the recommendation of the Committee was to reduce that danger. Still they did not want to single out one place of amusement, and if they prohibited children attending picture-palaces and allowed them to attend other places the danger remained. (Hear, hear.) Alderman BOLTON objected to either the Educa- tion Committee or the Health Committee saying that a single body of men should turn out and be inspectors. If things were irregular let these committees appoint their own inspectors and let them do their own "dirty work." He did not believe in putting on the screw in such a direc- tion that an injustice would be inflicted on people catering for the benefit of the general public. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>LIMITED POWERS. Alderman TINNION thought the subject was one for very serious consideration, and he agreed that the picture-palaces were the only places concerned where infectious diseases could be spread. He thought Dr. Fox should withdraw : his amendment and allow the Medical Inspec- tion Sub-Committee to bring forward another recommendation to cover the point. The powers of the Watch Committee were somewhat limited over these places of amuse- ment. He questioned whether, if the managers chose to stand for what they might consider to be their rights, the Council could enforce the carrying out of the recommendations of the Medical Inspection Committee. Ire was sure the managers of licensed premises were anxious to carry out the wishes of the Watch Committee in a satisfactory manner. MORE STRINGENT.</p>
        <p>- - - Councillor HAWTHORN said that the restric- tions in force in other towns in reference to pic- ture premises were more stringent than any- thing which was proposed in Warrington. Chil- dren had been taken to the Medical Officer for inspection and they had been seen to walk away to a picture house, although they were kept at home for isolation purposes. Councillor Gouen suggested that Dr. Fox would be well advised to withdraw his amendment. Suppose the Council went further and said the resolutions should apply to Sunday schools, churches, the Parr Hall, and the theatres? (Laughter.) What was suggested by _the Health Committee was an attempt to get in the thin end of the wedge. The MAYOR said he was not at the Watch Com- mittee's meeting, but he would support their recommendation. . On a vote the amendment was carried by 11 votes to 6, and the rest of the minutes were con- firmed.</p>
        <p>HEALTH COMMITTEE'S SCHEME. The Health Committee submitted the.follow- ing scheme, which was adopted as part of the minutes of the Committee : " The Committee considered the following recommendations of the Education Committee with regard to cinematograph entertainments:— (1) Children under 14 years of age shall not be allowed to enter into or be in the licensed premises after the hour of 8.15 p.m., unaccom- panied by their parents or guardians. (2) Chil- dren under 14 years of age shall not be allowed to enter into or be in the licensed premises during the hours when the public elementary schools are open. (3) In the event of an epidemic of infectious disease arising and the Medical : Officer of Health advising that it is in the inter- ests of public health, the Watch Committee may, for such period as they think St, direct that all children under the'age of 14 shall be excluded, and after such direction has been notified to the licensees no child under 14 years shall be per- mitted to enter into or be in the licensed prem-</p>
        <p>CHILDREN AT ClNEMAS—continued.</p>
        <p>ises at any time during the period named. (4) Seats for the use of the audience shall not be allowed to be placed nearer than 20ft. from the screen on which the pictures are shown." Resolved, " That the recommendations be ap- proved with the following alterations: In clause No. I.—That the hour of 8.15 p.m. shall. he altered to 9 p.m. In clause No. 4.—That the words Children under 14' be substituted for the words 'the audience.'"</p>
        <p>THE POSITION. Councillor PEMBERTON : What is the position?</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: The recommendations are recom- mendations to the Watch Committee, who have not accepted them yet. Alderman BOLTON: And they shall not, if I have my way. The MAYOR It is no use discussing the matter now.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
