      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>OUR SUMMER EXCURSIONS.</head>
        <p>OUR SUMMER EXCURSIONS.</p>
        <p>Warrington Workers' Brief Respite by the Seaside.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>SOME OF THE FEATURES.</head>
        <p>SOME OF THE FEATURES.</p>
        <p>The trip season was in full swing at War- rington on Saturday. If the Friday was the children's day the last day of the week was the day of both adults and juveniles' and a large exodus occurred from the town. The majority went for a day trip only, but a considerable number availed themselves of the cheap tickets which were extended to cover the week-end, and could also be had for longer periods. Blackpool and Southport were once more chief favourites, and at both of these resorts Warrington folk were much in evidence, both on the beach, and in the places of amusement A few trips went to Welsh resorts, and that in connection with the Parish Church braved the long cross-country Journey to spend a few merry hours on the golden sands of Scarboro'. The destinations of the trippers were : Monks, Hall—Southport. St. Helens Cable—Blackpool. Warrington Corporation—Southport. • Richmond's—Blackpool. Houghton's (Sankey)—Black pool. Parish Church—Scarborough. Holy Trinity—Blackpool. St. George's Mission—Windermere.. Oddfellows—Windermere._ 1</p>
        <p>Gorton's (Paddington)—Rhyl. Legh-street Primitive Methodists—Llandudno. Padgate Wesleyans—BlackpooL</p>
        <p>St. Thomas's—C.' olwyn Bay: Latchford Choir—Colwyn Bay. . St. Peter's Choir—Southport. Some of the outstanding features of the chief trips are set forth below :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>RECORD RAILWAY BOOKINGS.</head>
        <p>RECORD RAILWAY BOOKINGS.</p>
        <p>Passengers Went All Over the lingdom</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Lines Bookings on Friday and Saturday were - chiefly for Douglas, North Wales, New Brighton, Hoy-lake and West Kirby, Buxton and Matlock, Sheffield, Liverpool, 'Man- chester and Southport. The Saturday book- ings, doubtless on account of the fine weather, created a record, and trips were run to the</p>
        <p>•bert, I :f you'</p>
        <p>chief stations.</p>
        <p>Bookings were also brisk at Bank Quay Station, Blackpool being very extensively pat- ronised.. A number of excursionists went to London and the South Coast, Scotland and North Wales, whilst the bookings were pretty fair for East Coast resorts.</p>
        <p>I can't find my bathing-dre§s any 'ye got it on."</p>
        <p>side resort was reached in good time. The party dispersed and enjoyed themselves to the full around the town, on the lake, and in the gardens, the firm having got various concessions to their people for entertainments. The trains arrived back in Warrington shortly before 11 o'clock, the outing being voted a most pleasant one.</p>
        <p>'</p>
        <p>Punch.'</p>
        <p>be removed to the Stanley Hospital, Liverpool. THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>The inquest was held by the Liverpool Cor- oner on Monday, and the evidence showed that the boy on Saturday was proceeding with his ' mother and other relatives for a trip to South- port. When the train was travelling at the rate of fifty miles an hour the door of the carriage, against which the deceased was standing, sud- denly flew open, and he was flung on to the permanent way. The train was stopped, and the unfortunate boy was taken to the Stanley Hospital. Here it was found that he was dead as the result of a fractured skull.</p>
        <p>Nonp of the occupants of the carriage saw the lad tampering with the lock, and a railway official explained that it could not fly open unless the catch was pulled by the hand. The lock, which was produced in Court, was, it was said, in perfect working order. A verdict of " Accidental death" was re- turned.</p>
