      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>ST. HELENS CABLE. Non-stop Dash to Blackpool.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS CABLE. Non-stop Dash to Blackpool.</p>
        <p>Over 300 St. Helens Cable and Rubber Co., Ltd., employes and their wives, together with 150 friends, held their 14th annual picnic on Saturday, when, leaving Bank Quay by an early train, they journeyed to Blackpool with a non- stop run. Here lunch was partaken of at Clarke and Heap's British Restaurant on the Promenade, the menu of which contained the excellent advice: " Tide and trains wait for no man—so don't be late. Drink little but seldom —wine is a moCker, bad whisky is much worse." Addressing the assembly after the lunch, Mr. E. S. SEYMOUR, works' manager, expressed his pleasure and gratification at seeing so great a number present. He hoped that, as the years rolled by; their members would continue to in- crease, and that the years would continue to be as prosperous for all concerned as that which had just gone. (Applause.) Mr. Seymour then went on to express his , regret at the unavoidable alksoce of Mr. J. H. C. Brooking, their president, 'And read a tele- gram from him in the following terms: " Best wishes for a happy time for all. Don't forget the mottoes !"—advice which was implicitly followed. The excursionists then separated into parties, and many made an ascent of the Tower, which was allowed free of charge by arrangement with the management. Other places of amusement were liberally patronised. The return was made in the evening from the Talbot-road Station. The industrious committee who had charge of the arrangements consisted of: Messrs. J. 11. C. Brooking, president; E. S. Seymour, vice-presi- dent; W. Bradbury, chairman; W. Watson, R. Ellsworth, T. Tweedle, W. Locker, T. Lawrence, hon. secretary; and A. Ratcliffe, hon. treasurer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOY NEARLY</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOY NEARLY</p>
        <p>• ... '. , ~ j. \ inn , t ' (Regd. Trade .1.!ar:.1 COFFEE EasENCIS The Cal Tee Maid, el Syminetares, says ":'-'71.1:4. a song of Symington's, the kettle's on. ll' A little drop of Essence., makes Coffee for a THos. symx:;ons ,c co.. ridothurgh</p>
        <p>DROWNED AT BLACKPOOL. An exciting incident occurred at Blackpool on Saturday morning, when Edwin Francis Jen- nings (16), of 14, Glazebrook-street, Warrington, had a narrow escape from drowning. Jennings, who is the eldest son of Mrs. Jane Jennings and Is employed as a clerk in Messrs. Richmond's offices, had gone with the trip from that Works, and was bathing in the sea on the South Shore with several companions, when one of them, ' Richard S. Parr, of 8, Egerton-street, noticed lie was in difficulties and raised an alarm. John Edward Bomber, of 11, Ibbison-street, Black- pool, was on the sands with several horses, and hastened to the lad's assistance John Richard Sinith, the proprietor of a stud of donkeys, also hurried up, and the two men succeeded ' in bringing Jennings ashore. Artificial respiration was kept up for an hour and a half with suc- cess, and subsequently the youth was conveyed in the ambulance to the Victoria Hospital, where he gradually recovered. He was brought back to Warrington on Monday by his mother.</p>
        <p>; :1.</p>
        <p>, . 4. • the s:Tr:o kina." a. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>PEACOCK'S BAZAAR EMPLOYES.</head>
        <p>PEACOCK'S BAZAAR EMPLOYES.</p>
        <p>The- employes of Messrs. Peacock's Bazaar Co. had a most enjoyable outing on Thursday, when they journeyed to Southport for their annual excursion. A party of about 44 saw the sights of this resort, and had a glorious time. The Mayor and Mayoress joined the party at tea which was partaken of at Rown- tree's Cafe, Lord-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>GORTON'S MEN AT Rini.</head>
        <p>GORTON'S MEN AT Rini.</p>
        <p>Sights Thoroughly Inspected.</p>
        <p>Rhyl was the venue of a party of about 50 employes of Mr. Edward Gorton, glue manu- facturer, Paddington, on Saturday. The pleasure-seekers arrived there early, and, divid- ing into groups, spent a very enjoyable day in visiting the Marble Church, Rhuddlan, Llan- fairtalhaiarm, and other outlying places. Most of the excursionists returned early in the even- ing. The party was in the charge of Mr. W. Jump, secretary- of the firm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>BOY EXCURSIONIST KILLED.</head>
        <p>BOY EXCURSIONIST KILLED.</p>
        <p>Fell Out of Monks, Hall Trip Train.</p>
        <p>I The Monks, Hall works' trith to Southport had a gloom cast over it by a fatal accident to a six-year-old boy, who was one of the excur- sionists. He was John Wood, son of an iron- worker, living at 35, New-road, Latchford, and employed at Monks, Hall, and the boy was a passenger in the second train specially run from Warrington to Southport in connection with the trip. As the train was running at a high speed through Fazakerley, a small place about half-way between Warrington and South- port, the carriage door flew open and the boy was thrown out. He fell on the line and was so seriously injured that he died before he could</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>WARRINGTON CALEDONIAN ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CALEDONIAN ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The members and lady friends of the above had their annual outing to Prodsham. On arrival the green at the Bear's Paw .Hotel was rather damp, so some of the party made their way to the top of the hill and passed the time enjoying themselves on the helter-skelter, etc. After tea the bowling drive was played, and re- sulted': 1, Mrs. W. Murray; 2, Mrs. Stirling. Mr. W. Murray acted as M.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>LEGII-STREET PRIMITIVE ;METHODISTS.</head>
        <p>LEGII-STREET PRIMITIVE ;METHODISTS.</p>
        <p>Members of the Legh-street Primitive Metho- dist Church choir and friends, numbering about 30, had their annual picnic on Saturday. Leav- ing Bank Quay Station by an early train, the party partook of dinner upon arrival at Llan- dudno. The weather was glorious, and, divid- ing into grouNl, the holiday-makers passed a very pleasant afternoon in boating and rambling over the Great Orme. After tea, the party re- turned home. Mr. A. Spruce, the choirmaster, ably superintended the arrangements.</p>
        <p>RICHMOND'S TWENTIETH TRIP. Messrs. Richmond's employes held their 20th annual trip on Saturday. 'when about 1,200 left Bank Quay by two special trains early in the morning, en route for Blackpool. Favoured there with splendid weather, all en- joyed themselves to the full extent by visiting the various places of amusement or by sailing. The return was made from the Central Station early in the evening.</p>
        <p>HAIRDRESSERS' RESPITE. There was no hairdressing in the town on Thursday, for then the Warrington and Dis- trict Hairdressers' Association kept their annual holiday. The weather was splendid, and although there was, no organised picnic for the association, its members, in different groups, spent a very enjoyable time in the Lake District and the Isle of Man. Others visited I3eeston Castle, or passed the day in fishing and cycling.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>PARISH CHURCH CHOIR.</head>
        <p>PARISH CHURCH CHOIR.</p>
        <p>This year's annual outing of the adult mem- bers of the Parish Church Choir was of two days' duration, and had been arranged by Mr. A. Simkin, the secretary. Leaving Bank Quay early on Friday morning, the holiday-makers trained to York, where they visited Messrs. Rowntree's cocoa works and had the various processes of production explained to them. Lunch was partaken of at Terry's Restaurant. Immediately after tea the party entrained for Scarborough, where they spent an enjoyable evening at different entertainments. Sleeping accommodation was provided at the Nicholas Boarding Establishment. Saturday was passed in visiting the various places of interest, boat- ing and fishing. Mr. F. M. Darby, the church organist, accompanied the trippers.</p>
      </div>
