      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>r• JULY 11, 1914.</head>
        <p>r• JULY 11, 1914.</p>
        <p>PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>TIIE TANNERS' STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Correspondence has taken place during the week between the Master Tanners' Association and the Tanners' Union, but a black situation has become only blacker in consequence. Last week-end the Mayor tendered his offices as mediator, and the men accepted. They also caused it to be known that they were willing to meet the masters and promised to observe certain rules for the future. They, for in- stance, undertook not to strike again without giving notice. The masters, however, have declined the services of the Mayor, stating that the trade "is full of intricacies which only be dealt with by the parties themselves." As to meeting the men they stipulate that the men shall agree as a preliminary to such meet- ing that fourteen days' notice before leaving shall be given in future, and that no distinc- tion shall be Made between union and non- union men. They 'also indicate that the works , will not require as many men in the future as before the strike, and they reserve to them- selves the right to refuse to take back any men or man they may wish not to employ. The men have replied stating, in effect, that the acceptance or otherwise of these stipula- tions ought to form part of the business of a conference, as they are not prepared to accept any conditions until they know exactly what they are going to receive in return. The masters' answer to this is to re-state their re- quirements without alteration, but with the comment that they are framed with a view to bringing about peace for the future. Although the masters have laid down strin- gent conditions precedent to meeting the union officials, they have made a great advance in agreeing to meet the union officials. This is a change of attitude, which, we think' the men should welcome as a stepping-stone to peace. It is a concession on the other side that may well be placed against the concessions that are asked of them. In any case the situation has become very strained, and no opportunity should be lost that is likely to lead to peace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>CHILDREN AT CINEMAS.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN AT CINEMAS.</p>
        <p>_ The question of regulating the attendance of children at cinemas, raised by the Education Committee, was discussed this week by the Town Council who were 'asked by the Watch Committee to adopt two of the recommenda- tions they had been asked to consider. The committee had rejected the third recommenda- tion and suspended the consideration of the fourth; and the Council, led by Dr. Fox and Dr. Joseph, decided .to send the matter back to the Watch Committee for further consider- ation, which meant the inclusion of the re- jected suggested regulation. Alderman Bol- ton, who said the Watch Committee would refuse, to do the "dirty work" of the Educa- tion Committee, did not give much hope of his committee making further concessions, but he was reminded by a Councillor that there was ' more than one man on the Watch Committee, the "point" in the remark being the fact that Alderman Bolton does not think there is. The regulations that the Watch Committee are willing to stipulate before they issue licences to picture houses are that children under fourteen shall not-be allowed in such buildings aften nine at night, unless accom- panied by an adult; and that during epidemics of disease children under fourteen may be ex- cluded altogether. They refused to pass the recommendation that children shall not be allowed to enter picture-houses during school hours; and they deferred the question of in- sisting that the space between the pictures and the audience should not be less than 20 feet. It was the question of the admission of children who ought to be at school that raised the dis- cussion, the suggestion being that children excluded from school because of haying dis- ease upon them sometimes fill in their time by going to the pictures. Councillor Pemberton did not see how the rule could effect any good purpose, as the children concerned could go to the pictures immediately the schools closed, and would be quite as dangerous as they were a few minutes befOre: He thought it was intolerable, and that the Council would be compelling people directly to consult them as to what they should cat or wear. CoMicillor Roberts pointed out That cinema proprietors were concerned more at being singled out for special treatment than at the proposed restric- tions, and it was this consideration that largely influenced the vote in favour of further and fuller consideration of the very complex ques- tion</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>PARR HALL ORGAN.</head>
        <p>PARR HALL ORGAN.</p>
        <p>THE IMPOSSIBLE LORDS. The House of Lbrds have this week shown how impossible they are as a " revising" chamber. They were given the opportunity of so amending the Home Rule Bill as to make it acceptable to their own friends, with- out making it too distasteful to its authors. Having learnt nothing by past experience they proceeded at once to wreck the Bill by their partisan extravagance. Had they been starting at the beginning of the controversy instead of at the end they could not have been' more reckless or light- hearted. In brief, they say that the only way to avoid trouble is to give the Orangemen all they ask, and they pro- po6e to give it them. Nay, more, they not only say that Ulster as a whole must be excluded for all time, but they go outside Ulster and remove the Unionists in the -South and West of Ireland from the jurisdiction of the judiciary appointed by the Home Rule Goyernment, and withdraw the admin- istration of -the Land Purchase Acts from the control of the Irish Parlia- ment. May we pause to ask, if a small minority in Nationalist Ireland are to be specially exempted, while the large Nationalist minority, in Ulster are to be left voiceless to the Belfast fanatics? But Wis not a question of reason or logic ; it is a question of try- ing to ride rough-shod over every- thing, to their own hearts' desire: That is to wreck the whole Bill. The sincerity of the Lords, as of the whole Tory party, whose , game the Lords are willing to play to the end, may be approximately gauged by the act that although" they have been lamouring for an election of the ngli3O-1 people, who have little to do with the subject, they are refusing to illow the question to go to the vote in iister among the people whom it Malty concerns, and whom Mr. ‘SQUITII proposes to consult before he includes or excludes. The Tory party here stand convicted of insincerity out of their -own mouth. Ulster, they know, taken as a whole and apart from Belfast, contains a majority of Home Rulers. If we were disposed to press the point we could urge that Ulster, even including Belfast, returns a majority of Nationalist Members to Parliament. Yet they are not to be allowed t say whether they agree to be disposed of according to CARSONITE ideas or their Own. To bolster up these ridiculous pretensions the Tories are talking of setting, •up a provisional Government, and their champion is being greeted as "King." It is a huge advertising farce such as modern Conservatism affects to perfection. It it rather nauseating, but the 'people will see very well through it all.</p>
        <p>The fund for providing a reliable organ for Parr Hall is not making the progress its pro- moters were entitled to expect. About half the amount required has been promised— £Bls 165., irrespective of the fund being raised by the committee of the Musical Soeiety—and it has been decided to expedite matters by fix- ing a time limit. We understand that the committee suggest that the fund should be closed on October Ist, it being feared that indefinite delay might lead to the loss of some of the donations already promised. It would be a thousand pities were failure to attend the attempt to place Warrington in possession of an instrument worthy of the town. its public hall and its musical reputation. But we do not expect failure. By the date named no doubt sufficient funds Will be in sight to justify the, committee in making final arrangements for the purchase. It will be a good invest- ment, adding as it must, to an "incalculable extent, to the amenities of the town through the pleasantest of mediums—music.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR AND THE CRIPPLES. His Worship the Mayor, with his well-known generosity and kindness of heart—and we, Of Course, couple Mrs. Peacock's name with her husband's in this respect—invites all the cripples in the borough to a garden party at the Town Hall on Thursday afternoon, July 30th. Application for tickets, which are avail- able for cripples of all ages, should be made to the Town (perk before Monday, the 20th Inst.</p>
        <p>UNCONSCIOUS INFLUENCE. One of the greatest surprises of the Hereafter is reserved for those who, by their unconscious influence here on earth, are the means, 'in God's hands, of raising to a higher life, their fellow- men; for their' reward will exceed their expec- tations. Little as they know it, the strange indefinable charm which their lives possess-- more potent for good than a thousand sermons —silently, and, for the most part it may be,. imperceptibly, does its work of blessing, in ways varied and innumerable, bringing life and hope to all around, as does the gentle shower upon the parched soil to the flowers , which else would fade and die. _</p>
        <p>WALTER CARTER. Summerville Gardens, Latchford Without.</p>
        <p>EVERirWHERE,</p>
        <p>We miss most of our blessings by refusing burdens.</p>
        <p>Dia6 your own footprints instead of following in the other fellow's.</p>
        <p>One good deed can deserve another a long time without getting it.</p>
        <p>Some people have the knack of stretching the truth without breaking their word.</p>
        <p>The reason a man bets is so lie can brag if he wins and forget to mention it if he loses.</p>
        <p>A dog doesn't appreciate the simple life until after he has had a tin can tied to his tail.</p>
        <p>The best thing to keep an argument going indefinitely is ignorance of the subject on both sides. '</p>
        <p>A reputation for being clever can make a per- son act more like a fool than anything else in the world.</p>
        <p>ft</p>
        <p>a,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>GOLF FOR THE WORKING CLASSES.</head>
        <p>GOLF FOR THE WORKING CLASSES.</p>
        <p>Victoria Park Extension. BOATING, SWIMMING AND ANGLING</p>
        <p>"It will meet with your hearty approval and will be of lasting benefit to the citizens of Warrington."</p>
        <p>„ The Council did approve by a general and heartily expressed "Hear, hear,” and there can be little doubt that the purchase will prove to be of lasting benefit to the town. The comment quoted was that of the Mayor, who, in opening the monthly meeting of the Council on Tuesday night, announced that, acting upon the instructions of the Council, he and the Town Clerk had, through the agent of the Earl of Ellesmere, purchased 38 acres of land adjoining Victoria Park for £4,150, which was equal to twenty years' purchase. A NINETY-ACRE PARK. Victoria Park, which was acquired in 1896 and thrown open to the public in 1897, to cele- brate the Queen's Diamond Jubilee and the jubilee of the incorporation of the borough, is 52 acres in extent. The purchase now an- nounced will not only increase its size to 90 acres, but it will place the town in possession of the whole of the peninsula-shaped piece of land that lies between the river, the canal, and Knutsford-road.</p>
        <p>Another vital improvement that has taken place at Victoria Park since its acquisition has reference to the all-important question of access. The new swing bridge has wade the grounds available not only for Latchi 1, but for Howley, St. John's and Fairfield .rds, which means that it is now available ft,.. the children of half of the town. It is being largely and generally used. But it is to be more fully patronised in the future, the latest purchase having made it possible to introduce features which could only be dreamed about hitherto. Councillor Henshall, who has been very active in promoting this purchase, approached by "The Examiner" representative, having pointed out that the purchase was the cheapest the Corporation had made in its history, out- lined in brief the scheme that the Parks Com- mittee have in contemplation. MUNICIPAL GOLF. Asked whether this was the maturing of the idea of municipal golf links for the working classes, to whicn we drew attention last Nov- ember, the Councillor replied in the affirma- tive.</p>
        <p>"In Lovely-lane," he said, "the Town Coun- cil have arranged to make not only a couple of bowling greens, but tennis courts. Bowling greens are provided in several parts of the town, and there is accommodation for swim- ming and other sports and pastimes. Why should we not also have golf links for the working classes?" PROPOSED LARGE LAKE. "Victoria Park will be put to other uses, as well?"</p>
        <p>"Oh, yes," replied Councillor Ilenshall. "The depression in this ground that is known as the old bed of the river would make a splendid lake if filled in at both ends. A stream, fed by springs, rune' through it. Up to the present the town has owned only one bank of the depression; now it will own both, and there are no obstacles in the way of im- pounding the water." " What size do you think the lake will be?" "It 'should cover from seven acres to eight acres. The Parks and Baths Committee have been considering the making of a lake for some time, and this purchase will enable them to make one twice the size of the one originally suggested."</p>
        <p>"And its uses?" "It will be used for boating, swimming, and fishing. Mr. Lyon Whittle, the Town Clerk, has already volunteered to stock it with fish." There are already two bowling greens in the Park that are very well patronised. There is also a large and increasing demand for the small-holdings that have for years been a fea- ture of Victoria Park. The purchase will allow of further development in this respect. Cricket in summer and football in winter find a convenient home in Victoria Park, which is becoming more and more popular. THE RIVER AND AN "IF." Unfortunately the river which sweeps round the town side of the ground is badly polluted. We drew attention to this question some months ago, pointing out that as a preliminary to its purification it was necessary that we should ourselves, as a c. mmunity, cease from turning in our sewage. A sewerage scheme for Warrington means the expenditure of a quarter of a million of money, which, we •fear, means many weary debates in the Colin. cil and much cutting to waste of time. We pote from the minutes of the Health Committee that at a recent meeting the Town Clerk reported that the Paving and Sewerage Committee had appointed a special sob-com- mittee to report upon the condition of the War- rington sewers, and that until this report had been prepared it was inadvisable to consider the</p>
        <p>(Continued in next column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>"A PENNY FOR THE FAIR." Cheap Price to Entice Everybody to Warrington's Great Fete Next Week. RECORD CROWD EXPECTED.</head>
        <p>"A PENNY FOR THE FAIR." Cheap Price to Entice Everybody to Warrington's Great Fete Next Week. RECORD CROWD EXPECTED.</p>
        <p>"He gave me a penny, a brand new penny for the fair." So ran an old-time ballad.</p>
        <p>It is to be hoped everyone in Warrington and district will have a penny next week to use as admission money to the great Old English Fair, to be held in the Victoria Park on Thursday,' Friday, and Saturday. The fair in the olden days was a great feature in the life of the people; to-day this one, on behalf of the Infir- mary and other local charities, should prove equally as interesting. The olden-time lasses used to insist upon the purchase, by their sweet- hearts, of " a bunch of blue ribbons at the fair." If present-day swains cannot find blue ribbons at the Old English Fair next week they can find something else which will please the belles equally as well, and thus married couples, courting couples, bachelors and maids, boys and girls, and even the tiniest new-comers should be found crowding Victoria Park in their thou- sands. At a penny a head they can contribute a goodly sum to a great cause. SPECIAL TRAVELLING FACILITIES. There need be no excuse that the distance to the Park is too great to walk. The Fair Com- mittee have arranged with the Corporation to run a special and continuous service of trams, so it will be possible to ride in comfort to the scene of the merry-making. Two special en- trances are to be provided. One will be on the II Howley side and the other from the Knutsford road, thus the public have the choice of two I ways of paying their pennies and entering into the joy of the Fair. . . .</p>
        <p>The Park will he transformed into an arena of amusement. All manner of entertainments will be provided, and as the greatest of show- men, Barnum once said: "The galaxy of talent and fun will fill the soul with joy." Shows of all kinds will be there, all calculated to tempt the public to give from their purses to help War- rington's most deserving charities. Space pro- hibits detailing the attractions. The better way is for our readers to go—taking also their friends —and see the fun of the fair for themselves. One item, however, which should appeal to the lovers of sport is the boxing competitions. For this most of the cleverest local amateur boxers have entered, and some good exhibitions of the "noble art" should be witnessed. Professionals will also give exhibitions. A NEW RECORD EXPECTED.</p>
        <p>Provided the weather clerk sends a genial sample of weather the success of the Fair is practically assured. The various sub-committees have been working like Trojans for some time, and their hustling ought to produce the best results. Two years ago some 50,000 persons attended, and this time there are signs that even this fine record will be eclipsed. In fact every- body in Warrington seems bent upon going to the Fair, and he (or she) who does not will truly be "odd man (or woman) out." OPENED BY WELL-KNOWN PEOPLE. The Fair will be marked by an interesting opening ceremony each day. On Thursday after- noon Sir William Cobbett, who practically earned his well-deserved knighthood on account of the noble work he has achieved on behalf of the movement for the healing of the sick and maimed, will declare the Fair open. Sir William is one of the best known of Lancashire citizens, and is a leader of charity in Manchester. He is chairman of the Manchester Royal Infirmary. Mr. Roger C. Parr will preside on this occasion. On Friday the Fair will be opened by Mr. A. H. Crosfield, who will be accompanied by Mrs. Crosfield, a couple deeply interested in the --rk of the Infirmary and other kindred insti- ti....ms. The Mayor, Councillor Peter Peacock", will act as chairman.</p>
        <p>On Saturday Mr. Harold Smith, who will be accompanied by his future wife, Miss Furneaux, will perform the opening ceremony. Colonel Charles Lyon, of Appleton Hall, will attend his first public function in Warrington when he will preside at Saturday's opening of the Fair. During the time the Fair is in progress the children from the Padgate Homes and the Blue- coat School will be admitted free to the fete.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>LICENCE GRANTED.</head>
        <p>LICENCE GRANTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. H. Unsworth applied for an occasional licence for Mr. G. F. Freeman, of the Bull's Head Hotel, to sell between the hours of 2 p.m. and 10 p.m. at Victoria Park on Thursday, Friday, and Saturday, July, 16, 17, and 18, on the occasion of the Old English Fair. The licence was readily granted.</p>
      </div>
