      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>A Brief Council Meeting. --e.JIIIY meeting of the Warrington Town ~..9uncil on Tuesday was marked by its brevity. !lie sitting lasted just over an hour and a quarter, yet much transacted.</p>
        <p>Open-air Music. Mr. T. J. Down's Military Band will occupy the Stand in Bank Park on Thursday evening.</p>
        <p>IsrMiss Crosfield's Yachting - 188 Crosfield has invited the ladies of the Party. . -"MlaittA,.... •. Ass . of the Warrington Women's Liberal , °elation to take a trip from Degatiwy with °la Monday in her yacht. 1,, Opening of the Liberal Chili. .4 'le extended Liberal Club will be opened by • •211._,8. John Crosfield next Thursday week. July ,;P• Pull particulars of the arrangements, .11ch are now almost complete, will appear "The Examiner" next week.</p>
        <p>~ , , ' .1.1 Extension of Victoria Park. L 11;:le, Mayor announced at the meeting of the 4(,tsill'ington Town Council on Tuesday that, th ng oPon the ''C ouncil's instructions, he and 1 4 4Titmla Clerk had secured the 38 acres of land `ne extension of Victoria Park through the 41;11,,1 of the Earl of Ellesmere for 4:4,150, or ; Rtt-T7 to 20 years' purchase. He was sure that (A' would meet with the Council's approval. • liPlaus)</p>
        <p>Black Bear Bridge Widening. Ultimate re-building and widening of t Bear Bridge was mentioned at the 4 nail yesterday morning, when Mr. Dudley, Local Government Board in- :Or, held an inquiry into the Corporation's !Cation to borrow ..t1,410 for property pur- -14 and redemption of ground rents in con- with the widening of Cockhedge-lane, (s"ford-road, and Oliver-street.</p>
        <p>Negligent Committee Members. °llricillor Gough, at the Town Council meet- ecln Tuesday, called attention to the attend- Z_councillors at the Education Committee</p>
        <p>iZings. They numbered nine, and the co- tft't, Inembers out-numbered the councillors b-eilad qualities which peculiarly fitted them WI the Education Committee. He thought aLn°o-attendance was a censure on the Zan of the Education Committee.—'lie It is a censure on the members who ° eet their duties. (Laughter.) ql.Warrington Musical Society. R. T. Fairclough presided at the annual leg of the Warrington Musical Society, t4l Cairo-street Schools last night. The LI:14/A of accounts, submitted by Mr. Rey- -148, showed that the balance due to the bank /Zll3 was A:25 us., but that sum had been °ff, owing to the increase in the pro- - "V the opera from .R. 31 9s. 2d. to £62 is. ae society had now a credit balance of hai;• 41. Mr. Reynolds made an appeal on Of the Parr Hall Organ Fund.</p>
        <p>isttrance and Health Committees. ...,tbe 29t1L Royal Sanitary Institute annual which was officially opened by the (If Derby on Monday at Blackpool, a dis- Oh</p>
        <p>- . 'eula arose on the influence of the Insur- Oct The Medical Officer of Health (Dr. [l3, ;N• Joseph) pointed out that whilst insurancci Committfrs apparently set )It,i,tll the idea that they were going to ab- ,o'lle Health Committees, it should he km.l recognised that each had a different -`lllent of work in which to engage:</p>
        <p>A Councillor's Complaint. Gough, at the Town Council on Tuesday, dealing with the minutes Street Improvement Committee, pointed at they had only two plans for new a to pass, and lie wanted to know if , 'check, screw, or pressm43 tad peen ot on building by the committee."—The N These are buildings.--Councillor I said there was not any likelihood—this 'll4 he complained aboutor'gettinu cot- built as long as they insisteil—on- the Planning Act of 20 houses to the' acre.— ainutes were approved.</p>
        <p>pPresentation. 4tkt Dorutaiir)l(niem Class ber of the _l3,ible Class con- with St. John's Presh3terian Church, 4allPool-causeway, left England for A us- ;‘'ea." On Wednesday evening. On the previous ,Att -1,14 his co-members of the class presented ‘`Bll7 Earl, of 19, Mitchell-street, Stocktonr i'ts4l,tl), with a purse of silver as a._ token of arid esteem, and expressed their good ▪ or his success in the laud of the Southern The Rev. W. Reid made the presentit- ii9h.,aild several members of the class illade Nri,2,latte little speeches, Mr. Earl suitably ,• -"t4ing• He was given a hearty send-off at on Wednesday, and again everyone hj siweess. At thilanil-gergeant Honoured. e Drill Hall on Monday eveningt Ba l goot FttNlt witnessed the presentation o ami isrg t Itigby' 4th South Lancashires, of iiae• t7i2ed photograph of the band and an oak subscribed to by his old band ruin: "ke °lti the occasion of his retirement oro.) after 31 years' service. In. thn sas,L: ,f Major Appleton, the presentutio. who tiVer%7 c Wainwright, bandmaste.i, Sr ihe. ,longi. and dutiful service of y•ZiY %pint Rig Much affected, Sergeant in replying, that he hoped the s6l7l7iers'„hers of the band would stick to the a Ifing aiwa nd try .mll. thatbeat had dcorbceL e n He 1,, a 3s feel oil( lie "1 so 4414:4'1111)er of :the band of the 4th South ipes.</p>
        <p>Nlneili A Classified Market. °r Dickenson, at the Warrington rOpe l meeting on Tuesday, asked that the 8411 to let a stall for biscuits and sweets 'llt.},"3tlltng section of the Ifarket should for -akk to the Markets Committee. They I.3.ears, he said, endeavoured to classify tee4.4et, but the new proposal by the corn- ca was an attempt to upset the system of ti ktill ,L°ll. Ile moved the matter back.— % c)r Henslia : I second, but I do not 41. 3.4113-thing about it. (Laughter.)—Coun- -I;kins did not think it wise to have near stalls where second-hand NIZ was sold.—Councillor Chrimes said ch,""iabs no fear of the biscuits being near e4ttd es.----The proposal to send back was it, only Councillor Dickinson voting</p>
        <p>e sslon to Secondary School. wi fo° • 14 at tl, nng is an alphabetically arranged 14:ailiCe candidates who have been successful 143 admission to the Secondary School,</p>
        <p>W 114411, Arthur. Kerfoot, Ida. Henry. Knight, Elizabeth 31. It‘trin--"tnel A.. Knowles, Edna M. 04t.ciii, Rowland. Lee, Harry. eti,a, 31'il G. H. Lowe, Frank. dward J. Lygo, Lucy. iiNth•uessie. Lyttle, Emma E. •tirto,,, Sydney. Mills, John. Sdi f 4llin eld , Lillie.nscrew, Newton. Charles. ii ' Padmore, John N. tt:4l.4,,t,Tioris L. Parsons, Ruth M. clett,r,t, Ernest. Pass, Margaret A. eIP4n, t iteginald H. Plinston, Lilian. ekiv rnest. Roberts, Lily. orth Annie. Sankey, Alice M. Vlbs, )11a• • Scotson. Margaret. • ,-•tz•i„ van. Scott, Jane M. 4,, •,gtori Evelyn. haw, John P. Simeock, Wilfrid B. ir ", A • 'qt•::4ll4.lßrhellle• Soutlierclen, Mildred. ghs•-iion ,- -, SYdney R. Starkey, Thomas. Albert. Sutton, Emily. Vavasor, Vernon E. Weston, Mary. ku`rtiph°,ll, Minnie. Whittingliam, Thomas. rlie VeYg. i en. L. Willett, Clara. 11it,,,,,' Tildred E. Woodward, Sidney. 4 'ts ' •riarr •• ben. 3* Woolridge, Ernest. q e.., IR. Wright. Frances. Nt eslie B. Wright, llenry. 1), kit,'-':;-:.------ •</p>
        <p>3 Alp,. Ltt RD from 15/-, GARDEN ROLLERS from LAltt wri T 'n•DN'Ter Repairs a Speciality.—J. S. `rohreonger, 51, Bridge-street, War.</p>
        <p>Scarlet Fever on the Increase.</p>
        <p>--- There were 90 cases of scarlet fever in the Warrington hospital last week. Nineteen new cases had been reported.</p>
        <p>Secretaries' Institute Associates. Mr. T. Cecil Locker and Mr. W. Landon, of Warrington, have been successful in passing the recent associateship examination of the Chartered Institute of Secretaries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Births and Deal</head>
        <p>Births and Deal</p>
        <p>The births in Warrington last week numbered 31, giving a rate of 22.2, compared with a rate of 32.3 for the same week last year. The deaths totalled 24, giving a rate of 16.6, compared with 8.4 last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>rammar School Scholarships..</head>
        <p>rammar School Scholarships..</p>
        <p>The' following boys have been successful in gaining Council Scholarships to the Boteler Grammar School : Davenport, Ernest. Taylor, Henry Chile.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Territorial Advanee Party.</head>
        <p>Territorial Advanee Party.</p>
        <p>The Territorial summer camp advance party, consisting of non-commissioned officers and men, will leave Warrington on the 30th July for Hornby, where they will prepare the camp, which commences three days afterwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>For the Children's Cot.</head>
        <p>For the Children's Cot.</p>
        <p>The hon. treasurer of the Children's Cot Fund of the Warrington Infirmary begs to acknow- ledge with thanks the sum of £5 18s. 10d., being proceeds of Walking Day Collection by the Buckley-street Mission Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Successful Law Student.</head>
        <p>Successful Law Student.</p>
        <p>At the recent Law (Intermediate) Examina- tion, held in June, the name of Mr. C. H. Uns- worth, jun. (son of Mr. C. H. Unsworth, soli- citor, with whom he is serving his articles of clerkship), appears in the list of successful can- didates.</p>
        <p>Free Church Open-air Service. The second of the open-air services to be held by the Free Church Council on the second Sun- day in each month takes place to-morrow even- ing at quarter past eight near St. John's Church, Wilderspool Causeway.</p>
        <p>Liberal Women's Trip. The preparations for the trip of the Warring- ton Women's Liberal Association to Blackpool on 30th July are now well advanced. Mrs, Osborne, the honorary secretary, will again be at the Liberal Offices, Bewsey-street, on the 16th and 23rd of July, from two till five p.m., and six to eight p.m., to issue tickets.</p>
        <p>Mr. Payton's Pupils' Successes. The following pupils of Mr. W. H. Payton have been successful in passing in pianoforte playing in the recent examinations held in Liverpool by Trinity College, London: Inter- mediate, Miss Elsie Boardman; Junior, Miss Bessie Taylor and Miss Blodwen Jones. The latter pupil obtained honours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Libelling a Magistrate.</head>
        <p>Libelling a Magistrate.</p>
        <p>At Staffordshire Assizes on Wednesday, Chas. Humphries (45), dealer, Tiuttermarket-street, Warrington, pleaded guilty to writing a defa- matory letter concerning Mr. W. W. Doughty, Tipton, a magistrate. Mr. Justice Rowlatt bound prisoner over, and said that accused would hear no more about the case if he did not write more letters.</p>
        <p>Cemetery Chapel Improved. "The Parish Church Magazine" refers to the kindness of the Cemetery Committee in placing iv the eastern recess of the Church of England Chapel an altar table in place of "the ugly desk which used to stand on the north side. It adds immensely •to the devotional appearance of the chapel," continues the com- ment, "and will be appreciated by all who from time to time are called upon to take part in, the burjal service of the Church." Musical Successes. The fallowing pupils of Mr. William Cooke, organist of Lymm Parish Church, were success- ful in obtaining himours in their respective ex- aminations in pianoforte playing, held by Trinity College of Music, London, last month: Master Alan Brown (son of Mr. Harry Brown, !" Clovellv," Lymrn), and Miss Frances Bostock, "Pool Hollow." Runcorn, intermediate honours; find lfiss Polly Ratcliffe; also of Runcorn, hon. ours in the junior division.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Pal tit for Flel eller, Russell's.</head>
        <p>Pal tit for Flel eller, Russell's.</p>
        <p>- 111 r. T. W. Fletcher and Fletcher, Russell, and Co.. Ltd., of Warrington, have patented "gas cooking stoves." The object is to provide a cooking stove the interior and lower part of which is more accessible, more cleanly, and more readily cleaned than stoves at present in use. According to this invention the front skirting below the door is done away with, the sides or stiles on each side of the door being supported by a gas pipe or stay rod passing transversely across the stove and being secured to the sides of the stove.</p>
        <p>Pigeon Wins £l2O. Warrington figured prominently in the Lan- cashire Nantes club's classic race on Saturday week, when the 2nd and sth prizes were secured 63- local fanciers. Air. T. Malkin, of thle Royal Exchange Inn, Warrington, obtained the second place with his "Warrington Jewel," which was bred in 1910 by Mr. F. G. Dixon, of Bostock-street. /The second prize was over .{l2O. 'Messrs. fanciers,ssrs.Crnk Bros., the well-known Warrington secured the fifth place, winning •1:34.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Wesleyan Changes.</head>
        <p>Wesleyan Changes.</p>
        <p>In the first draft of ministerial appointments for the new connexional year, prepared by the Stationing Committee of the Wesleyan Confer- ence, which met in London last week, the follow- ing names appear in the Liverpool district: Warrington.—*W. Thompson Derry, *Arthur J. Morton, (Padgate), Hugh Williamson, *G. , Elliott Lee (Penketh), John Gunnell, Joseph Howard (a), T. Hawkins Pasidyn (Dymm), super- numeraries. Frodsham (Warrington).—William Stevenson, who shall change on two Sundays. in every quarter with the ministers of the Runcorn Cir- cuit; George Scott (c), supernumerary..</p>
        <p>„ The asterisk denotes a change of appointment.</p>
        <p>Secondary School Junior Scholarships.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the candidates (arranged in order of merit) who have been suc- cessful in gaining Junior Scholarships to the Secondary School : Tilling, Norman. Hatch, Margaret. Chidlaw, Muriel E. Whitfield, Robert. Crank, Eva. Kenwright, Cecil. Carter, Norah. . Jeffery, may. Robinson, James. Grant, John K. Boyle, Harold. Leigh, Sarah. Taylor, Dorothy A. Ellison, Florrie. Lloyd, Marie. Bradley, Charles A. Davies, Frederic B. Greenhalgh, Stanley. Harding, Arthur G. Twiss, Phyllis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Musical Successes.</head>
        <p>Musical Successes.</p>
        <p>The following pupils of Miss M. Jennings, 142, The Causeway, passed very successfully at the recent examination held in Manchester in con- nection with the Victoria College of Music, London : Miss Flossie Morris, 4, Museum-street, piano, for Diploma of Associate, which entitles her to wear the college cap, gown, and hood, also to append the letters A.V.C.M., after her name, she , having previously obtained the bronze medal certificate for theory with the maximum number of marks (100). Advanced Senior Grade Piano: James Whittingham, 43, Lovely-lane, 92 marks, first class with honours. Senior Grade: Ellen Miller, 12, Nora-street, 84 marks, first class'with honours. Intermediate Grade: Alice Furnival, Bewsey-street, 92 marks, first class with honours. Junior Grade: Mary Hart, Eustace-street, 90 marks, first class with hon- ours. Primary Grade: Ruth Loxley, Penketh, 90 marks, first class with honours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>TILE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 11, 1914. LOCAL WEDDINGS.' MAYOR WINS IN COURT ACTION.</head>
        <p>TILE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 11, 1914. LOCAL WEDDINGS.' MAYOR WINS IN COURT ACTION.</p>
        <p>DUTTON-BILLINGTON.</p>
        <p>A pretty wedding was solemnised at All Hallows' Church, Orford, on Saturday, when Miss Gladys Dutton, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. John Dutton, of 3, Norris-street, became the wife of Mr. Clifton Billington, son of Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Billington, of Lovely-lane, who is employed in Messrs. Crosfield's laboratories. The ceremony was conducted by the Rev. G. Vale-Owen. The bride, who was given away by her father, wore a lovely dress of white satin. Her hat was of white Tagel, trimmed with white silk and rose-buds, and she carried a bouquet of lilies, orange blossom, and fern. She also wore a gold locket and chain, the gift of the bridegroom. The bridesmaids were Misses Mabel. Dutton (sister of the bride) and Olive Billington (sister of the bridegroom). Each was attired in a dress of salmon silk suede, trimmed with black velvet, with hat to match, and carried a bouquet of lilies and sweet peas. They wore beautiful cameo brooches, the gift of the bride- groom.</p>
        <p>- The duties of best man were discharged by Mr. Frank Dutton (brother of the bride). After the ceremony a reception, attended by about 30 guests, was held at the bride's house, after which Mr. and Mrs. Billington left for Llan- dudno, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travelling costume was of navy blue serge, with hat to match.</p>
        <p>Many useful and valuable presents were re- ceived.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>FAWCETT-BOLTON.</head>
        <p>FAWCETT-BOLTON.</p>
        <p>At St. Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath, on Wednesday, the vicar, the Rev. James Collins, assisted by the Rev. W. Bracecamp, officiated at the wedding of Miss Florence Ellen (Nellie) Fawcett, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. A. Fawcett, of 10, Fairfield-road, Stockton Heath, and Mr. Sidney Bolton, son of Alderman and Mrs. Bolton, of Mill Bank, Wilderspool. After a reception at Atkinson's Cafe, Bridge- street, Mr. paid Mrs. Bolton left for North Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>HOUGHTON-JENNINGS.</head>
        <p>HOUGHTON-JENNINGS.</p>
        <p>In the presence of a large assembly, the wed- ding was solemnised on Thursday at St. Paul's Church, of Miss Minnie Houghton, daughter of the late Mr. and Mrs. James Houghton, of War- rington, and Mr. James Jennings, jun., son of Mr. and Mrs. James Jennings, of the White Bull Inn, Horsemarket-street. The ceremony was conducted by the Rey. W. Bra,cecamp. A reception, held at the Winmarleigh Cafe, was attended by the Mayor (who proposed the health of the bride) and the Mayoress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>LIAM RECTOR HONOURED. Last Night's Presentation.</head>
        <p>LIAM RECTOR HONOURED. Last Night's Presentation.</p>
        <p>Last night, at the Lymm Schools, in the presence of a large attendance, presentations were made to the Rector and Mrs. Thurston, and Mr. Callender, late churchwarden. Mr. F. E. Evans, Rector's warden, presided, and paid a tribute to both gentlemen, characteris- ing them as loyal sons of the Church. Mr. A. Watkin presented a silver plate and an address to Mrs. Thurston, who briefly thanked them for the reception given her, a complete stranger. The Rector replied and handed a gold watch to Mr. Callender, and a pair of silver candlesticks to Mrs. Callender.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>BOLD-STREET CHOIR TRIUMPH. Successful Feat at the Crystal Palace</head>
        <p>BOLD-STREET CHOIR TRIUMPH. Successful Feat at the Crystal Palace</p>
        <p>Competition."</p>
        <p>NINE POINTS AHEAD OF PAST WINNERS.</p>
        <p>Hearty congratulations to• the Bold-street Wes- leyan Church choir, who at a contest at the Crystal Palace on Saturday secured the first place in an open competition for choirs of from 16 to 25 voices. The contest—the 26th annual festival of the Nonconformist Choir Union—was attended by about 4,200 singers. The test pieces in the class in which the War- rington choir was successful were: "To my request and earnest cry" S. S. Wesley) and "There sits a bird on yonder tree" (Walthew). The competitors in this class were St. Alban's Trinity Congregational (the winners last year), Brighton Primitive Methodist, Sutton Wes- leyan, Holmfirth -United Methodist, Chatham Ebenezer Congregational, Tipton Park-lane Wes- leyan, Bold-street, Warrington, St. Mary Cray, Temple Congregational.</p>
        <p>' ADJUDICATOR'S PRAISE. Loud applause greeted the decision of the ad- judicator (Mr. Allan Gill), who in giving the Warrington choir 122 points placed them in the first position with nine points ahead of last year's winning choir, who were awarded the second place:</p>
        <p>The adjudicator in his comment upon the per- formance of the winners said that they made an excellent start in the anthem, the words and phrasing being particularly commendable. The words were really made to mean'something. The crescendos were very well marked, and the ex- pression was quite warm. It was an anthem. Referring to the part song, Mr. Gill said the Bold-street choir made a rather weak start, but the expression was very satisfactory. The words were beautifully clear, the tone was good, and the whole rendering was effective.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>SOPRANO'S SUCCESS,</head>
        <p>SOPRANO'S SUCCESS,</p>
        <p>This was not the only honour; for Miss Carrie Hastings, one of the members of the choir, gained the second prize in the soprano solo com- petition, in which there were 26 entrants. This test piece was Mackenne's "The Lord is My Shepherd." All the choir soloists were well mentioned, three of them only missing the final test by one point.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>PRIZES PRESENTED.</head>
        <p>PRIZES PRESENTED.</p>
        <p>During the festival concert Mr. Frank Hick- man, the conductor of the Bold-street choir, re- ceived the prize, which was composed of a hand- some silver-mounted baton and a cheque for .t 3 35., whilst a large framed photograph of the festival choir and a certificate of merit will also be given. The rules of the competition demand that all must be bona-fide members for at least three months beforehand, and the choir must be trained by its own conductor. This fact makes the success of Bold-street more noteworthy. Another point which makes the victory of the Warrington choir more acceptable is the fact that last year fortune was against them, as they arrived in London too late to compete. This was due to the breakdown of a char-a-bang whicib was conveying them to Chester, and as a conse- quence they missed the train by which they had intended to travel. This year, however, they made up their minds to be in time, for they travelled the previous night, and returned home at six o'clock on Sunday morning, tired but happy.</p>
        <p>THE UP-TO-DATE HOUSEWIFE for making her Puddings and Pastry, buys SHREDDED ATORA Beef Suet No chopping, ready for use, goes further, sweet and whole- some. ' Your grocer sells it. Insist upon ATORA, refuse substituted brands.</p>
        <p>Early Fame. At thirty-three Napoleon was Emperor of France; Phil Sheridan rode on a foaming steed twenty miles, seized his retreating army and hurling it upon Early, snatched victory out of the jaws of defeat, sending him, with his hosts, flying up the valley of the Shenandoah; Wolfe scaled the Heights of Abraham at Quebec, dis- possessed the French of their possessions in Canada and gave two provinces to England; Correggio had produced his three world- renowned pictures, The Assumption of the Vir- gin, Ecce Homo, and The Penitent Magdalen; George Stephenson made his first locomotive; Edison had harnessed electricity to the uses of man; Gray wrote his Elegy; Poe his Raven, and Thomas Jefferson the Declaration of Independ- ence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Sequel to a "Good Turn " Performed by Councillor Peacock.</head>
        <p>Sequel to a "Good Turn " Performed by Councillor Peacock.</p>
        <p>JUDGE SAYS THERE IS NO DISCREDIT TO HIM.</p>
        <p>Judgment was given for the Mayor at the County Court on Thursday in the case in which he figured as defendant A fortnight ago Timothy Morgan, retired ironworker, 131, Lovely-lane, claimed from Councillor Peter Peacock, 5, Palmyra-square, the sum of .4.;23, being the difference between the price paid by Mr. Morgan to, Councillor Peacock for the fix- tures, B:c., at the fishmonger's shop, 41, Horse- market-street, and the amount realised for such fixtures by the plaintiff at his sale on the 7th April last. The plaintiff also claimed damages for misrepresentation. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. J. C. Jackson-. (Manchester), instructed by Mr. A. Browne., appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. T. S. Steel for the defendant. Mr. Jackson then said they claimed damages for misrepresentation, not with respect to the sale of a business, but with respect to the fix- tures sold in the business. Mr. Morgan was on the look-out for a business in Warrington, and had heard that a fish shop was vacant at ' 41, Horsemarket-street. This had been tenanted by Mr. Gilligan, but the real owner was Coun- cillor Peacock, who was. at the present time the Mayor of the borough. He understood Coun- cillor Peacock had a. number of businesses, which were nce carried on in his own name. His client inquired from Councillor Peacock about the business, and the latter said, "You can have the business at the same price that r paid: Councillor Peacock then said he wasr to spend any more money to go in otiht, £35, including all' fixturesqind' everything neotshgoopi.n; on with the business. ITe said Gilligan received 355. or 365. a week, and then balanced up with him (Councillor Peacock). After further nego- tiations his client said he was prepared to pay gp and Councillor Peacock, "We had better let turning n forng t everything, ver Gilligan,n said it go at that," and Gilligan said, "'yes." Plaintiff put the money on the table, and Coun- cillor Peacock, turning to/Gilligan, said; "Y had better pick it up and put it in your pocket," but Gilligan said he did not title going about with that much money in his pocket. Defendant said, "I will look after it going be put forward was that Gilligan efe was to the owner and that Council- lor Peacock had nothing to do with it, except that the two people were constituents in the constituency of which he was a member in the Council, and that was the only interest lie had in it. Councillor Peacock said to Gilligan, "Everything is in the shop, isn't it?" and Gil- ligan replied in the affirmative. Nothing like the things purchased were handed over to his client. Plaintiff received a receipt for the money, written by Councillor Peacock, and ' signed by Mr. Gilligan. Stock was taken at the shop, and it was there stated that there were about 250 telephone calls paid for. Plain- tiff paid £3 3s. for those, and £3 for other odd things in the shop. The shop did not pay, and he understood it was a most unfortunate shop, for his client was not the only person who could not make it pay. The premises were closed and his client instructed an auctioneer to sell the fixtures and stock. Then it was found that they were not his client's fixtures at all, and the landlord claimed them. All that could be realised was £6, and that for things for which plaintiff paid ..t3O. They could not get a telephone call, and all the 500 calls had been taken up. The whole of the business had been financed by Councillor Pea- cock, who had guaranteed accounts for game, and also the bank balance. Plaintiff corroborated counsel's statement, and said that Councillor Peacock had told him it would not do for his name to be put on the shop. There was no goodwill mentioned when he was buying the fixtures. He was under the impression all the time that the business be- longed to Councillor Peacock.</p>
        <p>MUCH CORRESPONDENCE. A great deal of correspondence was read by Mr. Jackson, including the following letter, which Councillor Peacock sent in reply to the one from Mr. Browne threatening proceedings: "Excuse delay in replying, but I have been very busy. I never sold any fixtures to Mr. Morgan, and I never told anyone that Mr. Gil- ligan's shop was mine, or that the fixtures were mine. The shop was let to Mr. Gilligan, and I expect the tenant's fixtures were his own. They had nothing to do with me, and Mr. Morgan must refer to Mr. Gilligan. I simply allowed Mr. Morgan to come to my house as a favour to one of my constituents in Whitecross Ward, and knowing both parties. I never received the money that was paid to Mr. Gilligan, and I had no interest whatever in the sale.—P.S. : Mr. Morgan knows all these facts full well, and whilst I would willingly assist him to ob- tain redress from Mr. Gilligan, if possible, I know nothing of the details as to whether Mr. Gilligan sold what were not his or otherwise.— P.P.S.: I expected Mr. Gilligan would have paid even his liabilities, but, unfortunately, lie has not, and his affairs are in the hands of Mr. Titmas, to whom Mr. Gilligan has accounted for the £3O Mr. Morgan paid Mr. Gilligan!" The hearing was then adjourned, and Mr. Steel said that Councillor Peacock denied very strongly, and it would be corroborated by wit- nesses, that he held out the business as his, and did not sell it to the plaintiff. Strong con- tradictions would be made to the plaintiff's statements.</p>
        <p>PLAINTIFF CROSS-EXAMINED. On Thursday Mr. Morgan was cross-examined at length by Mr. Steel, who first asked: " How long have you been living in Warrington?" to which plaintiff replied "Fourteen years." not know that Mrs. Porter had car- knew this shop in Horsemarket- s t rpAe e dtd ? you— T No ol n. ried on business there?—l did not know her. Did you know Gilligan had worked for her? —No. You had known a man named Lomas for about a week?—Yes. Had you been desirous of buying a business for him?—Certainly. You looked round at two or three shops?— Yes. You live in Whitecross Ward, of which Mr. Peacock is the representative on the Town Council?—l live in Lovely-lane. That is Whitecross Ward. You had known Mr. Peacock as Councillor Peacock?—l had never known him before this business. How long had you lived in the ward?—Since last October. I did not know the man until this business. After questioning plaintiff in regard to what Councillor Peacock said about letting Gilligan Put it to you to borrow 'an- thhaavte bmeonsaeyid,,ll,l,ro. iSlitieegalnsawidan: tsl other £5O from me?"—He said he wanted some more money in the business, and that he was not going to put any more in. Did he tell you he had given a guarantee at the bank for £lOO to enable Gilligan to get and carry on this business?—No. Did he not go on to say that if you liked he would see Gilligan, and that perhaps he would sell his business?—No. Did he mention that at the night meeting or at the Town Hall next morning?-lle never mentioned Gilligan at all to me. Was there any conversation about Gilligan at all?—Not at all. Except that he wanted some more money in the business?—That was all he spoke to me about. - -</p>
        <p>Did Mr. Peacock tell you he knew nothing about the price of the business and that he would see Gilligan and pave the way for you? —No. _ _ _</p>
        <p>When you were at the shop did Mr. Peacock say he had spoken to Gilligan and that the latter was prepared to sell the fixtures, &amp;c.?— No.</p>
        <p>In answer to further questions plaintiff denied that he wanted the business for „t 5 cheaper, because he had seen the shop wanted +Aug through. He had not been through the shop prior to buying the business. ti? had not spoken to Gilligan before he met him at the Mayor's house. Mr. Steel: Did Gilligan say I.e wanted £55 for the business at the shop9—No, the Mayor said that at his own house.</p>
        <p>SIGNING THE RECEIP. I put it to you that when y 1 were in his house Mr. Peacock did not take any interest</p>
        <p>in what you and Gilligan were transactirg, and that he was in and out f the t.)onr, also had his supper whilst you were the/el. He was, there all the time, and never left the room, except to fetch a drop of whiAy You knew Gilliga.n was signing the receipt? —Yes.</p>
        <p>_ Who got the stamp? Did cot i.iilign ay for it?--No,. the Mayor got the stamp, and Gilligan did. not pay for it.</p>
        <p>After having, as sta—te. , negotiations 's ith Mr. Peacock; you took the receipt Figned by Gilligan. You never asked Mr. Peacock to sign- it?—No; he wrote it out. Why did you accept this receipt from Giili- gan if you were dealing with Mr. l'eacivek?—l thought I was being, treated as a man and that it was all right. Is your allegation to-day that Mr. Peacock committed a fraud upon you ?1 c'o not now what he has done. You ought to know ..,our- self.</p>
        <p>You say you thought you were dealing with a gentleman and got the receipt from Gilligan? —4 picked the receipt up after Gilligan had signed it. DO you allege Nfr. Peacock has committed a fraud upon you?—lt is for you to say. It is your case?—l distinctly tell you all I have got to say. I put that to you. Do you refuse to answer? —I refuse to answer. You must not try to drag in things I do not know much about, you know.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel produced an agreement of tenancy, which was signed by Gilligan. Mr. Jackson: This has not been disclosed. Mr. Steel: I can send' for a clerk from Greenall's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>JUDGE'S QUESTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE JUDGE'S QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>His Honour (to plaintiff): You understood the business was Mr. Peacock's and that Gilli- gan was his manager?—Certainly. If that was so, did you not expect Mr. Pea- cock to be the tenant?—l did not know who was the tenant at the time.</p>
        <p>When did you get to know Gilligan was the tenant?—After I had paid the money and bought it. I certainly thought it was Mr. Peacock's business. You read the receipt?—Yes. . _</p>
        <p>_ You would see the statement that Gilligan was the tenant?—l saw it was signed by Gilli- gan, that's all. That was the first time I knew Gilligan was the tenant. Mr. Steel: You made no remark when Gilli- gan signed the receipt?—No. But you say Mr. Peacock wrote it and Mr. Gilligan signed it?—l thought it was a bit peculiar, but did not say any thing. I thought Gilligan was employed by Mr. Peacock. His Honour: I could understand a genit- man acting for the principal when the ;.ritiej. pal was not there, but when a gentleman acts when the principal is there it looks odd. doesn't it?—Yes; it does. I thought he was doing it as Mr. Peacock's agent. Did you know Mr: Gilligan had actually bought the business at a sale either for himself or someone else ?—I did not know.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel Why did you not sue OilFgatta I had had no transaction with him. Was it not because Gilligan had got a deed of assignment for the benefit of his (miiturs? —Certainly not. But you knew that?—Yes. And that an offer had been made to the creditors and that there was very little for them?—l could not tell you. By Mr. Jackson: Even when you knew Gil- ligan was the tenant and that lie signed the receipt and that his name was over the shop, who business did you think it was?—Mr. Pea- cock's.</p>
        <p>Even though it was in the name of Gilligan? —Yes. I understood Gilligan to be a workruqu. Was there any reason given why Mr. Peacock had not got his name over the shon?—He said it would not do for him t,o have it there. He has been a manager of a works up to this last year?—Yes. Are managers of works in Warrington al- lowed to have their names on shops do not know.</p>
        <p>'earbek I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>AUCTIONEER'S STATEMENT.</head>
        <p>AUCTIONEER'S STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>- _ Thomas Arthur Sutton, auctioneer, said that when he sold Mrs. Porter's fixtures Gilligan bought them for £2l 2s. Mr. Steel: Was Mr. Peacock at the sale?—l do not know, but I think he was there. Witness continued that on the 7th April he conducted a sale for Morgan and realised £5 6s. on what he was allowed to sell. These were included in Gilligan's purchase.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>TELEPHONE AG.REEMENT.</head>
        <p>THE TELEPHONE AG.REEMENT.</p>
        <p>Geot:ge—Fa-rringdon, a telephone official, said the 500 calls were used up by Gilligan, and he had had to pay for extra calls. He had no right to transfer a telephone call. By Mr. Steel: The agreement was with Gilli- gan. This closed the case for the plaintiff. Mr. Steel said he had no case to answer, but his client was very desirous of going into the witness-box to give a full explanation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>MAYOR'S VERSION.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S VERSION.</p>
        <p>A Long Cross-examination.</p>
        <p>The Mayor said he had known Mr. Gilligan as a workman for Mrs. Porter and afterwards when in husiness for himself. Mr. Gilligan ap- proached him the night before Mrs. Porter's sale, and in consequence of what was said he made an appointment for Gilligan to meet him next morning, which he did. As a result of a conver- sation he agreed that if Gilligan could make the business all right he would give him a guarantee at the bank for £lOO. Of course, he was assured that Gilligan was going to make money out of it or he would not have guaranteed it. Ile thought he would be able to withdraw his guarantee in about six months.</p>
        <p>"DOING A GOOD TURN." Mr. Steel: Why did you do that? Had you any object of making money out of it?—No. do it very often for people. If I can do a, good turn for people at what appears to be no cost I am willing to help them.. Unfortunately I had to pay in this case.</p>
        <p>Were you to receive any interest?—No, none whatever. Had you any interest in the business?—No. .... •</p>
        <p>• Has 'Gilligan ever given any account to YOU of how the business was going on—No; onlY at a meeting of the creditors on January Bth, at which I was present. Have you had anything to do with the keeping of the books?—Nothing at all. Continuing, the Mayor said that when Morgan saw him he said he was going to put a young man in Gilligan's shop. He (defendant) said he would see Gilligan with the plaintiff. Mr. Steel Why were you interesting yourself on behalf of Morgan? He wrote me a letter, and I am always willing to help people. I had known him for years. I told Morgan at the Town Hall that it was not my business and that I had told Gilligan there was not another " mug "in Warrington like me. I told Morgan he would have to see Gilligan about the fix- tures, etc. . .</p>
        <p>Referring to the visit to the Mayor's house, Mr. Steel asked: Did you take any part in the negotiations?—None at all—only just at the last minute. They asked me for pen, ink, and paper, and I gave it them. How came you to make out the receipt?—l think Gilligan said: " Will you make it out, Mr. Peacock?" and I did. I was glad to get rid of them. I gave them a drink of whisky, but I did not have any. I was about sick and tired of the whole thing. You are a creditor?—l am a creditor for the overdraft at the bank and another guarantee of £5O. NO POLITICAL OPPONENTS.</p>
        <p>• By Mr. Jackson: Have you known Morgan very well?—I have known him in the political world, and have said "Good afternoon" to him when I have met him.</p>
        <p>He is a political opponent of yours?—l am friends with my political opponents. Mr. Steel is my opponent and Mr. Browne is on the same side as me. We are all friends. I hive said</p>
        <p>" Good morning" to that officer over there (a Court official). . _ _ _</p>
        <p>What about the story of what passed at your house and the Town Hall?—Perfectly untrue. I have always made it perfectly clear it was not my business. Were you surprised that anyone thought you had any interest in the business?—Yes. You who were guaranteeing accounts at the bank, guaranteeing the man in the shop, and having these men at the shop and at your house. Come now, are you really surprised that any- one should think you were interested in that business?—l am surprised at Mr. Morgan, for he knows. All his statements are deliberate falsehoods.</p>
        <p>Do you know what was done with the .00?-1 understand that Gilligan paid the reat, eke- tricity, and gas accounts. Can you give me any reason why you should guarantee this man?—l thought he was a very deserving. man. I. have done it before, and I did a similar thing only last week with one man. I thought there was no risk. with Gilligan.</p>
        <p>Were you gating anything out of the busi• ness?—No.</p>
        <p>Was it well known to Crasfield's that you were in business in the town ?—Yes; and I was com- plimented on a good deal r made by one of the firm last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>GILLIGAN'S STORY.</head>
        <p>GILLIGAN'S STORY.</p>
        <p>Michael Thomas Gilligan said he was now a labourer at Messrs. Richmond's works. He de- nied. that Mr. Peacock was to get anything out of the business whatever, and stated Mr. Pea- cock had nothing to do with the selling of the fixtures.</p>
        <p>His Honour: Was this sale by you or by Mr. Peacock?—With all due respect to your Honour, it was on my own behalf. Had Mr. Peacock in your presence repre- sented he was the owner of the business— Absolutely no. __</p>
        <p>-- Mr. Jackson: Do you deny that you have ever said this was Mr. Peacock's business?—l have not.</p>
        <p>Do you swear that?—Yes. Mr. Jackson (to his Honour): May I call in Mrs. and Miss Morgan? His Honour: Yes.</p>
        <p>The two ladies entered the court, and then I,lr. Jackson asked witness: Do you remember going into the shop and talking to Mrs. and Miss Morgan in February 7—No. Do you remember saying you were glad they had got rid of Lomas?—No. Did you say "If I had seen your master this morning he would not have paid it. It is not my business, it is Mr. Peacock's"?—No, I-swear it. Why did you apply to Mr. Peacock to guar- antee you at the bank. Had he guaranteed you in anything else?—No. Why should he guarantee you in this?—l met him in different friendly societies and I thought he was the right one to approach. After answering further questions supporting the Mayor's denial, witness was asked in regard to the 500 telephone calls, of which he sold 250 to Morgan, but these, counsel alleged, had all I been used. Mr. Morgan said he did not know they had all gone. He must have forgotten they had been used.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jackson: Two days before you know yoa had extra calls; do you want to wriggle out of it by saying you had forgotten. You got £3 ss. for 250 calls which were used, but which you said were not used ?-I did not know. You ask us to believe that?—l do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>NOT THE MAYOR'S BUSINESS.</head>
        <p>NOT THE MAYOR'S BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>Joseph LOMAS , 12, Pierpoini--sireei7;;bo was present at one of the interviews, said the Mayor never made out the business was his or that he had any interest in it. By Mr. Jackson: He had not said Gilligan was the manager for Mr. Peacock or that he could not make it pay, and the business would go flop, the reason being that Gilligan was not there early enough. He denied having said that he could not understand why the money was left on Mr. Peacock's table, and that it was only a blind. He said it was rumoured in the town that it was Mr. Peacock's business. Mr. Jackson: Whose business did you think it was?—Well, to speak correctly, and knowing Mr. Gilligan for many years, it was a puzzle to me how he came to jump up in such a manner as he had done, to have ft sign over his door. I simply passed the remark— Never mind that. Whose business did you really think it was?—l could not answer that question. • Did you think it was Gilligan's shop?—Yes, I did, because he paid my wages, etc. I had no reason to think any different. Mr. Steel: Was there any question as to whose business it was at the interview?—No, sir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>JUDGE GIVES DECISION.</head>
        <p>JUDGE GIVES DECISION.</p>
        <p>His Honour: I am prepared to give my deci- sion, Mr. Jackson. Mr. Jackson: I don't think anything I could say could alter the decision. In the course of his summing up His Honour said: "This is a transaction in which Gilligan took a part, whether a principal part or not is one of the questions under discussion. He took a very important part, but this is not an action against Gilligan, and it is not necessary whether I should decide that Gilligan took an unfair advantage of plaintiff or not in respect of the fixtures or the telephone calls. What I have to decide is whether plaintiff has satisfied me that he has got a case for action against Mr. Pea- cock. He alleges that he bought from Mr. Pea- cock a certain business, including certain fix- tures, and that Mr. Peacock made certain! representations with regard to the business which were not true, and in consequence of these representations he bought the fixtures, which were not Mr. Peacock's to sell, and which Mr. Peacock had no right to sell and lost money.</p>
        <p>NO DISCREDIT TO MAYOR. I decide on that question that plaintiff has entirely failed to make out his case. I think he did not buy the business from defendant. I think he said Gilligan, for the receipts for the fixtures and stock were signed by Gilligan. I think that no representations were made by Mr. Peacock to induce plaintiff to believe it was • his business. I find for the defendant, and I think it is only right to say that nothing has been proved in this ease that throws any discredit upon Mr. Pea- cock's character. Judgment was entered accordingly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>WARNING TO SHOPKEEPERS AND</head>
        <p>A WARNING TO SHOPKEEPERS AND</p>
        <p>THE PUBLIC.</p>
        <p>The attention of the public, and shopkeepers in particular, is called to frauds at present being practised upon shopkeepers in various parts of the country by persons selling penny packets of pills, and stating them to be "Bonn's Backache Kidney Pills." Such statement constitutes a fraud and renders the person making it liable to prosecution for obtaining money under false pretences. Any person or persons making a claim of this kind should be immediately given in charge and the Foster-McClellan Co. notified at once by wire. The genuine "Doan's Backache Kidney Pills" are sold in 2s. 9d. boxes only. The wrapper is blue-grey and bears the full name, "Doan's Backache Kidney Pills," and the name and ad- dress of the sole proprietors, Foster-McClellan 'Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. They are never sold loose or in penny packets. "Doan's Backache Kidney Pills" are a special medicine for the relief of Backache, Rheuma- tism, Dropsy, Sciatica, Lumbago, Gravel, Stone and Impure Blood, bladder, troubles, and all uric acid diseases. They act on the kidneys only, and have no ill effects. The best test of a medicine is its lasting effect, and the merit of Doan's Backache Kidney Pills I is vouched for in your own town and your own Doan's Publicly en- dorsed years, cures b y newspapers by your own neighbours, who week t after m week permanency forr marrenycy Back- ache Kidney Pills.</p>
        <p>1 Such statements are the most straightforward that anyone could wish, and explain the splen- 'did reputation which the genuine Doan's Back- ache Kidney Pills enjoy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>BANK DIVIDENDS.</head>
        <p>BANK DIVIDENDS.</p>
        <p>Union Bank of Manchester</p>
        <p>The net profits of the Union Bank of Man- chester, Limited, for the half-year ended June 30th, 1914, including .t 12,113 brought fora ard, amount to ,161,545. The directhrs have declared a dividend of 121 per cent, less income tax (being the same as last year), absorbing £32,227; have provided £2,300 for income tax; £3,000 for premises reduction; 1'2,000 for pension fond; £5,000 for reserve fund iraising that fund to £355,000); car4ing forward £17,018.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>IRISH PICNICS.</head>
        <p>IRISH PICNICS.</p>
        <p>Men Whom Tory Cheap Trips Cannot "Gonvert.- HOME RULE NECESSARY.</p>
        <p>Some Rebellious Trippers Who Saw and Spoke for Themselves.</p>
        <p>With the Home Rule Bill passed a.nd the responsible men of all political parties recog- nising that Home Rule in some form will have to be granted to Ireland, with all these sec- tions showing anxiety to avert a calamity by devising a way out of an admittedly grave situation, it is strange that people are still to be found who are willing to finance trips to Ire- land "to study Home Rule on the spot." It has been "studied on the spot" for a century by men far better equipped than trippers from England can possibly hope to be, and the vast majority of the people concerned—even in "loyal" gun-running Ulster—have declared, election after election, in favour of Home Rule. What service, then, can these belated, sub- sidised students hope to do? Only to play the Tory dodge of making as much bother as is possible in the short time that remains between now and the General Election. The gun-running is a device to damage the chances of reform, and to bolster up the House of Lords. It is a desperate flank movement, made with the knowledge that a frontal attack would' end in final disaster for the attacking party.</p>
        <p>Home Rule has been conceded. Really loyal men will combine to see that this new law, like all other laws, is respected. To endeavour to perpetuate the class hatreds of the past by standing in the way of a settlement which is almost accomplished is the real blun- der and crime in the case. It is worse—it is fortunately futile-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>MEN XI HO SPOKE THEIR MIND.</head>
        <p>MEN XI HO SPOKE THEIR MIND.</p>
        <p>Will Bring Pt-ace and Contentment.</p>
        <p>Tory cheap trips to Ireland (says the "Daily Chronicle") are not proving so effective in con- verting the trippers as the promoters antici- pated.</p>
        <p>Four of the six Mid-Devon " delegates " re- fused to • sign the official report prepared for them by the conductors of the tour. They drew up a report of their own, in which they said :</p>
        <p>We consider that Home Rule for the por- tion of the country we have travelled through from Dublin to Portadown is abso- lutely necessary to bring about peace and contentment. From Portaclown to Belfast we find a very strong feeling against Home Rule. In our opinion, we consider the Nation- alists have the ability to govern their own affairs.</p>
        <p>The tourists are loud in their praises of the manner in which they were entertained. They were dined and feted to their hearts' content, they say, and submitted to no end of sermonis- ing by Tory speakers; yet they saw nothing to convince them,of the danger of Home Rule.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>ROME RULE A CURE FOR DISSENSION. Rebel Trippers With Independent Views. " F.W.R. " writes:—</head>
        <p>ROME RULE A CURE FOR DISSENSION. Rebel Trippers With Independent Views. " F.W.R. " writes:—</p>
        <p>It is almost impossible to believe that these Tory-led—and Tory-fed—tourists are really de- ceived by their patrons; if we are to assume they do not thoroughly understand the game, we must credit them with more than infantile simplicity and with a stupidity almost im- measurable. Of coursse, if the opportunity arises, Irish Nationalists will welcome men who display a jsincere desire to learn facts; though the Englisliman who accepts Tory rail- way tickets and:motor drives, and whose hotel bills are paid by Tories, knows perfectly well what he is about There have been rare in- stances of revolt on the part of "deputationists." Three of the nomads from Darlington who were recently in Ireland thought it well to make a few independent inquiries; and in his report the Secretary of the Deputation—a Mr. O'Brien—severely reproved the adventur- ous explorers in the following terms:— Ulster Volunteer Movement—Any opinion offered by a small section of the deputation on this aspect of the Irish problem must be, judged in the light of the refusal of the small section—knit by political ties—to avail them- selves of the opportunity of making personal inquiries. Without intimation to the officials of the deputation this group absented them- selves for several hours on an occasion when the good faith of the majority induced them to readily accept a favourable opportunity for inquiry—the latter remembered and respected their undertaking." -That is to say, Mr. O'Brien and his friends fulfilled their undertaking to see what their paymasters wished to show them; the other renegades "absented" themselves against the terms of their contract, and as a result of this treacherous refusal to see with Mr. O'Brien's eyes and hear with his ears, one of the absen- tee wrote :</p>
        <p>"And now, having carefully summarised all I have seen and heard in Ireland, I can but conclude that the opposition to Home Rule arises entirely from religious differences, but I believe that the establishment of a Parliament in Ireland would do much to remove the bigotry and misapprehension which at present exists, and that it would be the means of im- proving the social conditions of the people, if only by enforcing to the full the observation of the existing Land, Education, Public Health and other Acts."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>llome HuJe and Unit.</head>
        <p>llome HuJe and Unit.</p>
        <p>Another declared his views in these words:- -my opinions on the Home Rule question are—that the whole trouble is a religious one. I also think that Rome Rule and a Parliament in Dublin would be the means of bringing the people of the North and the South of Ireland into closer unity with each other, and that it should be given a trial." The Ascendency politicians who have main- tained their hold on tlfb throat of Ireland for centuries because of their success in keeping some people of the North of Ireland in a frame of mind which is not "religious," but the negation of religion, must have felt that their . money was .wasted on the Darlingtenians who wrote thus. The Nationalists in Ireland do not desire to hold aloof from these deputations, but are pleased to welcome them, and would be still .• more pleased to show them prosperous Natio alist districts—if they were allowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>IEATH OF MR. W. PILKINGTON.</head>
        <p>IEATH OF MR. W. PILKINGTON.</p>
        <p>son and ° f : cit.Ll.l W trusteee il' Our read -'-------- will regret to bear of the death Pilkington, 48, Norris-street,. who was Knowles ty years employed by the Pear-% Was wles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd. Ch of the -Be wsey-road Wesleyan " , a member of the Silver-street Bible class, and prominently identified with the Citi- zens'Temperance League, of which he had been secretary and auditor, and the Independent.- ' Order of Rechabites. Prior to the interment, a service was, cofl.. ducted by the Bev. W. A. Beckett in the Bea,: sey-road WesleYari Church. . The chief mourners were Mrs. Pilkington, (widow), Miss Filkington (daughter), Mr. James Pilkington (son), Mrs. Gerard (sister), Messrs.• A. Hayes, J. Bury, A..Asliton (brothers-in-law). Mesdames J. Pilkington, Hayes, Bury,,' .. Wilson, Ashton (sisters-in-law), Mr. Herbert Mr. Edward Rigby (cousin), .` Gerard (nePhew), Mrs, EigbY, Mrs. Sparks, Dr. Ferguson, Mr. J. ' Upson, Mr. Andrews, Mr. Skinner, Mr. Fair- . clough, Mr. Leslie Hodgkinson, Mr. Sadler, Miss Wheeldon, Messrs. E. C. Entwistle, Colley, T. Dale, Sherlock, Thompson, Healey, Stevens; and Guthrie.</p>
        <p>1</p>
      </div>
