      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Council's Diversity of Opinion Upon the Question.</head>
        <p>Council's Diversity of Opinion Upon the Question.</p>
        <p>MORE SQUEALING NEXT YEAR."</p>
        <p>The ever-green question of whether gas should be cheapened and no profit allocated to reduce the rates was discussed at the Warring- ton Town Council meeting on Tuesday night. The matter was raised upon the report of the Gas lianager. This stated the following points : " The net profit has fallen to £8,619 or .25,829 less than last year, due to increased cast of coal, lower prices for residuals, and increased cost of repairs. "As coal is offered at last year's prices and by-products are still falling, the prospects for , next year are very unfavourable. " Coal cost over £5,500 more, as we used I an extra 3,400 tons and the average price was higher. " Wages for carbonising are £5OO more, due to working extra coal and the wages being slightly higher. "Repairs on plant are £3,691 more, due to renewing the coal-handling plant and the gas- cooling and distributing plants. "The retorts have cost £l5B more for wages and £llO for material. "Wages on mains are up £129 and pipes, &amp;c., 236."</p>
        <p>A PESSIMISTIC REPORT. Councillor OSBORNE thought the Gas Manager seemed to have taken too pessimistic a view of the year's working. The net profit had fallen to £8,619, or £5,829 less than last year, due to increased cost of coal, lower prices for resi- duals, and increased cost of repairs. The largest item was that for repairs of plant, which amounted to £3,691 more than in the previous year. It seemed to him (Councillor Osborne) that some special reference should have been made to the reason for such extraordinary ex- penditure. Last year a great song was made as to its being an exceptional year with a record production. This year, however, the production was 6.6 per cent. higher than any previous record, the increased make of gas being 34,247,000 cubic feet. One was almost led to believe that the pessimistic aspect had been given to the year's working for a purpose. He said it advisedly. Some members of the Council were anxious that the price of gas to the consumer should be very considerably reduced, and when extra- ordinary expenditure was incurred it ought to be specially notified, in order that they might know how the profit had been reduced. The Supply of Coke. It seemed to be an oversight that no refer- ence was made in the report to the question of coke. The summary, howeier, showed that the stock of coke was £1,200 more than in 1913. The present, therefore, seemed an opportune time for the opening of depots in various parts of the town for the supply of coke, as it was very inconvenient for many people to have to go to Longford for it. It was better that the coke should be sold at reasonable prices than that it should be warehoused by the Corpora- tion. Too gloomy a view shoul4 not be taken of the report, as a considerable profit had been made, and he hoped that next year, when the extra expenditure had been accounted for, the com- mittee would "see their way further to reduce the price of gas, and also to grant facilities for consumers, especially the poor, to get supplies of coke more easily than at the present.</p>
        <p>Committee had that ob*t in view. They were afraid that some members of the Council would ask for a further reduction of the price. If the Council really wanted to do justice to the ratepayers and to prospective new manu- facturers, they would place the rates at their real figure of 9s. 2d.—at which they would stand without the relief given by Corporation undertakings—instead of making them appear Bs. in the .2. It was a bad system to bolster up their position in that way. In his opinion the extra expenditure of £3,000 on coal ought to have brought in extra profit. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>MUST KEEP PACE. Alderman TINNION said it had been a costly year for raw material. It had always been his experience, and everybody else's, that if raw material cost £5,500 more, the probability was that a concern would make less profit. The price of fuel last year advanced at the rate of 2s. to 2s. 6d. per ton on over 40,000 tons. Councillor OSBORNE : But you bought 3,400 extra tons.</p>
        <p>Aldermao TINNION said it was true that they carbonised more coal, but the large part of this .t. 5,500 was the increased cost per ton. With reference to the additional expenditure of £3,691 on repairs, it should be remembered an up-to-date gas works must alter its plant to keep pace with the various improvements. The establishment of depots for the distribu- tion of coke would, he continued, be a wasteful experiment The less they handled coke the better, as its value was thereby lessened. It had been suggested that certain so-called alarming paragraphs had been placed in the report for a purpose. He resented that very strongly. If there was one man in Warrington, it was W. S. Haddock—</p>
        <p>A SCENE. Councillor GOI7GH : I rise to a point of order, and— Alderman TINNION : I object to being inter- rupted. I object to give way to him. The Mayon: What is your point of order, Councillor Gough? Alderman TINNION : I object to give way. Councillor GOVOR : He said I am out of order. It is not true what he said. Alderman TINNION : I positively have the right to decline to give way. I am perfectly right, according to the Standing Orders. A MEMBER: You are. Councillor Gorcii : I said it was a point of order. The MAYOR: Alderman Tinnion objects to your raising a point of order. He is on his feet, and I cannot allow the interruption. That is the rule.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNios. : The charge has been made that it was done for a purpose. Councillor Gouon : We did not say the report had been done. Be right. Tell the truth. Alderman TINNION : I shall not have to follow you if I do.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gormi : Well, that does not matter. The MAYOR: Order, Councillor Gough. Alderman TINNION.: Go on; I've plenty of time.</p>
        <p>PEACE RESTORED.</p>
        <p>. _ Alderman TINNION was then allowed to pro- ceed. He denied the suggestion that certain statements in the report had been made for a I purpose. The Gas Engineer had, in season and out of season, contended that it was unfair to the undertaking and to himself that it should have to contribute to the relief of rates. He had always urged that gas should be sold at the lowest possible price, and the production there- by increased. That proved that, as far as Mr. Haddock was concerned, the report was not drafted for any ulterior purpose. As Councillor Roberts had, said, there would be more squealing ,next year. He (Alderman ,Tinnion) did not. mind telling ' them, whether it was pessimistic or not, that the profits would, in his opinion, be still lower. One of the principal reasons was that the various gas residuals and by-products, from which the profits were largely made, were yield- ing much lower prices. It had been a happy thing for Warrington that the gas works had been so well managed, that during the last 10 or 12 years they had contributed £120,000 to the relief of the rates. They were not going to reach that very high water mark in the future. If they were going to reduce the price of gas very soon to the consumer they would be giving him something at the expense of the ratepayer. The report was not a pessimistic one, but faced difficulties fairly, instead of shelving them. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>MUCH ALARMED.</head>
        <p>MUCH ALARMED.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON said he was much alarmed by the decrease in the profits. Some explanation of the very high expenditure should have been given. If the place had been properly conducted and kept in anything like a reasonable state of repair in the past, one could hardly conceive the necessity for doub- ling the expenditure in one year. Was there nothing in the item that ought to be placed to capital account? There seemed to be para- graphs put in that report he had never before seen in a gas report. :They had never before had such an alarming report, and he thought it showed a serious state of affairs. Councillor ARCHER said that the Chairman of the Gas Committee had promised to provide coke distributing centres, and the Gas Com- mittee had not agreed to the desire of the Council. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"MORE SQUEALING." Councillor H. ROBERTS said the reduction in the profits had been anticipated, to a very great extent, by the Gas Committee. They had been hammered from time to time by various mem- bers of the Council with a view to reducing the price of gas and abolishing certain charges, and they had carried out the wishes of the Council. They had, however, carried them out at the risk—in fact, the certainty—of the loss of a considerable amount of profit. "You are squealing now," continued Councillor Roberts, " but you will squeal a great deal harder next year when you find a very small. amount of money left at your disposal for the purpose of relieving the general rates of the town. This gas undertaking has been an extremely satisfactory one, from the point of view of assisting in keeping the rates down at a very reasonable figure, but that day seems to be very nearly over. If you will have gas supplied at a price very little above cost you must expect, as a natural consequence, that the margin of profit formerly available for the relief of rates having disappeared, you will have to pay your rates in full, and upon their own bottom." I quite expect, he added, that the gentlemen who have been so particularly anxious to serve the gas consumer at the expense of the rate- payer will find themselves in the difficulty of explaining the situation which will arise next</p>
        <p>year, if it does not arise earlier. Councillor OSBORNE : My sole point is this:— That it is not owing to the amount that has been given back to the consumer that the profit has decreased; it is because of the extra expen- diture. In 1910-11-- The MAYOR: I think you have explained it now.</p>
        <p>ONLY A MARGIN OF PHOFITS. Councillor ROBERTS: It is qui-,e correct, as Councillor Osborne says, that he has been call- ing special attention to one large item of penditure. But the various members of the Council who have pressed so hard upon the 'committee to reduce the price of gas have overlooked the fact that you have only a cer- ,tain margin of profit, ana if you destroy that ,profit by reducing the priee of the article you sell, you cannot have the profit that you want' to relieve the rates. Councillor OSBORNE : After that explanation am entitled to call the attention of the Coun- eil the fact that, if it had not been for this 'extra expenditure of over 100 per cent., the profit this year would have begn more than in. 1910 and 1911. Alderman BOLTON held that they were ear- marking profits for reductions of price, and the increased cost of fuel and labour and im- provements of plant could only be met out of revenue. They could not borrow money on the expectation of a certain definite expenditure, when it was apt to fluctuate considerably. The removal of coke to depots would not be worth the candle, as it would involve expense and its value would be lessened. Councillor LINDLEY said the Gas Committee were considering the advisability of adopting , some other method of coke distribution, but up I to the presentTthheey dhihgahsntooctkbes oefn acobklee otbotasionlevde the problem. e -orks in the country. What would at all gas if the stock at Warrington nnootnekendoltvo, reducehut tf ha er have to be done price out of reason. btheecaemome mthiotteheeahvayd be founddid Councillor WILKINSON thought the £3,691 for hSaEv SETTLED T L beenED 11 borrowed. -qnnot borrow money</p>
        <p>repairs should . Alderman BOLTON: You ca, for repairs. Councillor WILKINSON: I can. Alderman TINNION : Well. then. (Laughter.) Councillor GOUGE inquired ceptional expenditure on brought forward for the pi the profit shown? He real/</p>
        <p>rather think you</p>
        <p>I. that settles it.</p>
        <p>whether the ex- plant had been )urpose of reducing ly believed the Gas</p>
