      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>SYMPTOMS OF KIDNEY TROUBLE.</head>
        <p>SYMPTOMS OF KIDNEY TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>ARE YOU IN DANGER THROUGH</p>
        <p>IGNORANCE ?</p>
        <p>Nearly all of our readers suffer from aches and pains of some kind at one time or another. Aches and pains very often mean kidney troubles. Such symptoms as Constipation, Back- ache, improper action of the bowels, pains in muscles and joints, nasty taste in the mouth first thing in the morning, swelling of Or feet, Icloudy urine, a general feeling of weakness and irritability, backache, all these are symptoms of kidney trouble. They are causth by the poisonous acid in the blood which the kidneys should remove, but they do not owing to their being in a i 1 eak state.' To cure Kidney Trouble of any kind, you must positively get rid of the cause—the poisonous Uric Acid. Remember, to do this, a real remedy must pass through the kidneys and the bladder, and not through the bowels as most kidney pills do. —Mien you see the urine change to a muddy bluish colour—a feature which distinguishes Be Witt's Pills from all others—you know beyond all doubt that they have put their healing touch on the right spot—the kidneys and the bladder. You can get these wonderful little pills from every one of Boots 566 shops, and from every Chemist and Drug Store. The price is 2/9 per large box. Try them at once and you will thank the lucky day you first took them. But you must ask for Be Witt's. Do not put off, because weak kidneys very often mean Bright's disease in the end. But when you buy the pills be sure you get</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>De Witt 's Kidney&amp;BladderPilis Nature's S,fest Remedy. in large blue and gold boxes.</head>
        <p>De Witt 's Kidney&amp;BladderPilis Nature's S,fest Remedy. in large blue and gold boxes.</p>
        <p>THE SURREY FOWL.</p>
        <p>- - Though no time-honoured tradition is associ- ated with its existence, and no one has thought it worth while to immortalise it in prose or verse, there is a creature which ought, if it is not, to be just as famous at this season of the year as the turkey itself. That creature is the " Surrey " fowl, which, strange as it may seem, rarely has any connection at all with that par- ticular county. As a matter of fact, nine-tenths of the Surrey fowls which find their way into the market every year come from Sussex— mostly from the Heathfield and Buxted district —while the greater part of the other tenth comes from anywhere. It is the system of its prepara- tion for the table rather than its place of origin that gives its popular title to the Surrey fowl, which is the way with some other things, such as Cheddar cheese and Devonshire cream. Some say that the process Of f` cramming," which is essential to the production of the Surrey fowl efixrestel and which gives it that rotundity of form and exploitedlenceoffl in tire flavour for u nwthyi col ft it Surrey,is butnoted, theva s system originated so long ago that no one can say whether this suggestion be cOrrect or not; and the Sussex " fatter " (the longer and more grammatical word "fattener" is never used in this connection) would indignantly repudiate the idea that anybody but a Sussex man in- vented " cramming."</p>
      </div>
