      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>EXAMINERS SATURDAYS JULY 11, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINERS SATURDAYS JULY 11, 1914.</p>
        <p>1N THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>WIFE VERSUS lIUSBAND.</p>
        <p>The Woman's Application Fails.</p>
        <p>The story of domestic infelicity was narrated at the Warrington Borough Police Court on Tuesday—before Councillor H. Roberts and other magistrates—when eg young couple were in- volved in a case for separation. Sarah Jane Heath, 116, Winwick-road, sought separation and an order for a weekly payment from Samuel Heath, labourer, 157, Orford-lane, on the ground of persistent cruelty. Mr. T. S. Steel, for the husband, said the case was first before the Court on 9th June, and it was decided that persistent cruelty had not been made out, and the case was adjourned in the hope of bringing the young couple together, and Mr. Laws, the Court Missionary had attempted the task.</p>
        <p>Mr. Laws said the wife absolutely refused to go back to her husband, who still had his home waiting for her return. Mrs. Heath: I do not intend to go back to him. When he comes to see me he is always in a temper. He wants all his own way. Mr. Laws: That is the trouble; neither will give way an inch. Councillor Roberts said persistent cruelty had not been made out and the case would be dis- missed. Mr. Steel: The husband desires his wife to go back to him. The Wife: I do not intend to go back.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>"ONE-EYED NELSON." Mother Assaults Daughter and an Elderly Woman. TWO MONTHS' SENTENCE.</head>
        <p>"ONE-EYED NELSON." Mother Assaults Daughter and an Elderly Woman. TWO MONTHS' SENTENCE.</p>
        <p>The case in which Mary Harris, 19, Beswick- street, was charged with assaulting her daugh- ter, Ethel, and an elderly woman, Elizabeth Barrow, was not without its humorous side. The girl said her mother struck her on the cheek and in the mouth. Her mother had had drink, but she was all right when sober. Elizabeth Lavin, who was a little deaf, ap- peared with a shade over her eye, and in lead- ing her the Chief Constable said: "Your name is Elizabeth Lavin?"—Yes. And is your wife?—l am the wife (smiling). My mistake. You are net deaf now. (Laugh- ter.)</p>
        <p>Witness: I am the wife of Mr, Barrow.</p>
        <p>The Chief -Constable: Your husband is Joe Barrow, a general labourer, 3, Beswick-street? —Yes. _ _</p>
        <p>What did she do to you?—She called me "One- eyed Nelson." The Chief Constable: That was a compliment. Witness: She said her daughter was in my house, and I said she was not. She knocked me on the floor and got me by the throat. Where did she strike you?—Against my own gate. (Laughter.) Yes, but what part of the body P—Against the face. It is not the first time she has called me "One-eyed Nelson." You are the only woman who dare go down this street against her. All the others are pul- verised by her ?—Yes. The Chief Constable: The prisoner is a nuis- ance in the street. She does not treat the girl as she should. Harris was sent to prison for two months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>WOMAN'S PATHETIC CAREER. Petty Pilfering to Get Beer.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S PATHETIC CAREER. Petty Pilfering to Get Beer.</p>
        <p>.A touching story of a woman's downfall was narrated at the Warrington Borough Court on Tuesday by the Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls), when Elizabeth Lavin, 24, Portland- street, was charged with loitering with intent in Victoria-street and School-brow on Monday. Councillor Roberts was the chairman of the Bench. The woman denied that she was loitering, and the Chief Constable described her as a woman without any will-power. Lavin, added the Chief Constable, was before the Court eight weeks ago on two charges of stealing. She then admitted the offences, but in regard to her deplorable condition, brought about by drink, the police took compassion upon her and prevailed on the Bench to adjourn the case for eight weeks. During five weeks she went on very satisfactorily; then she lapsed into her old drunken, filthy habits. On Monday P.C. Turner observed her going from house to , house in the streets named. When she found a door unfastened she tapped and asked the occupant for a penny to get a gill of beer.</p>
        <p>A Penny and a Jug. At one house she got a penny and at another a jug, and then she went into a public-house and obtained a gill of beer in the jug for the penny. After that she visited several houses and asked the occupants to let her go into the back to drink the beer—she had pvidently seen .the officer watching her. The occupants refused, and Constable Turner then took her to the Police Station, where she was found to be in a terrible state. It was her habit to go into houses and steal articles. These she imme- diately sold and spent the money on drink. She had been several times before the Bench, charged with theft, and on each occasion was sent to prison for three months. Lavin: I had a son, I had not seen for years, come from America, and I got a little excited. Councillor Roberts asked if she would go to a home for six months if the case was adjourned. If not she would have to go again to prison. Finally Lavin agreed to go to a home. The Bench ordered her to be kept in custody. until Mr. Laws, the Court Missionary, could find a suitable place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. Before Messrs. R. T. Fairclough (in the chair), A. J. Jolley and T. Pemberton. Girl Struck With Flat-iron.—Albert Edward Gough, 3, Brackley-street, was charged with having assaulted Clara Lily Lawson, with whose mother he lodged.—The girl said that she was terribly afraid of the man when he was under the influence of drink. He had thrown .a flat-iron at her, and hit her on the head.—The Chief Constable said that when the man was in drink he would do the girl some serious injury, and she ought to be protected by the Court against such blackguardly assaults.—Mrs. Law- son said the man ." only skimmed the iron off the table and did not hit the girl."—The Chief Constable suggested • that the woman was screening the man, and questioned her as to her 'relationship with him, but she said the man was only a lodger, although she admitted that</p>
        <p>he was the father of one of her children.—The man, who had previously been convicted of assaulting the girl, was sent to prison for a month.</p>
        <p>• MONDAY. Before Dr. Joseph in the chair), Aldermen Bolton and Pierpoint, Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs. W. Roberts, S. Wright, and G. Pilling., Handed Over.—George -Hilton, a former in- mate of the Newton Reformatory, who had pre- viously been charged with the theft of boots from 16, Knutsford-road, was handed over to an ' official of the Newton Reformatory School on giving an undertaking not to repeat the offence. Disorderly Conduct.—William Molyneux, 364, Knutsford-road; Albert Petherbridge, 23, Sand- hill-terrace; James Bradshaw, 30, Rock-road; and John Gleave, 18, Cumberland-street, were each bound over in the sum of .R 5 and ordered to pay the costs for having been guilty of dis- orderly behaviour in Knutsford-road the pre- vious night. Petherbridge was also charged with obstructing Acting-Sergeant Armstrong whilst in the ex,?cution of his duty.—lt was stated that the me ti were singing "We will all go down to where the 'scab' lives," and that there were 20 other men about.—P.C.'s Berry and Wood gave evidence as to the disorderly con- duct, corroborating Acting-Sergeant Armstrong's statements.—The Chief Constable spoke of the disorder created by men who claimed to be tanners on strike. In that case there was only one of the men a tanner, and they were singing near the home of a man who was at a works where there was no strike. Girl in a Bakehouse.—James Harding, 12, Dudley-street, was ordered to pay the costs for 'having employed a young girl in his bakehouse without having her registered or certified as required by the Factory Act.—Mr. H. E. Brothers prosecuted.—Defendant said the girl was his niece and had been like a member of the family.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. Before Councillor H. Roberts, and Messrs. J. Heoherd, J. Rymer Young; C. Jackson Holmes, and J. T. Locker.</p>
        <p>An Unf it Horse.—Joseph Phillips, fruiterer, Earlestown, was fined 10s. and Bs. 6d. costs for working a horse while in an unfit state.— Inspector Dee said the,-animal suffered from strains and sprains on the muscles of the fore- legs and was quite unfit for work.—Phillips admitted the charge, and added that he did not think the animal was so bad that it could not draw a light load of four cwt.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY. Before Mr. J. Rymer Young (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Mr. A. J. Jolley, and</p>
        <p>Dr. Naden. Clutched at Policeman's Throat.—Alfred Ros- tron, coal dealer, 8, Granville-street, Warring- ton; was charged with being drunk whilst in charge of a horse and lurry in Buttermarket- street on Wednesday night. He was further charged with having no light attached to the vehicle. P.C. McDonald stated that he was on duty in Buttermarket-street at 10.10 on the night in question accompanied by P.C. Morland. Witness observed that the horse was being driven in a very careless manner. When he went to make inquiries he found the prisoner in a state of drunkenness; there was no light on the lurry, and the witness took charge of the horse, and P.C. Morland apprehended the pris- oner, who became violent and clutched at his throat. The horse and cart was given into the care of a civilian, and witness went to assist P.C. Morland.—When asked if he wanted to question the officer, prisoner remarked, "I don't think it's any use asking him when he's telling lies like that."—P.C. Morland, in corroborating, said prisoner was quiet for about two or three minutes after he had been in custody, and than he suddenly became mad and clutched at his throat. They both fell, and the first witness came to his assistance. They put the " snaps " on prisoner and took him to the police station. Prisoner (showing his wrists): "They did put the snaps' on."—The Chief Constable said the prisoner had been charged three times previ- ously.—Prisoner said he had never hurt any- body in his life through his driving.—The Chief Constable: It is not your fault.—ln announcing that prisoner would be fined 20s. and costs, the chairman said they preferred the evidence, of a sober policeman to that of a drunken carter'.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY. Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Pierpoint and Smethurst, and Messrs. S. Wright, G. Pilling, T. Locker, and A. J.</p>
        <p>Jolley. - How the Kettle Came In.—Elizabeth Carlane, married woman, 28, Philip-street, was sum- moned by Annie Chrimes, married woman, 30, Philip-street, for breaking a window in the house of the prosecutrix.—The evidence of the prosecution showed that defendant threw a kettle, spittoon, and mug through the window, and an old lady witness said that soon after the prosecutrix went in her house the spittoon and mug came in through the window. (Laugh- ter.)—The Clerk: How did the kettle come in?— Through the window. (More laughter.)—De- fendant denied the offence, and was ordered to pay the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>CHESTER ASSIZES. Judge and the Value of Grand</head>
        <p>CHESTER ASSIZES. Judge and the Value of Grand</p>
        <p>Juries.</p>
        <p>Opening the Assizes at Chester on Monday, Mr. Justice Lawrence, addressing the Grand Jury, said the calendar, containing twenty cases, was a very serious one. The charges were of a shocking character, lust, violence, and drunkenness appearing to be the prevailing causes which induced them. • - The Grand Jury performed a very useful function to the State, and though some people who were rather shallow students of • human nature, were disposed to abolish the institution, he trusted it might long survive. Reverence and respect for authority were not the most rampant features of the present day, and it was necessary that confidence in the administration of justice should be encouraged as far as possible, and the existence of grand juries, to his mind, largely assisted that confi- dence. Gentlemen were present from all parts of the country, some coming long distances, and he congratulated them upon their devotibn to duty and service to the country.</p>
        <p>A SERIOUS RUNCORN CASE. The charges of attempting to murder his wife and of attempting to commit suicide at Runcorn on April 23, were preferred against James Simp- son, a labourer. Mr. T. H. Parry, M.P., for the prosecution, said that on the morning in question prisoner had a quarrel with his wife, struck her several times with his fist, and caught her by the throat, saying, " I will choke you. I will do for someone." Just as she got to the door prisoner put his left hand over her shoulder, and with his right hand drew something across her throat. She then found that her throat was cut. Prisoner called at the Halfway House, Halton, and he then had a wound in his throat. In reply to the landlady's inquiry as to how it was caused, the prisoner said, "I have cut my throat and served her the same." When arrested prisoner had a razor in his pocket, and in ,reply said it was caused through a married man. Prosecutrix admitted that she had given her husband cause for the jealousy through her conduct with another man. Harold Hammond, prisoner's stepson, said during the struggle when he saw his father with the razor, he ran and pulled him away. Martha Buckley, a neighbour, deposed to hearing prisoner and his wife quarrelling. She saw prisoner with his arm round his wife's neck. She thought he was making love to her before she went to work. She then saw that he was cutting her throat. Dr. Whitlaw said the woman had a wound in her throat five to six inches long and a quarter of an inch deep., Mr. Wynne Bankes, for the defence, urged the jury to say that the evidence did not show the prisoner assaulted his wife with intent to murder her or do her grievous bodily harm, and their verdict should be one of unlawful wound- ing.</p>
        <p>_ The jury found prisoner guilty of unlawful wounding._</p>
        <p>-- On Tuesday the accused came up for sentence. Dr. Pennell, medical officer at Knutsford Prison, said that the prisoner was very feeble, and had had two paralytic strokes. He would be well looked after in the prison hospital. The Judge said he would have sentenced prisoner to a lighter term than he intended but for the fact that he would be taken better care of in prison than when he was out. He would be imprisoned, and kept to whatever hard labour be was capable of for two years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>14TI DISTRICT.. pfç,3</head>
        <p>14TI DISTRICT.. pfç,3</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>TOWNSHIP UTILE-TATTLE.</p>
        <p>The Garden Society folk have been busy. It has been a week of hustle and two rambles. . _</p>
        <p>They are already counting the days to the August fete. Liberals will be much in evidence to-day. The annual fete takes place at Hill Cliff e. Sports and speeches will provide a happy afternoon.</p>
        <p>"-'l`he youngsters are dreaming about their Walking Day. It takes place on Friday—a brave show is promised. The Ship Canal has been extremely busy this week. 'The "Heathens" have found it so by the way They have been held up at the Swing Bridge.</p>
        <p>Steamship's • Visit.—The Danish steamer " Nelly,” from Hafss, has discharged 16,500 bundles of laths at the lay-by. Garden Society Ramble.—The adult section of the Stockton Heath Garden Society had a delightful little outing on Thursday evening, when they inspected the beautiful grounds around Mr. F. W. Monk's residence, known as StonecrOft, Rill Cliffe. The junior section have their annual picnic to-day.</p>
        <p>St. Thomas' Trip.—The annual trip of the St. Thomas' Church Choir took place last week, when, accompanied by the Rev. J. Collins (the vicar) and Mr. F. Rose (organist and choir- master), the party left Bank I.2uay Station shortly after 8 a.m., and spent a very happy day at Colwyn Bay, where they were favoured by excellent weather. Having lunched at the Central Hotel, the contingent had an enjoyable 24-mile drive through Talyeafn. Dinner was served at the same hotel, and the excursionists started for home soon after six p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>DROWNED IN THE DARK.</head>
        <p>DROWNED IN THE DARK.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Youth's Tragic End al</p>
        <p>Dutton. MYSTERY OF THE CANAL.</p>
        <p>At Dutton Workhouse on Saturday morning the story of how a young man from Stockton Heath came by his death, was unfolded at the inquest on William Pugh, the 17-year-old son of Peter Pugh, labourer, 10, Leonard-street, who was drowned in the North Staffordshire Canal at Dutton end on Thursday night of last week. Mr. E. Aubrey White was the Coroner. The father said his son was a horse driver employed by the Bridgewater Canal Company. The last time he saw him alive was on Sunday, June 28, when he was in good health and spirits. Deceased was leaving home to go to his work at the time. In reply to a juror witness said Ins son could not swim. - A CRY IN THE NIGHT.</p>
        <p>Frederick Wilkinson, lockkeeper, stationed at Dutton, and in the employ of the North Staf- fordshire Canal Company, said he was in bed on Thursday night when he heard somebody shout that there was a man in the canal. That would be about 11-35 p.m. He got a light and took it to the scene of the occurrence, where he found a cap floating in the water. He left the light on the spot and went for his grappling irons, but could not find the body with them. He entered a punt, and with the aid of a rake he found the body. The Coroner: How long would that be after you heard the cry?—About three and a half hours. The water at this spot would be seven or eight feet deep. Thomas Shaw, master of the canal boat Jane, employed by the Bridgewater Canal Company, said the deceased was employed on the same boat as a horse driver. At the time deceased got into the water a young man named Harry Jones was just behind the horse looking after the rope and witness was steering. The Coroner: On which side of the horse was the deceased?—Well, he should be on the water side.</p>
        <p>Is that the proper side for him to walk F—Yes. What was the first thing by which you knew that anything had happened P—l heard a splash in the water.</p>
        <p>Where were you when you heard the splash?— In the boat in the middle of the canal. In reply to further questions witness said it was a very dark night and be could not see the horse or man. He shouted for help, and some men were there in about a minute. Jones said be did not know how deceased got in the water. Every effort was made to rescue the man.</p>
        <p>WHEN THE SPLASH CAME. Harry Jones, who is employed on the same boat, said he lived at 19, Newfield Cottages. Lymm. At the time deceased got into the water witness was walking behind the horse for the purpose of guiding the rope. It was a "dirty wet night." Pugh joined the boat at Preston Brook about 10-30 the same night. He was quite sober, and was in his usual spirits. They had " legged " it through the tunnel and would be SO yards away from the tunnel when the accident happened. Witness heard a splash; he would then be two yards behind the horse. The Coroner: Had the rope anything to do With the boy falling into the water?—No, sir. Was the rope tight?—lt was just getting tight. It was just commencing to haul the boat when the splash came. You could not see how it happened, I suppose? —No, sir.</p>
        <p>But you are quite satisfied the rope hall nothing to do with it?—Yes, sir. Well, how do you suggest he fell in?—l cannot say.</p>
        <p>But what do you suppose?—Probably he stepped in owing to the darkness. And that is the only thing you can think of? —Yes, sir.</p>
        <p>_ The deceased would choose the place where he would walk. It would have nothing to do with you?—N, sir.</p>
        <p>MADE A GRAB.</p>
        <p>When he got into the water what did you do? —I stopped the horse, unhooked the line, and went to see where he had fallen. He was there then and I lay down and made a grab at him, but was unsuccessful. He was sinking then. After he sank that time did you see him again P--Not until he was pulled out. P.C. Collier, stationed at Preston Brook, said there were no marks whatever on the body to suggest death other than by drowning. „ . _ _</p>
        <p>A- verdict of "Accident-al death" was re- turned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>GRAPPENHAL L.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHAL L.</p>
        <p>Independent Methodist Church Picnic.—A party of about 37 of the congregation of the above had a delightful picnic at Pickmere on Walking Day afternoon. The journey was made via Arley Park by motor char-a-bane. Boating on the lake wps much indulged in, and tea was served at Spencer's on the lake side. The return journey was through Tabley and Mere, Grap- penhall being reached about niiie o'clock.</p>
        <p>Garden Fete.—A fete and sale ot work was held at Grappenhall Hall on Thursday afternoon in aid of the Royal Albert Institution for the Care of the Feeble-minded. Mr. P. A. Prost kindly placed his beautiful grounds at the dis- posal of the promoters. Some three hundred invitations had been sent out by Mrs. Frost, who is the president of the Warrington branch. The Grappenhall Brass Band gave their ser- vices in the evening, and these were much ap- preciated. The institution supplies an urgent need for the seven Northern counties, including Cheshire and Lancashire, at a cost for each patient of £35 a year. At the present time there are seven patients from the Warrington area. The subscriptions do not cover their cost, which means that they are receiving charity from other districts. The members now appeal to the puklic to assist in making the Warrington branch self-supporting. The proceeds of the sale were £52.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <p>T CAN'T BEAR TO HEAR YOUR I CHILD CRY. Keep his stomach in order and you will soon find his temper Unproved. Next time he seems irritable, try a Steedman's Powder. Nothing else is just as good. Observe the EE in Steedman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>LYMM. LINES FROM LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM. LINES FROM LYMM.</p>
        <p>The Rector and his wife - Are just emerging from a shower Of hearty congratulations. Last night they were furthered honoured By gifts from the parishioners. The May Queen's loyal subjects Are to be entertained during next week. The success of the May Festival Was responsible for a pleasant surprise in the village. "The Examiner" gave an exclusive aecoutt. Fishing in the lake is a favourite pastime For local admirers of Izaak Walton. Who has got the heaviest basket? Are there two claimants to the honour?</p>
        <p>Presentation to the Rector.—An account of the parishioners' presentation to the Rector and Mr. A. W. Callender will be found on an- other page.</p>
        <p>New Licensee.—Mr. William Almond, for- merly manager of the Lymm Conservative Club, had the licence of the Church Inn trans- ferred to him on Monday at Altrincham, from the executors of the late Mrs. McCreed.</p>
        <p>The Worst Evil.-- The greatest assistance that Churchpeople can give at all times in a parish is a regular and devoted attendance at their parish church," says the Lymm Parish Magazine. It concludes: "Slack attendance to public worship is, perhaps, without exception, the worst evil that can overtake a parish."</p>
        <p>Grammar School and the Rector.—The teach- ers and pupils at the Lymm Grammar School joined in giving a hearty welcome to the Rector and Mrs. Thurston. One day during the week they assembled in the large schoolroom. After several speeches Miss Hawkins presented a bouquet of roses to Mrs. Thurston.</p>
        <p>Liberal Activity.—For the purpose of wel- coming Mr. Edward Powell, the new Liberal candidatp, the Altrincham Division bLiberal Association have arranged to hold a demon- stration at Belle Vue on July 25th. The Attorney-General and Sir A. Haworth will speak.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>MAY QUEEN'S SUBJECTS. To Be Entertained to Tea and Sports.</head>
        <p>THE MAY QUEEN'S SUBJECTS. To Be Entertained to Tea and Sports.</p>
        <p>The children who took part in the Lymm May Queen Festival, which proved such a suc- cess, are to be entertained to tea and sports on July 15th, at Mr. J. B. Egerton's (late War- burton's) C.T.C. Farm, Booth's Hill, Lymm. The tea will commence at five o'clock and the sports, which will start later, will include flat races for boys and girls under eight years of age, between eight and twelve years, and over twelve years of age; wheelbarrow races, skip- ping-rope races, and three-legged races; and, in addition, there will be a race for the ladies of the committee.</p>
        <p>Two prizes will be awarded for each event, and at the conclusion of the sports Mrs. James Webb, jun., will hand over the prizes to the winners. No child who did not take part in the festival will be supplied with a ticket, and those who did take part and have not received tlicr ticket should attend the school play- ground at seven o'clock on Tuesday. A small charge will be made to friends who desire to witness the sports. The total income for the festival amounted to £lO4, and not £l4O, as reported last week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>DIED IN HER SLEEP.</head>
        <p>DIED IN HER SLEEP.</p>
        <p>Inquest on an 01(1 Lady.</p>
        <p>CORONER'S MEDICAL KNOWLEDGE.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at Lymm on Wednesday by Mr. H. C. Yates (the coroner) on Mary Blease (72), wife of George Blease, Broomedge- corner. .</p>
        <p>The husband stated that a doctor attended the deceased in December. On Sunday night they retired to bed at 10.30. When witness awoke at 4.15 in the morning he heard his wife breathing very heavily, and, as a consequence,' called his granddaughter, who attempted to give the deceased a drop of gin. Witness was present when his wife died. Mary Blease, the granddaughter, said her grandfather woke her up and she immediately went to see the deceased, and loosened all the clothing round her neck. She then attempted to give her grandmother some gin, but it run out of her mouth. The deceased did not say anything. The Coroner: She has been able to get about until Sunday?—Yes, sir. Would she do any house work?—No; I did it for her. The Coroner, in summing up, said that it was so long since a doctor last attended deceased that before he could give evidence a post- mortem examination would have to be held. However, he (the Coroner) was against post- mortems, unless they were absolutely neces- sary, and he hoped the jury would agree with him, when he said he thought there was no , need for such an examination in that case.. (Several Jurymen: "None whatever.") The old lady had reached her three score years and ten, and when they attained that ripe old age they might expect their end to come quicker than if they were younger. When one was troubled with the heart it was always desirable to see a doctor, not for him to he always at their door, but for him to guide them in their mode of living, their exer- cises, aud food, and so, by that means, they might prolong life. No doubt the excessive heat they had just experienced would affect the deceased's heart. The husband, when lie woke up early on Monday morning, heard his wife breathing heavily, and that, as they all knew, was a forerunner of heart failure. Pro- bably when the heart was beginning to fail brandy was the best thing, because it gave what the medical men called a fillip, for it assisted the heart to do its duty. Gin seemed to have been the deceased's peculiarity, and when the granddaughter attempted to give her some it dribbled out of her mouth, for possibly the poor old lady had then gone. She seemed to have passed away quite peacefully, and in the absence of medical evidence he suggested to them, as men of the world, that the deceased died from heart failure and senile decay. If they were satisfied with the evidence he sug- gested that should be their verdict. The jury agreed and returned a verdict a4K-.3rdingly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>HIGH LEGH.</head>
        <p>HIGH LEGH.</p>
        <p>Scarlet Fever.—Owing to the prevalence of scarlet fever in High Legh it has been decided to close the school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>GREAT SAN KEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SAN KEY.</p>
        <p>Sunday School Procession.—The scholars of the St. Mary's Church Sunday School held their annual procession and fete on Walking Day. The procession was headed by the Preston Brook Band, the Vicar (the Rev. J. H. Jones), Mr. F. T. Newbold and Mr. E. J. Dumbill (churchwardens), and the Sunday school office bearers. All the infants of the Sankey school carried baskets of flowers, and the Mission decorated crooks. The Sankey scholars marched to Penketh, where they were joined by the Mission children, making in all about 600. They then walked through the village, and along Warrington-road to Sankey Bridges and back again to Sankey schools, where tea was served to the children, the Vicar enter- taining the teachers at the Vicarage. After tea the children went to the Vicarage Fields for the sports and various other amusements. Dancing was specially favoured by the seniors, music being provided by the Preston Brook Band.</p>
        <p>Bandsman Injured--During the children's sports on Walking Day an accident occurred on the Vicarage Field. A young bandsman from Preston Brook, whilst standing up in one of the swinging boats, was thrown out. Al- though badly shaken and bruised, he was not seriously injured, and after attention and rest he was able to go home.</p>
      </div>
