      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>TABLE TALK.</head>
        <p>TABLE TALK.</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute oi Interesting</p>
        <p>Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSING.</p>
        <p>It is well known that it becomes fatiguing to curry a heavy weight in the band, such -as a valise, for any considerable distance, especially because it hinders the movement of the lower limbs. A Frenchman has found a way to pre- vent this inconvenience, which iS as simple as it is practicable, and all that is needed is to put under the arm a bulky packet, which is made up of a dozen newspapers folded together. In this way the handbag is far enough removed from the legs so as not to binder walking with ease- He says that the fatigue is much less and the weight appears to be lighter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>Uncorking a Bottle.</head>
        <p>Uncorking a Bottle.</p>
        <p>It often happens that in attempting to uncork a bottle, the stopper is forced into the bottle instead., and it thus floats on the liquid. This would perhaps not be a drawback were it not that each time the bottle is to be emptied the cork comes to the neck and causes an obstruc- tion, preventing the flow of the liquid. This can be avoided very readily, and all that is needed is to bend a piece of stiff iron wire in a long U-shape, properly fitting it in the neck of the bottle so that the loop portion projects soniewhat below the neck. Upon overturning the bottle the wire loop prevents the cork from reaching the neck to obstruct it.</p>
        <p>Peat Dust as Engine fuel.</p>
        <p>The fact that Hi von Porat, a Swedish engin- eer, has discovered and perfected a process for utilising peat powder as fuel for locomOtives has awakened great interest in Sweden, and a. num- ber of the railways in that country are preparing to use this new material instead of coal. In the von Porat system the peat powder is fed by an automatic process into the furnace of the loco- motive, which is specially arranged to consume it. Of his system Mr. von Porat says:—" The same results can be obtained from 11 tons of peat powder as from one ton of coal. As to firing, the work is almost nothing in comparison with firiog with coal, because the powder is forced into the furnace by automatic process. Another advantage is that no cold air can get into the firebox, and neither smoke nor sparks escape from the smokestack."</p>
        <p>Blue Beads as Charms.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Lovett in the course of a recent lantern lecture on the " Folk-lore of London," said many children of the poorest people wore a neck- lace of common blue beads as a charm against colds and bronchitis. He had himself gathered 67 independent records of this custom in all parts of London. The beads were worn under the clothing, put on in infancy and not taken off throughout life. He had seen several old women over 00 years of age who had worn the blue bead necklace from infancy. It must never be taken off on any account day or night. Be suggested that this custom might account for the great number of beads often found in ancient tombs.—" Science Siftings."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>Telephone Call for Sleepers.</head>
        <p>Telephone Call for Sleepers.</p>
        <p>If you want to catch an early train there is no need now to buy a special alarm clock; if you want, in fact, to do anything at any par- ticular time there is no need to rely either upon your own efforts or upon the possibility of Eliza Jane failing to call you. All you have to do (says the "Evening Standard ") is to ring up from your own number the supervisor of your own exchange. You tell her your number, and state that you want to be called, say, at 7.30 to- morrow morning, or, indeed, at any time of the day or night. The request is docketed by the supervisor and given to the operator, who rings your bell at the appointed time and keeps ring- ing it until you answer. For this service the charge of 3d. per call is added to your account.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>Artificial Wood.</head>
        <p>Artificial Wood.</p>
        <p>Information has been made public in Lyons, France, concerning an artificial wood, which, it is stated, will be of great value as a substitute for natural wood. The new product has been found after .years of study and practical experi- ments, the most recent of which have given eminently satisfactory results. The process con- sists in transforming straw into a solid material having the resistance of oak. The straw, after being cut into small pieces, is reduced to a paste by boiling, to which certain chemicals are added. When the paste has been reduced to a homo- geneous mass it is put into presses, and planks, beams, laths, and mouldings of all sizes are IleadilY made. This new material can be sawed like natural wood. As a fuel, it emits a bright flame and little smoke. It is further stated to ,be adaptable to the manufacture of match stems.</p>
        <p>Cure for Smoke Trouble. The laboratory of the chemist offers a solution for the big city's smoke problem, says "Science Siftings," but the cost as at present computed is too great for all plants to have it. . If an elec- tric spark is sent through the smoke, the smoke gathers in flakes and falls down immediately. This is shown in the laboratory by means of punk candles (candles giving a vile looking smoke) set burning in a glass jar fixed to the two terminals of a spark coil. The smoke rises as usual without the charge; but with it in operation it is condensed on the sides of the jar, and also falls in heavy flakes. The reason is the same that governs atmospheric phen- omena. It is a matter of common knowledge that after a big flash of lightning a heavy down- pour of rain takes place. Tie several little drops combine to form larger drops under elec- tric influence, and also more drops dre formed. Smoke consists of small particles, just as a cloud consists of small water particles. A trap could be made outside a factory chimney for the flakes, and a spark gap could be arranged ac:•oss the chimney to form the flakes; but the cost of maintenance would be a good Rol.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST</p>
        <p>—(continued).</p>
        <p>some distance before being discovered; then, having neither ticket nor money, he was of course unceremoniously bounced off there and then. Tramping forward to the next station he contrived again to stow himself away, but after awhile was kicked off as before. That having been repeated in the midst of. a prairie, he proceeded on foot until another opportunity for a cheap ride presented itelf. As he dragged his aching limbs toward a station, a compas- sionate stranger gave him some food and ques- tioned him respecting his prospects and desti- nation. "Where am I going?" repeated the weary one; "l'm going to New York if my trousers' seat 'll hold out!" •</p>
        <p>Well Coined. Being on my holidays, I have just re-read an article on " Some words and phrases" lately contributed to these columns, and I was re- minded of an amusing incident of the bygones. Years and years ago, I was walking along the main street of a small town in the West Riding when a little girl accosted me with, "Oh, please. do come; there's two boys fustling." Noticing that the lads who had so terrified the poor mite by their "scrap" had already separated, I pointed this out for her comfort, and went on— "But, tell me, what did you mean by `fustling'? Wherever did you get that funny word from?" And with a look of mingled pity and rebuke, my little maid exclaimed, 'Don't you know about Jacob fustling with the angel?"</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURDAY,JULY 11, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>Literary Gleanings. AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHY.</head>
        <p>Literary Gleanings. AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHY.</p>
        <p>CHANGING LONDON.</p>
        <p>I recently had the good fortune to introduce a little party of visitors to some of the historic spots of the Metropolis. One was a girl who had spent the whole of her life near London, and another one of those "country cousins" whose mission in life seems to be to make the Londoner find out something about his own city. They came without their guide-hooks, but with that requisite of the successful traveller—the Eye of Appreciation. Farringdon-street Station, on the Metro- politan Railway, is about as unromantic a spot as one could hope to find, yet we remember that the illustrated papers of a generation ago pre- sented their readers with pictures of it, and dilated on the marvels of the new terminus of the Underground Railway! In spite of it all. Farringdon-street, to hith who knows,, is full of historic interest. On its eastern side there stood in old times the grim Fleet Prison, where brave rebels of a former day met the conse- quences of their defiance of authority and made possible the liberties we now enjoy; where, too, the Fleet marriages were celebrated, and,, the debtors languished and longed for a better day —as the Dickens lover knows.</p>
        <p>But we turn away from Farringdon-street, and on the west enter Hatton Garden, the abode of the goldsmiths and diamond-cutters. Who of the thousands who pass Hatton Garden know its history? Less than four hundred years ago it was indeed a garden, where grew the most lovely roses. Twenty acres of it belonged to the Bishop of Ely. He had his residence there, and his church—since sold to the Roman Catholics, is to be found in Ely-place. Alas! in that age of freedom the Bishop's garden fell under the rapacious eyes of Sir Christopher Hatton, whom Queen Elizabeth had made Lord Chancellor. The Queen listened to the pleadings of her favourite, and requested the Bishop to give the Chancellor a portion of his fair garden. The Bishop naturally demurred, whereupon the Tudor Queen sent him this angry note:— Proud Prelate,—You know what you were before I made you what you are now. If you do not immediately comply with my request. I will unfrock you, by God! ELIZABETH." This letter had the desired effect of inducing the Bishop to resign a large portion of his estate, but he reserved to himself and his suc- cessors free access, through the gate-house, of walking in the garden, and leave to gather 20 bushels (if roses yearly therein. Twenty bushels of roses gathered on Holborn Hill! We wonder if the Bishop still collects them.—From an article by Montague Herbert' in the " Realm."</p>
        <p>THE INSURANCE ACT AT WORK. The concensus of testimony as to the office inefficiency of the London Insurance Committee Will, it may be hoped, lead to drastic action. A very large part of the unpopularity of the Act arises from difficulties and delays in getting the benefits. It is part of the same inference that the Insurance Commissioners should inter- vene more promptly and more actively to pro- tect the insured person from injustice. Under the scheme of the Act, as it worked out in prac- tice, the insured person has often no other pro- tector. His approved society, the agent, the sick visitor, the panel doctor, even the Insur- ance Committee, are apt to be useless, or worse than useless when his complaint is that he has been wrongly treated by one or more of them. Whether or not we would have it so, in this vast *scheme it is from the Insurance Com- missioners, and almost from them only, that the poor and ignorant insured person can obtain effective protection. It was difficult to get reli- able information on the question of small de- lays, but there are, without doubt, a number of • cases where • benefit is not received until the twelfth or thirteenth day.- The vast majority of the insured. have no further sum put by against kickeess (nor was it contemplated that the administration of the Act would render extra voluntary insurance almost a necessity), and many are able to get no credit, or, at the best, a few days' credit only, at local shops. Meantime, the patient is probably ordered extra nourishment by the doctor. The ten days' wait is already a grave matter, and the further delay, even of a day, in the receipt of the expected money, a delay which probably seems a small administrative blunder at the head office of a big Approved Society, may involve hardship amounting to cruelty. These instances of small delays never reach the ears of those in autho- rity.</p>
        <p>When the delay is extended to a week, a fortnight, or three weeks, the patient and his or her dependents may well be nearing desti- tution. l'oet eases of five weeks' delay are occurring.</p>
        <p>It is practically impossible for a sick visitor (with no medical training, often a promoted agent) to ascertain, in one or two brief visits, the state of the patient' A health. One case of heart disease was reported by an agent, where the sick visitor had made the patient, who died filteen days later, gat out of bed. A .doctor spoke of a woman with varicose veins for whom he had ordered complete rest, who was made by the sick visitor to get up and walk about the room. The activities of sick visitors need rigid restriction, and other arrangements for the detection of malingerers would he advisable.</p>
        <p>It was insisted on by all the medical men in- terviewed that a large proportion of the sick- ness was due to bad teeth, for which no treat- ment is provided. If the prevention of sickness is, as is stated, the aim of the Commissioners, the provision of dental treatment would seem to be essential and urgent.—Mr. Sidney Webb and Miss Rose Gardner in the "Contemporary Re- view" for July.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>RAG-TIME.</head>
        <p>RAG-TIME.</p>
        <p>It is a curious fact that persons of a small amount of musical culture often seem to have a difficulty in appreciating syncopation, while trained musicians and entirely unlearned rustics meet here on common ground. Musical synco- pation is a temporary disturbance for artistic purposes of the regular flow of rhythmical accents. It is a familiar part of the stock-in- trade of the great composer; it is a striking feature in Hungarian music; it can sometimes be heard in sailors' chanties. Only the mediocre composer and the mediocre performer seem to have no use for it. We remember in the 'sixties of the last century hearing that a professional musician said to a lady amateur, "You can play syncopation? Then you must be a good musician." . _</p>
        <p>The amusing title of "Rag-time" conferred on this well-known species of rhythm looks as if it had only recently been "discovered" by the average amateur on the other side of the Atlantic; and that be was as pleased with his discovery as is the London street arab, when, on being taken to the country, be discovers that flowers grow along the roadside. We are afraid, however, that our American cousins were fore- stalled by a few centuries. In Spitta's "Life of Bach" we read that Vivaldi, the violinist, who died in 1743, was supposed to have "invented syncopation; that it became popular in his day under the name of the "Lombardi° style," and that Bach and others imitated Vivaldi's use of it. But students of composition have always known syncopation under the 'name of the " Fourth species of Counterpoint"; apparently no one seems to have recognised that the "Lom- bardia style" was the same thing in smaller notes. Again, an instance of there being some- thing in the name by which the thing is called. Moreover, the students were not the only per- sons who knew the secret; the Ploughman in the Pitzwilliam Virginal book (formerly called Queen Elizabeth's Virginal Book), whistles more than half his merry air in what has ' hitherto been called syncopation, but which we now know should be called rag-time.</p>
        <p>The greatest rag-timist of the nineteenth cen- tury was Schumann. A Cambridge don once remarked to the present writer that Schumann sometimes carried syncopation to such a length as to incur a danger of the original accentua- tion being lost to the ear.—Marcus Woodward, in the "Evening Standard."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>Sir Benjamin Stone's Life-long Devotion.</head>
        <p>Sir Benjamin Stone's Life-long Devotion.</p>
        <p>A GREAT AMATEUR.</p>
        <p>Born in Birmingham about the same time that photography was born, that indefatigable arnal'mr, Sir Benjamin Stone,' followed the art all his life, and made himself one of the most distinguished of unpaid workers with the camera. One of his favourite ambitions was to obtain a photograph of every noted person he met, and for over twenty years he took por- traits of politicians and Members of Parlia- ment. It must be very pleasant to be able to follow one's hobby without let or hindrance, especially the hindrance of poverty, and the late Member for East Birmingham used his opportunities and his good fortune well. It IS many years now since the I' Strand Magazine" brought the political side of his work to public notice, almost all his "sitters" being posed standing at one of the gateways of the House. He leaves a legacy of a thousand negatives of politicians alone, and as' he travelled exten- sively from China to the Amazon, and had kings and rulers of almost every Country for his 'subjects," his photographic collection must be absolutely unique. It shows with how little genius a man who will follow a single line may make a worthy reputation. Sir Benjamin held the office of President of the National Photographic Record Association (I believe he was the founder) and urged the importance of establishing a national collection of photographs of historic events, himself pie- senting to the House of Commons a series of pictures of distinguished foreigners who had visited St. Stephens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>WARRINGTON MONUMENTS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MONUMENTS.</p>
        <p>O'Leary's Statue, Queen's Gardens,—The best aspect of this fine monument to South Lancashire men who fell in South Africa is from the south-east, and therefore early morn- ing ought to be chosen as the time for expos- ing. lf a box camera is used the best make is that which will allow the use of a swing- back, such as a Frena. The longest tripod that can be obtained ought to be brought into requisition by those who cannot command a swing-hackpost-cards arrangement, and then a very small stop is necessary, and a long exposure. Many f the photo st-cards give one the idea that of the soldier is falling backwards, showing that the operator has had to tilt his box backwards, too. It is by no means an easy subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>SNAPSHOTS.</head>
        <p>SNAPSHOTS.</p>
        <p>Colouring Prints With Dyes.—Dissolve the aniline colour (Id. packets of dye will do) in from loz. to loz. of water to a Id. packet, and for glossy prints add a little gum. If the work affects the gloss when finished, rub the print over with a piece of wool, slightly mois- tened with a solution of wax in benzole. To Spot Bromide Prints.—Mix together Indian ink and Payne's grey until the colour matches that of the film. Three Golden Rules to be observed with fix- ing of plates, &amp;c.:- 1. Note how long it takes the white emulsion to disappear; let the plates remain in the fixer as long again: 2. Always rinse the fingers in water; do not be content with wiping them merely. 3. If hypo is splashed or spilled about the dark room, mop it up with a cloth; do not let it dry up itself. The Red Book, 1914-15.—The fifteenth annual issue of the pocket book of the Affiliation of Photographic Societies contains the features which have been customary for some years past, the chief of which is a directory of affili- ated societies with their officers, headquarters, and times of meeting. Other contents are a series of tables and formulee, a list of lectures, and a gazetteer of brief information of sub- jects for photog,raphers in many parts of the United Kingdom.</p>
        <p>SILVER-STREET.</p>
        <p>"A DIRTY TRICK..O -----</p>
        <p>A remarkable story of a bicycle was related at Northwich, when Ernest Banks, chemical labourer, and William Ramsden, basketmaker, both of Oak-street, were fined 20s. and costs for stealing a wheel belonging to Alfred Nlycock, an iron turner, of Salford. The prisoners were on a. fishing expedition at Pickmere Lake, and Banks's wheel going wrong, they substituted the sound wheel from Prosecutor's bicycle. The Bench described the theft as " a dirty trick."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>IN TORTURE WITH ECZEMA.</head>
        <p>IN TORTURE WITH ECZEMA.</p>
        <p>93 Essex, Eng.— " The complaint Acacia - road; W ."oalntelitiomf the types of eczema. It started by a small pimple which festered, then broke and spread to the size of a penny. The back of my leg broke out into a raw patch, and on getting dry would leave gaping cracks which caused terrific pain while walking and used to itch terribly during my sleep. I used to , scratch and wake up with it perfectly raw. It began to spread more rapidly, and I suffered ,terrible torture- I would co'nstantly scratch it while walking; it was as if red hot irons were burning the flesh. "A friend of mine, seeing the agony I was in recommended Cuticura Soap and Ointment. I sent for a sample of each. The irritation rapidly decreased, and after seeing and feeling the relief obtained I purchased more. Now, after a month's treatment, Cuticura Soap and Ointment have cured, my skin is renewed and still re- maining clear." (Signed) A. Norris, Jan. 22, 1914. Although Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists throughout the world, a sample of each with 32-p. Skin Book will be sent post-free upon request. Address . post-card: F. Newbery di Sons, 27, Charterhouse-square, London, E.C.</p>
        <p>LETTERS THAT PLEASED. My Dear Children,— I have received letters which have pleased me very much, and they show that although you are now enjoying the summer time you have not forgotten the Children's Corner. The first one is a nice letter from our prize-winner, who writes:—</p>
        <p>"Dear "Uncle Jack,—l received my lovely prize, and I must thank you very much indeed. I know it will interest me, as 1 always like books about girls. When I have read it I will tell you bow I like it. Thanking you again for your kindess.—Your loving niece,</p>
        <p>MAGGIE RANDLES." The other letters are from two new members who wish to join our Corner. They are:— JOSEPH KITCHEN, 41, Bewsey-street. :MAURICE ARCHER, 11, Heath-street.</p>
        <p>I have much pleasure in enrolling them as members, and on behalf of all give them a moat hearty welcome.</p>
        <p>Your loving</p>
        <p>UNCLE JACii.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>CONUNDRUMS.</head>
        <p>CONUNDRUMS.</p>
        <p>How many weeks belong, to the year?—Forty- six; the other six are only lent (Lent). What history does a tallow chandler under- stand best?—The history of grease (Greece). Why is a bread and biscuit baker similar to a cripple?—Because be is never without twists. What relation is the door-step to the door?— A step-father (farther). What did. Adam first plant in the garden of Eden?—His foot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>CULT OF FERN-GROWING.</head>
        <p>CULT OF FERN-GROWING.</p>
        <p>I am sure that every reader is a lover of plants , and flowers, and that the readers of this corner also love to grow them. In towns, however, it is sometimes rather difficult to have a garden, and so one has to resort to flower-pots in the house. Of course, all plants do not grow in pots very well, but you can get a good many very beauti- ful ones well suited for growing in a pot. The fern, for instance, seems to be a great favourite with most people, and it can be grown very well in single pots, especially if it is cov- ered over with a bell glass. May is about the time to buy your fern—and be sure you buy it in a good market, even although you pay a little dearer for it than you would from a street vendor.</p>
        <p>It is good to begin with some common wayside fern, as then you can better imitate the situa- tion in which the fern grows. • .</p>
        <p>When you get your fern (be sure it is young and sturdy,), plant it in a pot Measuring at least six inches across the mouth, in peat soil, mixed with sharp sand and some leaf mould, and let it stand at a window facing the north. Do not keep the soil in a soaked state. but' give it Plenty of water. If you grow your fern under a bell glass, it is a good plan to immerse it in water once a week for half an hour. Twice a week, however, is necessary in midsummer. Let the water flow over the edge of the pot, and then allow it to drain off for an hour, when you may return it to the window.</p>
        <p>If you grow your fern in an open pot. it will require more watering, but in winter do not water it at all.</p>
        <p>With good attention you are sure to grow a beautiful fern, which should keep green nearly all the year round.—Yours sincerely, FERN.</p>
        <p>MY HERB GARDEN. [By E. S. }lonNo.) There is a corner in my garden Bordered by a sweet-briar hedge. Where the herbs, together gathered Close beside the tinted edge.:</p>
        <p>Mingle all their fragrant odour Sage and balsam, hay and rue, Sending forth sweet-smelling incense To the freshness of the dew.</p>
        <p>Thyme has crept with verdant tendrils Round the sundial's mossy base, And the rambler roses cluster On its lichen-covered face, Where for years the shadow finger Stole the passing hour away, From the earliest glimpse of sunrise To the last faint gleam of day. . . • Rosemary's tall sturdy brandies, Covered with pale lilac flowers, Scent the dewy eve With fragrance, While the petals fall in showers. Lavender beside the roses Breathes a voiceless hymn of praise To the Author of her beauty Through the drowsy summer days. Mint and southernwood; their perfume Blending in the shadowed light. Soft exhale their lulling scent-songs On the breezes of the night. Golden wallflowers gaily cluster O'er the velvet pansy's bed, By the old brick wall, which sunset Paints a richly glowing red. Low the breeze of twilight murmurs Round and through my garden swan; ,Darker, longer grow the shadows, Peace and fragrance reign o'er all.</p>
        <p>THE LITTLE CIRCUS-GIRL.</p>
        <p>" Neils! Neils!"</p>
        <p>The shrill voice rang through the CaraVUTI, and the required person, who was a little girl about twelve, hastily rose from the grass, where she was making a fire, and entered the dirty room. Go and fill this with water," said the gipsiy ' woman, handing a pail to the girl. Nella. sighed. This was her life--drudgery and work. Her white, pinched cheeks told of want, her dress being torn and dirty. From early morning till the circus Perform- ance at night she toiled for these gipsies, who were her guardians. They had been, great friends of her dead parents, who were also of gipsy birth. Nella had long, black curls, which hung about her shoulders, and her face was lit up with large brown eyes. When she returned with the water she was told to perform many other duties. After she was free she had to prepare for the circus, slipping a spangled dress over her thin form. "Mayn't I be free to-night?" she pleaded to her guardian, the circus proprietor. "I feel , so ill."</p>
        <p>"No," he said, brutally. " 11l or not, you will have to appear." Soon afterwards the time came for Nella to enter the ring. Her duty was to walk along the tight rope, and when she commenced she was feeling very sick and giddy. She had walked unsteadily to the cen+re of the rope, fearful lest she should fall, when a great faintness overpowered her, and she fell from the dizzy height to the ground below. Im- mediately a crowd gathered round little Nella, among them a well-dressed lady and gentleman. The lady gently raised the girl's head and forced some water through the white lips. The hazel eyes opened and gazed, full of surprise, at all the faces. " Are you feeling better now P' said the lady, Whose name was Mrs. Walton. "I am just a little shaken," replied Nella. who then remembered what had occurred. Her guardian and Mrs. and Mr. Walton went with her to the caravan. _</p>
        <p>"This life is not fit for ber; she is so deli- cate," said Mrs. Walton to the ginsies. "Would you like to live with my husband and I?" .Ite asked, turning to Nella.</p>
        <p>"Oh," said Nella, " I should love 'to." "May she?" said Mrs. Walton to the gipsies. They were doubtful at first, but when Mr. Wal- ton gave them a large sum of money, they readily consented.</p>
        <p>_ Nella was taken to live in a large house, with her new parents, who had no children of their own. From the drudgery of her former life she afterwards lived in rest and erafort.</p>
      </div>
