      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>+4,00++4•... 0:00416:•••,•?•••:*</head>
        <p>+4,00++4•... 0:00416:•••,•?•••:*</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S WORLD.</p>
        <p>up his own establishment in a small palace near that of his father, King Constantine, and is said to be a suitor for the hand of the beautiful Princess Elizabeth of Roumania, whilst rumour has also selected him as a probable husband for one of the daughters of the Czar of Russia. The Widow's Mourning Veil.</p>
        <p>The changes that mark the mourning habili- ments of women to-day as compared with those of even ten years ago are all for the better, from the point of view of comfort and practical com- mon sense. And in nothing is this change more apparent than in the widow's garments and headgear. Until quite recently the woman who mourned the loss of her husband was con- demned by custom to wear a style of millinery that was nothing short of a penance. Composed of the heaviest, blackest, stiffest crape, the ' widow's bonnet was as ugly and uncomfortable a species of headgear as custom and tradition could devise. The addition of a long, ungrace- ful crape veil—generally reaching to the knees —added a weight that in hot weather became a burden to bear, and was most funereal in appear- ance. Happily this penetential form of mourn- ing no longer obtains, and wiser, saner fashions have taken its place.</p>
        <p>The French Veil.</p>
        <p>French women, on the loss of a relative, near or distant, have for generations, as a sign of mourning, worn a long, light veil made of tulle, chiffon, or any thin, filmy material. A modified form of this graceful appendage is now donned by our younger widows, on a hat, preferably of the wide-brimmed variety. When picturesquely draped, the veil is most becoming and suitable alike to the youthful and to the elderly. Being light in effect and therefore more elegant, the widow's bonnet of to-day may claim to be an artistic confection, when compared with its pre- decessor. Filmy black materials, and the great improvement in the make of crape, have helped to render the widows' dresses lighter, more hygienic, and less funereal than formerly. The Newest Tunics.</p>
        <p>The deep polonaise tunic is extremely fashion- able, a new design that is less cumbersome round the hips than the pleated model having a yoke that fits the figure at the top. The skirts under these tunics are extremely narrow at the feet. Naturally the yoke tunic requires very careful fitting, but it is suited to the figure given to embonpoint. The long tunic is un-</p>
        <p>a doubtedly a graceful style especially suited to the tall and slim. More material is used in dress skirts of all kinds; the ultra-narrow type has happily disappeared from the centres of fashion; and will soon be no more seen. Some models show a double tunic separated by a flounce, while many lace and lingerie dresses have skirts composed of two or more flounces. In fact variety, individuality, and a blending of many periods and styles are the chief charac- teristics of this summer's modes. Collars and Blouses.</p>
        <p>It would seem that designs for collars must have long ago been exhausted, but, on the con- trary, every day sees a new shape, or a variation of some old one. Turn-down collars of lace or embroidered muslin, deck coats and corsages of every style and make. Many people are fortu- nate enough to possess embroidered or other kinds of collars, worn during the early years of the nineteenth century. As these are just now the height of fashion, they should be rescued from their hiding places and proudly worn. The sales offer golden opportunities for laying in a stock of these indispensable accessories at a merely nominal cost, and advantage should be taken of the chances 'afforded the busy woman who has no time to spend on the making of these dainty trifles that give light touches to dresses of all kinds. Blouses are more delight- ful than ever before, and a new design that is put over the head—for it has no fastenings— I ought to prove a boon and a blessing to the home dressmaker, since it is cut in one piece and so quickly made. The "Middy" blouse, too, is admirable for wearing at the seaside, especially when made of white drill or duck I and trimmed with a sailor collar of the same. edged with blue braid, and finished with a blue silk tie. Scotch Pearls.</p>
        <p>A young Scotch lady, on attaining her majo- rity, has been presented by her father's tenants with a handsome necklace of native pearls. which are found both in Scotland and Ireland. These gems were greatly admired by Queen Vic- toria, who was always willing to purchase a good specimen. A fine black pearl was recently found in the Tay. Native pearls have none of the sheen of those coming from the Orient, but are treasured for their opal tints of delicate rose and find many admirers. Several society women possess fine specimens of these pearls, which were greatly prized during the Roman occupation of Britain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Royal Widows.</p>
        <p>Two Royal widows, the Empress Marie of Russia and Queen Olga of Greece, are on visits to Queen Alexandra at Marlborough House, whilst the Dowager-Duchess of Saxe-Coburg- Gotha is visiting Princess Henry of .Battenburg at Kensington Palace. Queen Olga is a member of the Russian Imperial house and widow of the brother of Queen Alexandra. who was so cruelly assassinated towards the close of the Turko- Grecian War. She did much to further the native industries of Greece, more especially the making of the beautiful laces of Crete and Cyprus, which find a ready sale with tourists and visitors to Athens. Queen Olga's daughter- in-law, the present Queen of Greece, has also taken the greatest interest in this industry, as in various others, notably sericulture. which has lately been revived in the 3forea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>Versatile Queen.</head>
        <p>A Versatile Queen.</p>
        <p>Queen Sophie.of Greece, who is the youngest sister of the German Emperor, is almost as versatile, and quite as energetic as her Imperial brother, whose favourite she w.,-4 until she adopted the faith of her hush unl. The es- trangement happily no longer exts, and Queen Sophie earned the gratitude of her adopted country by her successful efforts to win the Kaiser's sympathy for Greece rather than for Turkey during the war with that country. The active part the Queen took in nursing the wounded will not be readily forgotten, and since the war ended Queen Sophie has com- pletely reorganised and very greatly improved the military hospitals throughout Greece. She has also induced a number of Greek girls to take up nursing as a profession. To the educa- tion of her children, the youngest of whom is a girl barely a year old, she has devoted much time and attention. As this Princess was born during the war, she very appropriately was given the Army and Navy as her godfathers. The eldest son is very popular, and has just set</p>
        <p>"We are giving our pastor a new drawing-room carpet on the too esFeesive." " Here is the very thing, Madam, rf-al Kidderminister."</p>
        <p>something that looks nice, but isn't</p>
