      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>UNWOMANLY POCKET.</head>
        <p>THE UNWOMANLY POCKET.</p>
        <p>A writer in the "New York Tribune" has been so much impressed by the weightiness of anti-Suffrage arguments that she holds that they should by no means be restricted to the task of preventing women from obtaining votes. They embrace a far wider sphere. They prove, not merely that women should not have votes, but that they should also be denied many other un- feminine privileges which are too lightly granted to them. For instance, why should women be permitted to have pockets? Miss Alice Miller emphatically declares that she is opposed to pockets:—</p>
        <p>" (1) Because pockets are not a natural right: (2) Because the great majority of women do . not want pockets. If they did, they. would have them. (3) Because whenever women have had pockets they have not used them. (4) Because women are expected to carry enough things as it is without the additional burden of pockets. (5) Because it would make dissension be- tween husband and wife as to whose pockets were to be filled. (6) Because it would destroy man's chivalry towards woman if he -did not have to carry all her things in his pockets. (7) Because men are men and women are women. We must not fly in the face of Nature. (8) Because pockets have been used by men to carry tobacco, pipes, whisky flasks, and compromising letters, and there is no reason to suppose that women would use them more wisely."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>RUNCORN CONSTABLE'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CONSTABLE'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>The death took place on Monday evening, at the age of 26, of Andrew Collier, who had been for the last three years a member of the Cheshire Constabulary and stationed at Run- corn. A native of Barrow, near Chester, he was of fine appearance, being 6ft. liin. in height, and weighing close on 17 stone.</p>
