        <p>his sympathy with their troubles, Mr. Bowskill was listened to as a mediatdr, and prevailed upon them to lay down their arms—what no Portuguese ever would have done. After his return from the native conference Mr. Bowskill and two of his native helpers were thrown into prison on the charge of inciting the natives to rebellion. After considerable effort, the mis- sionary was released. the British Consul exoner- ating him from all blame, or from any attempts to disturb the natives. Mr. Bowskill now awaits a civil trial, when he will have counsel from Europe. Most unjustly, the two native helpers, who are Christians, are still imprisored, suffering hunger and cruelty. The latest advices from San Salvador are that a military guard is placed around the house of Mr. Bowskill, and that all mails to the missionaries have been in- tercepted and opened.</p>
        <p>She was inclined to be excited?—No, it was him,_</p>
        <p>been so much hxmorrliage?—lt would have been more local. Mr. Steel: You found no injury to the throat? —Note at all. You heard the witness Lawless describe how Harris got his wife by the throat. If so, you would expect to find some traces of injury on the throat?—Yes.</p>
        <p>WIFE'S PAINFUL LOT.</p>
        <p>When she had a glass' or two was she not inclined to be excited?—She never took very much.</p>
        <p>When you came home with her why 'did not you go to your home ?—Because she asked me to stop..</p>
        <p>A Pathetic Case of Long Sun</p>
        <p>Did you anticipate there would be bother because she was away so long?—Yes; I expected that by his face. The Coroner: If Harris was asleep on the sofa when you Went in how could you tell he was excited?—When he got up from the sofa. Mr. Steel: You were asked to go into the house and sit with her?—Yes. He jumped uP from the sofa as soon as we got in. Do you know he had been lying on the sofa from 5.30 till nine o'clock waiting for his wife to come home for tea?—He said so.</p>
        <p>11, A curious story was unfolded at an 13rir on Monday afternoon, conducted by Mr. S. house, when inquiries were made into t' t 1 cumstances surrounding the death of El S. Kent, wife of Zakariah Kent, a puddler' Pierpoint-street. The husband said that his wife, who ie years of age, had been troubled with 10ber her head, and at times had been strange 'e4 manner. On October 17th last year he ret,A 6 home from work late at night and 11.ef-t, the, strange noise behind the front door, yr"- 60 result that he went to the back of the hogttife opened a window with the aid of his pen'tbe In the house he found his wife lying °,ufisf; floor with a' cut in her -neck, and there pool of blood on the kitchen floor. He 413"tlie a neighbour, sent for a doctor, and infornieullie police. His wife made no statement ,otoe next day she was removed to the Work'ytill Hospital and was there until Decernher,pok, when she was discharged. She was veil' and on Sunday, June 28th, she became veil/ again and died at 7.30 a.m. on Thursday, 2nd. unife</p>
        <p>Baptist congregations throughout the United Kingdom have forwarded strong resolutions on behalf of the native helpers and the mission- aries to Sir Edward Grey, and it is hoped that when pressure is put on the Foreign Office justice will once more be done on the Congo.</p>
        <p>Do you know he had been asleep?-lle looked dazed.</p>
        <p>You did not -tell us about him going out of the house?—He did not go out. I put it to you she began to quarrel and he went out of the house to avoid her?—He did not go outside the door. I put it he stood outside half an hour, and, when he cazne back, she began to quarrel again? —No, she did not.</p>
        <p>When he pushed her the first time he pushed her on to a chair?—Yes. Then she jumped up as she was excited?— No; it was him pulling her down that she did not like.</p>
        <p>She was excited and jumped up?—She did not seem much excited.</p>
        <p>In reply to the Coroner, witness said the ltrro4 was an ordinary table knife and was lYing the kitchen floor. The Coroner: Was it covered with hl°' did not pick it up. fo/' Had she ever attempted to do this be it Never. clo Was there any reason why she shoulu then?—No. 4 • No trouble?—None whatever. The husband further stated that IV- Doi: troubled with pains in her head before "ewe' ried her. She was blind in one eye and 11 blind in the other. oft,e,,r Detective-Sergeant Holland stated ths"diciu" the woman had attempted to corn/311! spolice; she was removed to the Infirmary by tl'e e 01, and after watch had been kept on her found to be insane and was remove° Workhouse. Pernicious Anemia. e ev Dr. Nightingale, resident surgeon at tj„idtal'-rtdt house Hospital, said ,the woman was aC to the hospital on October 18th with throat, and was discharged on Deceraberry, cll The Coroner: As a result of the lnju she lose a great deal of blood?—Yes. titoli°;;, And did it materially affect her conaditiae —Yes, taking into account her general Co fed Do you think the wound she i.ilte'jeathiier- herself hastened or accelerated' „as 01-tbe Probably it hastened her death. S 11- ing from pernicious anemia and Pr° Li loss of blood would. receice Might she have died if she had n°t this wound?—Probably. sa"` he cliid The Coroner, in summing up, raj •tteu t.lPf think it safe to say the deceased ea?, hat ..,'pot cide. The doctor said " probablY, jfe (110,40, found the verdict on actual facts. d'etor, "rly• say that disrespectfully against the ° cleased he thought, had given his evidence !"erZaece'lfer His (the Coroner's) suggestion was thtai't „fog died from "pernicious anemia, ,h due the jury to decide. „. to rerpicious anwinia." nor,- The jury returned a verdict of "</p>
        <p>Why did she get out of the chair and go across the room to him?—She did not want to be pushed down. What did she say?—l do not remember. Did she raise her arm?—No; he jumped up, and that was when he struck her. When she jumped up she was very vexed with him?—l expect she was. I should have been vexed.</p>
        <p>Can you remember if she threatened to do something to him?—No. Have you known her to have fainting bouts? —No. She said she had been nervous.</p>
        <p>Harris referred to fainting fits when he said, he had seen her like that before P—l have never seen her like that before. BANANAS AND WHISKY.</p>
        <p>_ During the time you were out had you any- thing to eat?—Yes, sir. Where did you get anything to eatP—From a shop in Winwick-street. We had fruit.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : What did you have?—We had some bananas.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel: Bananas and whisky?—Yes. The Coroner: I do not know what would be the result of that combination.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel: You had no proper food?—No. BRAIN H/EMORRHAGE.</p>
        <p>Dr. Reginald Smith, police surgeon, reported the results of a post-mortem examination He found several external bruises, which were done recently, probably within 24 hours of death. The bruises might have been done at the same time. There was no injury to the throat to indicate that the woman had been throttled. From an internal examination he found a widely-spread hteraorrhage on the brain. No fracture of the skull was discovered. Death was due to the brain hremorrhage. He thought the fall be- tween the chair and the sewing machine would bring on the hoemorrhage. Mr. Hallaway : If it had been caused by the bursting of a blood vessel there would not have</p>
        <p>Moo</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>DETECTIVE'S STORY.</head>
        <p>THE DETECTIVE'S STORY.</p>
        <p>Detective-Sergeant Holland said he was called to the house on Saturday morning and found the woman dead. Hannah Lawless was there and made a statement. This was read over in the presence of Harris and he said: "Yes, I cannot deny it. It is quite true." Later Harris said: "I have had some trouble since I married her, and I never thought it would come to this." When charged at the Police Station, Harris said: "I cannot say anything; it's too horrible."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>GOOD CHARACTER.</head>
        <p>A GOOD CHARACTER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel said Harris had been in Warrington for 21 years. He came when the Ship Canal Was being cut, and he was employed on the Ship Canal for 17 years. He was later employed by Messrs. Crosfield. He was 52 years of age, was a very sober. man, and there was nothing against him,</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said Harris was well- behaved, and the police had never had a com- plaint against him.</p>
        <p>THE VERDICT.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said the question for the jury was to ascertain if there was sufficient evidence to send the man for trial on a charge of man- slaughter. The woman had not died from natural causes, • as Dr. Smith had made very clear. The luernorrhage which had caused death was due to injuries received, and the jury would have to find if the injuries were inflicted by Harris.</p>
        <p>The jury, after about 15 minutes' absence, returned a verdict that the woman died from haemorrhage on the surface of the brain, caused by a fall, but there was not sufficient evidence to show how the injury was sustained.</p>
        <p>HARRIS BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES. Mr. A. T. Hallaway appeared to prosecute at the Police Court on Monday, when Harris was charged on suspicion with causing the death of his wife, Lena, by striking her or choking her, in the house, 21, Thelwall-lane, on Friday. Mr. Hallaway: I appear for the police in this case, which is the outcome of a disgraceful scene between husband and wife on Walking Day, during which the woman fell and her head was either thrown against the wall or she fell into it. The result of the squabble is that the woman is dead and the man, I understand, is represented by Mr. Steel, who cannot be present to-day. In view of the fact that the inquest will be held this afternoon, I do not propose to ask for any evidence, but to ask for an adjournment for a week, when the ease will be brought up again. If a certain verdict is brought in by the Coroner's jury the case will come up at once. I understand Mr. Steel con- curs with that.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Dr. Joseph) : Demanded for a week.</p>
