      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>OPPRESSED BAPTIST MISSIONARY. Serves the Portuguese and is Then Imprisoned.</head>
        <p>OPPRESSED BAPTIST MISSIONARY. Serves the Portuguese and is Then Imprisoned.</p>
        <p>LOCAL REPRESENTATIONS.</p>
        <p>Copies of the resolution recently passed at Golborne-street Baptist Church relative to the imprisonment of the Rev. J. S. Bowskill and his native helpers at San Salvador have been sent to the Foreign Secretary, Sir Edward Grey, and the local member of Parliament.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. S. Bowskill is a Baptist mis- sionary upon the Congo, and has been in that service since 1899. Hence he is a man of tried abilities. For some time native labour has been recruited for the cocoa plantations of San Thome and Princepe. The missionaries, as in the Congo atrocities, dared the Government in supporting the natives against recruiting that was little better than slavery. As Belgium, in the case of the rubber atrocities, resented missionary support of the natives, so now at San Salvador the missionaries have been arrested and punished for their endeavours to alleviate the lot of the natives. Matters were brought to a head between the Portuguese Government and the natives by the action of one of the local chiefs. He had promised not to recruit any further for the cocoa plantations, and any natives he secured should not be passed on for this forced slavery. He betrayed his trust to his fellow-natives, and sent forced labour to the cocoa plantations at San Thome. The other chiefs took up arms against him, and there was evidence of very serious trouh. around the Portuguese settlement. The natives refused to listen to any Portuguese intervention. No Portuguese dared to approach them for fear of their lives, and the Governor in his distress applied to Mr. Bowskill. Known for his work amongst the natives, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <p>. „• Orr I! TRIP tc's, -% ';`,l" Ar 3 • THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 11, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>4.4. 4.4. 4.4.</head>
        <p>4.4. 4.4. 4.4.</p>
        <p>MEMS. FOR MOTHERS.</p>
        <p>Feedinu Bottles and Diarrhea.</p>
        <p>If every mother could only realise the terrible risk of artificial feeding she would make every possible effort to keep herself fit so as to be able to nurse her baby. Now that summer is with us the question of bottle-feeding is an acute one, because of the dust and flies which are every- where so abundant. The following paragraph is from the report of Dr. W. Stewart Stalker, and the warning is, we are sorry to say, greatly needed :</p>
        <p>" Infant diarrlicea is the almost invariable 'result of artificial feeding without regard to the infinite care and cleanliness which must be observed in the administration of food. The dirty home, the defective bottle, the uncovered milk, the house-fly, and the privy, each contri- bute their potentialities in the distribution of death from zymotic enteritis. The rearing of infants by other means than breast-feeding is an artificial process brought about by artificial conditions of life and perpetuated by custom, example, and convenience.. A Signal Failure. _</p>
        <p>The human race would appear to be the only species of the mamalia in which signal failure of the pyschological process of milk secretion is in evidence, and it cannot be denied that psychological rather than pathological pro- cesses are at work in the production of such an unhappy result. The force of example is great and the desire to adopt customs which appar- ently lessen responsibilities and ties—apparentl?' is said advisedly, because in reality responsi- bilities are enormously increased by artificial feeding—is in the main but the acceptance of an immoral doctrine which example, as opposed to rational teaching, has expounded."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>COOKERY CORNER.</head>
        <p>COOKERY CORNER.</p>
        <p>Some Simple Summer Drinks.</p>
        <p>Sherbet, that old favourite of our early cliihl- hood, can be easily made at home. Mix half a pound of sugar and sixty drups of essence of lemon, and dry thoroughly. Ad.l four ounces of tartaric acid, four ounces of carlyniata of soda, and pass all through a fine sieve. Bottle and cork tightly. Allow ona teasooonful to every tumbler of water. Sodaic Powders.</p>
        <p>To obtain perfect soda-water instantaneo.isly in its utmost perfection, take five drachms of citric or tartaric acid, pound it finely, and divide into twelve parts, folding each in %%bite raper. Take six drachms of carbonate of soda. pound it finely and fold in blue uaper. Half fil two tumblers with water, stir into one a powder from the white paper and in the other one from the blue. When the powders are quite dissolved pour one into the other, and you will at once have perfect soda water.</p>
        <p>Lemon Squash. • Have you ever tried making your own lemon Squash? It is not at all impossible and decid- edly cheaper if you want a good brand, and your own you are sure of, if any, are you not? Very well, then, take the juice of eight lemons, sweeten in the proportion of one tablespoonful of sugar to every lemon used. Put into a very clean pan, and simmer gently till the sugar is all dissolved. Beat up the white of one egg, add it to the syrup, and stir well till the mixture boils, let it boil for a minute or two and pour gently through a jelly bag. When cold, add a quarter of an ounce of citric acid, bottle and cork tightly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>DR. JAMES HOPE MOULTON AND THE</head>
        <p>DR. JAMES HOPE MOULTON AND THE</p>
        <p>PEACE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Accepting an invitation to become a vice- president of the Peace Society, Dr. Moulton writes :</p>
        <p>My Dear Sir,—l need not say that I very much appreciate the honour Which the com- mittee of the Peace Society has done me, and your own kind words in informing me. I am coming more and more emphatically towards the Quaker position on the subject of Peace, and indeed I do.not know whether there is any- thing in which Ido not entirely hold it. I also regard it as a matter of absolutely first class importance, and therefore I can associate my- self with the Peace Society with the utmost heartiness. Please express to the committee my great appreciation.—Yours faithfully, JAMES HOPE MOULTON. Didsbury College, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>DAY.</head>
        <p>DAY.</p>
        <p>Woman Dies after a Struggle with Her Husband.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>JURY RETURN AN OPEN VERDICT.</head>
        <p>JURY RETURN AN OPEN VERDICT.</p>
        <p>Tragedy marked the close of the Warrington Walking pay. A married woman, Lena Harris (45), living with her husband, Alfred Charles Harris, labourer, at 21, Thelwall-lane, died under mysterious circumstances, which took Mr. S. Brighouse, the coroner, and a jury nearly two hours on Monday to unravel. The husband earlier in the day was remanded at the Police Court on a charge of having occasioned the woman's death. The allegation then was that on Friday night he had struck and attempted to strangle his wife, but the Coroner's jury returned an open verdict to the effect that the woman had died from hanmorrhage on the brain and that there was not sufficient evidence to show how it was caused.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. S. Steel appeared for the husband, and Mr. A. T. Hallaway (Deputy Town Clerk) ap- peared on behalf of the police. The foreman of the jury was Mr. H. Reading.</p>
        <p>WHISKIES AND TONICS. The chief witness was Hannah Lawless, wife of a puddler, living at 77, Thelwall-lane. Mrs. Lawless said Mrs. Harris was a friend of hers, and they went, about two o'clock on Walking Day, to the Blueeoat School, for the purpose of seeing the daughter of Mrs. Harris, who was an inmate of the school. They remained at the school half an hour, and then went to the British Tar publie-house.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: You had each a glass of beer there?—Yes, sir.</p>
        <p>About five o'clock did you and Mrs. Harris go to the Theatre Tavern, Scotland-road, and did you each have three whiskies and tpnic split?—Yes, sir. You left there about 7.3o?—Yes, that would be about the time.</p>
        <p>Do you mean to say you were in the public- house two hours and a half and had only•three drinks?—Yes. A cousin of Harris is there, and we stopped talking with her about friends. You got to the house of Harris about nine o'clock.—Yes.</p>
        <p>The husband was then lying on the kitchen sofa P—Yes.</p>
        <p>--I--n what condition was Mrs. Harris—drunk or sober?—Sober.</p>
        <p>Were you aide to form an opinion as to the condition of Mr. Harris?—Yes, he seemed to be dazed.</p>
        <p>Was he drunk or sober?—l cannot tell you. He was excited. He wanted to know where his wife had been and she said " You know where." What did be say?—lle said: "What time do you call this for coming home?" What did she say?—" It is not late and you know where we have been."</p>
        <p>THE QUARREL. I suppose then they quarrelled P—Yes, Where was Mrs. llarris at this time?—Stand- ing on the hearthrug. What did Harris do to her P—Pushed her, and</p>
        <p>she fell on a chair. He said "Sit there!" Did she sit there?—No. she got up from the chair and went to him on the other side of the room. _</p>
        <p>What did he do?-11e jumped p struck her, and pushed her between the sewing machine and the chair. What did he strike her with?-11is hand. Opened or closed?—l cannot tell you that. On what part of the body did he strike her?— I cannot say. I got between them. She fell down and her head struck the wall. What happened after that?—lle got hold of her by the throat. Whilst she was on the floor?—Yes, sir. What did you do?-.-I pulled him away and threw him on the sofa. I then picked up Mrs. Harris, as I thought she had fainted. Did you ask the husband to do something?— I asked him to fetch water, but he would not. What did he say when he refiised?-11e said: "Leave her alone! I've seen her like this before." Then lie brought a jug of water and threw the water all over her.</p>
        <p>"YOU HAVE KILLED HER." You said something to him?—l said: "Good God, Mr. Harris, you have killed her! She is dead!" He said: "I'll soon bring her round." Then lie threw a mug of water at her. A son came in and I asked him to go for a doctor; another boy came in and asked what was wrong with his mother. I said I did not know, and he called the man a murderer, and said: "I thought y would come to this." _ . _</p>
        <p>Two of you left at two o'clock and came back at nine o'clock. and during the seven hours you visited two public-houses and in one you had a glass of beer and in the other you had three whiskies and tonics?—Yes; it was Walking Day. She was talking to her cousin a long time in the last public-house. Could the husband see she had had some drink?—He did not say. Mr. Hallaway : Did you tell Detective-Sergeant Rolland that while Mrs. Harris was standing on the rug Harris struck her several times about the face and head?—No, sir. The Coroner: Did you say, in fact: " I would not like to tell you half he did to her "?—No. Did you say: "I tried to separate them, but could not do so "?—Yes, I said that. I said he struck her and she fell between the chair and the sewing machine. Mr. Hallaway : Did he strike her while she was standing on the rug?—Yes. Mr. llallaway (to the Coroner) : The statement of the witness was read in the presence of Harris and he said: "Yes, I cannot deny it; it's true."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>CROSS-EXAMINATION.</head>
        <p>THE CROSS-EXAMINATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Seel: How long had you known this lady? —About eight years. When you have been in Harris's house did they seem to get on very happily9—Yes, sir.</p>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Some Useful Information.</p>
        <p>A good remedy for muscular rheumatism is: Take a hot air bath two or three times a week, using the bath cabinet. Avoid tea, coffee, and cocoa, as well as alcoholic beverages and meat, including fish. You can take lemonade or orangeade freely, especially if you are inclined to stoutness, but if you are thin take them very sparingly. • [Goitre and Its Cure.</p>
        <p>It is held to-day that the only cure, if it can be called a cure of this disease, is to excise it, and when the disease is simple, that is, not complicated with any nervous symptoms, the result is good. Sometimes one finds that great benefit, amounting to a cure, results from the use of some of the animal extracts, like a thyroid or thymus gland, and friction of the part itself with Red lodide of Mercury Ointment. but every case must be treated with care by a medical man who has examined it well. Stoutness and Nervousnesi.</p>
        <p>Stoutness and nervousness, as well as tired- ness, often accompany each other, with, in addi- tion, anemia, especially in women. In such cases what is wanted is first to treat the amemia with some form of iron, and then to diet the patient so that fat may be lost. In addition several miles must be walked each day in the I open air. A diet like this would soon reduce fat in a suitable case. Breakfast, three ounces of fish, a little dry toast and a cup of tea with saxininstead of sugar. Lunch, four to six ounces of lean meat, with one potato and a little fruit. Tea in the afternoon, with saxin and no food. Supper, four ounces lean meat or fish, or ham, with one piece of dry bread.</p>
        <p>THE GIRL WHO WORKS.</p>
        <p>She Rivals in Many Ways Her Sister Who is In Society.</p>
        <p>According to Mrs. Frederick M. Smith, who is prominent in New York society and club life, many of the girls who at an early age are forced into the working field, are, without doubt, the superiors of many society girls. " They possess more character, as a rule, more tact, more in- genuity," states Mrs. Smith. 'A girl who is naturally of a strong character is helped by being thrown on her own resources. " Clothes, of course, make a material differ- ence. Take a poor girl who is. graceful—and nowadays the majority, even the poor ones, are— put her into a pretty gown and she becomes identical with her sister of the '400.'</p>
        <p>"The girls who go forth daily to earn their living are usually neat, chic and well groomed. They take better care of themselves than the society girls. It is required that they be at work at an appointed time each day. So necessarily they have a regular hour for rising, and cannot attend an interminable number of functions that would keep them up in the small hours. "Society girls spend half their days in bed and half their nights at dances. Needless to say, the little working lassie, whose gala nights are few and far between, presents a far more blooming appearance than the fagged debutante of a gay season. _ _</p>
        <p>"While clothes go far toward making the woman, they do not complete her by any means. They add, they do not make—the real woman must be in the clothes and not a puppet; the natural refinement must be there or it will not take long to discover the fraud. "A pretty girl in a dream of a gown, possessed of a good figure and the accompanying erect carriage, will command homage, granted, of course, that she be refined and ladylike. "It is quite rare to find a working girl who does not pay considerable attention to her per- sonal appearance. Nowadays the inexpensive, ready-made suits are cut on the same lines as the highest priced, and the transition from a ..£2 garment to a .R4O one concerns not so much style and fit as material. Even the emigrant girl soon acquires grace and neatness and re- finement is almost certain to follow, provided the girl is ambitious to make the best of herself —and it is surprising how many are."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>TRAGEDY OF WALKING</head>
        <p>TRAGEDY OF WALKING</p>
        <p>DEATH FOLLOWS A FIT.</p>
        <p>Man Collapses in Bridge Street.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse conducted an inquest on Monday on Thomas Cliffe (30), of 677, Knutsford- road, who died as the result of a fall on Satur- day night. Samuel Cliffe, car conductor, in the employ of the Warrington Corporation, said the de- ceased was his brother and resided with him at 677, Knutsford-road. Be suffered from fainting fits and on several occasions had fallen in the street owing to the ireakness of his legs. On Saturday night deceased went to town by car, and about eight o'clock witness saw him, when he was in his usual good health and spirits. When witness reached home at 12.30 on Sunday morning he was informed that his brother had fallen in Bridge-street. Dr. Robin- . _</p>
        <p>son was called and he made about five visits during the night, but despite his attention deceased died at 8.25 on Sunday morning. Edward Chaney, motorman, employed by the Warrington Corporation, of 687, Knutsford-road. said the deceased was his brother-in-law. At 8.40 on Saturday night he was on his car, when he saw a crowd of people in Bridge-street. Be went to the scene and found his brother-in-law lying on the floor. He had suddenly collapsed and fallen. Witness rendered all the assistance</p>
        <p>he-c,-;iiii-,---got.ilie-dece;sed-c; the car, and him home. Dr. Robinson said that death was due to hemorrhage on the brain, the result of the fall. A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
