      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON LOSE.</p>
        <p>Cheetham visited Warrington on Saturday, and with a margin of 17 runs returned home victors. Batting first, the visitors opened none too well, for the first two men only made two and one respectively. However, mainly through the instrumentality of H. Blight (27) and F. A. Foote (21), the Cheetham eleven mustered an aggregate of eight runs short of the century. Warrington opened in promising fashion, but the bowling of H. Blight, who secured five wickets for 17 runs, and W. Bolton, three for six, played havoc with the home team, for after S. L. Partridge had been dismissed for 43 runs and W. Richardson for 11, the scores read: 0,4, 7,0, 0,4, 0,0, 6. This brought the total score to 75. _ _</p>
        <p>For Warrington, W. Bennett was the most successful with the ball. He secured six wickets at the cost of but 17 runs; Hannam, the professional, and S. L. Partridge divided the remainder of the wickets at the price re- spectively of 22 and 30 runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>CONSERVATIVES WIN.</head>
        <p>CONSERVATIVES WIN.</p>
        <p>When the Warrington Conservatives visited Weaverham on Saturday they did not expect to dispose of the home team for 37, and then run up a score almost double that of their opponents. However, that was the case. For the Warrington men the highest score was obtained by C. Holt with 21, F. Lemon and E. Sanders coming next with 16 and 14 runs respec- tively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>THURSDAY TEAM'S FINE WIN.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY TEAM'S FINE WIN.</p>
        <p>Cadishead were entertained at Arpley on Thursday afternoon by the Warrington Thurs- day eleven, and were easily defeated. They were only able to muster 76 runs, a figure al- most reached by J. C. Bradburn, of the home team, who had compiled 75 before he was out lbw to a ball delivered by Taylor. W. Rich- ardson (21) was the next highest scorer for Warrington, whilst J. Hannan came third with 14 runs to his credit. J. W. Wells and F. Cook (who secured seven runs each), F. Par- tridge (6), C. Holt (3), and G. Mynekyme (1), were the only other contributors to an aggre- gate of 137.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>Warrington Thursday v. Cadishead.</head>
        <p>Warrington Thursday v. Cadishead.</p>
        <p>At Arpley on Thursday. Scores:—</p>
        <p>Warrington Thursday.—J. C. Bradburn lbw b Taylor 75, W. Richardson b Holmes 21, J. W. Wells c Taylor b Holmes 7, F. Partridge b Holmes 6, J. Mottram b Bell 0, F. Cook b Bell 7, J. Hannam b Bell 14, C. Holt b Taylor 3, J. E. White b Taylor 0, G. blynekyme not out 1, T. Foster b Bell 0, extras 3; total 137. Cadishead.—E. Holmes run out 3, H. Holmes b Hannam 23, A. Bell b Holt 10, W. Blaekwell c Mottram b Holt 5, J. Taylor b Holt 0, L. Taylor c 3fynekyme b Richardson 5, E. Banks b Holt 5, J. Wrigley b Bradburn 13, John Tay- lor c Hannam b Richardson 6, C. Cooke not out 0, S. Taylor b Richardson 0, extras 6; total 76.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>Warrington v. Cheetham.</head>
        <p>Warrington v. Cheetham.</p>
        <p>Played at Arpley on Saturday. Scores : Cheetham.—C. 11. Sharp b Hannam 2, 11. J. Milsom c Baxter b Bennett 1, F. J. Smethurst c Richardson b Bennett 6, S. Bolton b Hannam 7, C. N. Holt b Bennett 0, F. A. Foote b Bennett 21, H. Blight b Bennett 27, W. Bolton b Part- ridge 5, S. Smethurst b Bennett 3, H. Robinson b Partridge 0, H. Boumphrey not out 7, extras 11; total 92. Warrington.—W. Richardson b Smethurst 11, S. L. Partridge c Holt b Blight 43, G. Woodcock c and b S. Smethurst 0, B. Silcock c Robinson b Blight 4, S. Baxter b Blight 7. Hannam b Blight 0, J. S. Broome b Blight 0, H. Hawthorn c Milsom 13 W. Bolton 4, W.V.Turton not out 0, A. Duckworth c S. Smethurst b W. Bolton 0, W. Bennett b W. Bolton 6, extras 0; total, 75.</p>
        <p>Warrington Conservatives v. Weaverham.</p>
        <p>Played at Weaverham on Saturday. Scores: Weaverham.—H. Collier b Barrow 4, T. Cal- lon b Holt 0, P. Johnson b Holt 2, W. Johnson b Holt 4, C. Inglefield b Holt 2, J. Ryder b Batrow 5, J. Newton St Hunter b Holt, 6, A. Woodward c Sanders b Holt 7, r. Jones c and b Holt 0, H. Newton not out 3, A. Nicholas b Holt 0, extras 4; total 37. _ _</p>
        <p>Conservatives.—E. Sanders run out 14, W. Hunter c and b Johnson 0, J. Constable b Johnson 2, L. Peas c Woodward b Johnson 1, T. Knight b Johnson 2, F. Lemon c and b Nicholas 16, P. Knight c Ryder b Johnson 1, C. Holt c Inglefield b Nicholas 21, T. Williams b Johnson 0, F. Tole b Johnson 3, W. Barrow not out 10, extras 2; total 72.</p>
        <p>• Grappenhall v. Altrincham Wesleyans. _ _ -</p>
        <p>Played at Grappenhall on Saturday. Scores: Altrincham Wesleyans.—J. Percy b Hutch- ings 5, J. E. Cocker c Ford b Hutchings 3, B.* Warrington b Ford 0, W. Worrall b Ford 1, E. Proctor not out 12' A. Shepherd c Parr b Ford 2, W. Hanson b Ford 3, J. Brazendale b Hutchings 1, W. Brazendale 13 Hutchings 0, W. Cottingham c Hatton b Hutchings 0, P. Gibbon c Hutchings b Ford 0, extras 1; total 28.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall.—J. Ford b Proctor 8, T. Wood- ward b Proctor 3, C. G. Hutchings c J. Brazen- dale b Worrall 7, H. E. Hankey h Proctor 0, A. E. Evans b Proctor 5, C. A. Miller b Proc- tor 6, J. Peters hit wicket b J. Brazendale 1, A. H. Hatton b Proctor 0, V. Darbyshire b Proctor 0, C. Hough b Proctor 0, F. Parr not out 0, extras 4; total 34.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>MOTOR NOTES.</head>
        <p>MOTOR NOTES.</p>
        <p>When the question of plain tyres as distin- guished from nonskids comes up for considera- tion—at the beginning of the season let us say, or when spares are being laid in on the eve of a long tour—many motorists appear to forget that their cars are four wheeled vehicles. That is to say, there is often a strong tendency (origin unknown) to provide an outfit of Michelin Square Treads for the front wheels only. The backs wheels similarly are treated to an un- varying accompaniment of Nonskid. But why limit Square Treads in this way? Two nonskids on the back wheels are an excellent equipment; but this does not imply that a nonskid and a Square Tread, or two Square Treads, are never to be used on. the rear wheels.</p>
        <p>The 3ficlielin Square Tread is well able to put up an excellent working record on back wheels, i simply because t is made for back wheel work— as you will appreciate when you come to exam- ine the details of its construction. To begin with, it is moulded m one piece. There are no i joints; the tread s not made separately and vulcanised on to the body. This feature in itself makes for far greater strength than that enjoyed by any all-rubber cover made in several parts. Then again the tread of the Michelin Square Tread is strongly compressed. When inflated, the tyre presents a broad square surface of tough compressed rubber of remarkable thickness. With its beautiful resiliency it makes easy riding, and with its tremendous strength and toughness it makes mileage to satisfy;the heart and pocket of the most ardent tourist. Not every road and not every car Fy any means demands a full. nonskid armoury. There- fore take the foregoing remarks also into ac- count and use Michelin Square Treads—or at least make one of the pair a Michelin Square Tread—on the back wheels of your</p>
        <p>BIBENDUK.</p>
      </div>
