      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>An Ultimatum From the Masters and a Reply By the Men.</head>
        <p>An Ultimatum From the Masters and a Reply By the Men.</p>
        <p>POSITION MORE SERIOUS.</p>
        <p>Fresh developments have ensued in connec- tion with the Tanners' Strike. The mas- ters have sent the men an ultimatum, which they say is their last word, and the men have issued a reply. Last night a complete dead- lock had been reached and what the next few days may bring forth it is impossible to say.</p>
        <p>THE MASTERS AND PROPOSED CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Conditions Stipulated.</p>
        <p>UNION DISTINCTIONS NOT TO BE MADE.</p>
        <p>All Men Cannot Re-start.</p>
        <p>The Master Tanners' Association met on Monday at Warrington to consider the letters which had been written on behalf of the men, and the following reply was forwarded at the close or the deliberations :</p>
        <p>however, allow the union men who are now working at some tanneries to remain at work, whilst other members of the union have coin- .pelled other tanneries to close."</p>
        <p>The Strike Committee had considered the masters' letter, and if the course proposed therein was adopted the union would make every effort to bring out on Monday other tanneries— including Posnett's, Runcorn—which are linked with the Masters' Association.</p>
        <p>`; This is no idle threat," he added, "there are not 20 non-union men in the whole of the dispute area."</p>
        <p>MEN "WILL NOT CONSENT."</p>
        <p>Bth July, 1914. To Mr. C. H. Mitchell, Liverpool, Dear Sir,—ln answer to your letter of to- day's date I refer you to my previous letter, which distinctly states that the members will not consent to the employers' conditions con- tained in your letter of the 6th inst. My committee are still willing to meet the Master Tanners' Association, and discuss the whole question, when they feel sure a settlement agreeable to both parties can be adopted. Trusting you will make the necessary arrangements.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>EDWARD ORMSBY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>MASTER TANNERS' ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>MASTER TANNERS' ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>22, Lord-street, Liverpool. July 6th, 1914. Mr. Ed. Ormsby, General Secretary, Tanners'</p>
        <p>Union.</p>
        <p>Your letters of theist:2-n- d and 3rd inst. were brought before a meeting of the Master Tanners' Association to-day.</p>
        <p>The association' are pleased to hear that a proper understanding exists between your mem- bers, but they desire an assurance that it also exists between the members and your union, and that you have power and authority to bind the men.</p>
        <p>In view of what has occurred in the past, and so that there may be no misunderstanding, I am directed to inform you that certain conditions must be accepted by your union and its mem- bers before any meeting can be arranged. The present unsatisfactory position cannot be allowed to crop up from time to time in the tanning trade, as it dislocates business and causes heavy loss. Your union have materially helped to bring the present position about by their policy of supporting the men who downed tools. In our opinion a refusal of strike pay would have kept the men in check better than any other way. However, in order to prevent the existing state of affairs from arising again, my association feel bound to stipulate that the following conditions shall be agreed to before any meeting takes place:- 1. The men when they return will do so sub- ject to uniform rules to obtain in all the yards. One of these will be that 14 days' notice must be given before leaving. 2. No distinction can be drawn by the em- ployers between union and non-union men. The union to give an assurance that no attempt will be made to coerce men into joining. The union and the.members to undertake that no strike will take place at any yard because men decline to be- come members of the union, and that non- union men who have remained loyal to the masters shall not be molested or interfered with. 3: The employers cannot take back all the men who came out. Some works will not require as many men in the future. The employers reserve to themselves the right to refuse to take back any man or men they may not wish to employ. On hearing that your union and the members are prepared to agree to these conditions my association will give you an early appointment for a sub-committee representing your union to discuss a settlement with a sub-committee of my association.</p>
        <p>-As to the Liverpool strike, practically all the master tanners are now united with the other districts into a large association, and all corre- spondence should be conducted through me. At the present time I do not think any object would be gained by a meeting. The men came out on strike and the union have countenanced them in their, action. Now that the master tanners have formed an association comprising the various districts they feel that it is unfair for certain tanneries to be closed through the strike of your members whilst union men are working at other tanneries. They therefore propose to take united action and to put all tanneries on the same footing. My association do not in the slightest degree desire to crush your union, as has been suggested, nor do they in any way object to treat with the union. They cannot, however, allow the union men who are now working at some tanneries to remain at work, whilst members of the union have compelled other tanneries to close, causing great loss to the master tanners. Notices will, therefore, at once be given to all union men terminating their employment. If you desire to meet ray asso- ciation afterwards a sub-committee which has been appointed will discuss a settlement. I am directed, however, to explain that an end cannot be brought to the Liverpool strike with- out a settlement of that at Warrington. The master tanners mean to bring both disputes to a termination at one and the same time. Before the settlement of the strike at Liverpool can he discussed we shall require an assurance that the three conditions above set out with regard to Warrington will also be agreed to with regard to Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>Yours faithfully, (Signed) C. H. MITCHELL.</head>
        <p>Yours faithfully, (Signed) C. H. MITCHELL.</p>
        <p>MAYOR THANKED, BUT-</p>
        <p>On Tuesday the Mayor (Councillor Peter Pea- cock) received the following letter :</p>
        <p>Orford Tannery, near Warrington, July 6th, 1914. To His Worship the Mayor of Warrington, Town Hall, Warrington. Dear Mr. Mayor,—l brought your letter of the 3rd instant before a meeting of the Master Tanners' Association to-day. In reply I am directed to inform you that while the association sincerely thank you for your kind offer, they regret they cannot avail themselves of your services as mediator. The tanning trade, as you are no doubt aware, is lull of intricacies, which, in our opinion, can only be dealt with by the parties themselves. The association very much appreciate the interest you have taken in the matter, and the desire you have to bring about a settlement.</p>
        <p>Yours faithfully,P H. PERY DENSHAM, Chairman of the Master Tanners' Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>MEN HOLD HURRIED MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>MEN HOLD HURRIED MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>"Prepared to Fight for Ever."</p>
        <p>Upon the receipt of the masters' ultimatum mass meetings of the men were hurriedly organ- ised, when the employers' conditions were read. The Warrington men said the conditions were outrageous, and they resolved at their meeting, which was largely attended, not to entertain them under any circumstances. If necessary, said one official, they were pre- pared to "fight for ever" than go back on such terms.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harrison, organising secretary, in an in- terview with "The Examiner" representative, said that Mr. E. Ormsby, the general secretary, had sent the following letter as a reply to the masters :—" Your letter was submitted to mem- bers, when the following resolution was passed unanimously : 'That the em6loyers' conditions cannot be accepted, until they have been de- bated by our representatives with the Master Tanners' Association. The men are not pre- pared to accept any conditions until they know exactly what they are going .to receive in re- turn. We request, and are prepared, to meet your association at your earliest convenience.'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>MASTERS "VERY INCONSISTENT."</head>
        <p>MASTERS "VERY INCONSISTENT."</p>
        <p>Mr. Harrison said the masters in- consistent in their latest letter, for in .Clause 2 they state that "no distinction can be drawn by the employers between union and non-union men," and later they state that "they cannot,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>MASTERS STILL FIRM.</head>
        <p>MASTERS STILL FIRM.</p>
        <p>Their Conditions Explained.</p>
        <p>The employers sent the following lefter to the strikers yesterday:- 22, Lord-street, Liverpool, July 10th, 1914. Mr. Edward Ormsby, General Secretary,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>Tanners' Union.</head>
        <p>Tanners' Union.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—l am in receipt of your letter of the Bth inst. My association cannot grant a meeting to discuss the whole question until the conditions mentioned in my letter of the 6th have been agreed to. These conditions were framed with a view to preventing the present position from arising again in the tanning trade, and are quite reasonable and proper. Judging by certain statements by you and the officials of the union, as reported in the newspapers, there seems to be some misappre- hension on the part of either the union or its members as to conditions 1 and 2. With re- gard to condition 1, my association are satis- fied, in view of what has happened in the past, and to bring peace in the future, that uniform rules must be framed for all the yards. The essential rule is that 14 days' notice shall be given by the men before leaving. The other rules can be subsequently fixed between repre- sentatives of the union, and my association, when the question of a settlement comes to be discussed. Before a meeting to discuss a settlement can be arranged, the condition that uniform rules shall come into existence, one of which shall provide for 14 days' notice, must be agreed to.</p>
        <p>With regard to condition 2, the object of this is not to smash the union, or to prevent it from carrying on its work. As far as the masters are concerned, they decline to draw any distinction between union and non-union men. The men will be left free to join the union or not, as they please. My association are, however, determined that the union shall not call upon them, as employers, to make any distinction between union and non-union men, and will not consent, in any way, to the coer- cion of their workmen to join the union, nor will they submit to strikes to compel the men to join the union. Of course, the union are at liberty to use persuasion to try and obtain members, but they must agree not to molest or interfere with non-union workmen.</p>
        <p>My association are only asking for fair condi- tions, and cannot grant any meeting to discuss a settlement until receipt of a letter from you that the three conditions asked for are ac- cepted.—Yours faithfully.</p>
        <p>(Signed) C. H. MITCHELL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>ANOTHER STATEMENT BY THE</head>
        <p>ANOTHER STATEMENT BY THE</p>
        <p>MEN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harrison, the organising secretaly, in an interview with "The Examiner" representa- tive last night, said they were quite well aware of the fact that they would have to be governed by certain rules, and they also recognise that one of the conditions would have to embody the principle of giving notice, but they were not prepared to consider the 14 day? sugges- tion until they knew what they were going to get in return. _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Harrison said that with respect to the masters' refusal to accept the Mayor as mediator, the employers said it was owing to the intricacies of the trade and that the mat- ter could only be dealt with by the parties themselves, yet the masters agreed to two other men acting as arbitrators, who were in a similar position as to a knowledge of those "intricacies?' _ _</p>
        <p>It was stated that between 200 and 250 of the men on strike had found casual work else- where.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>DANGEROUS DAWN</head>
        <p>THE DANGEROUS DAWN</p>
        <p>OF WOMANHOOD.</p>
        <p>Girls upon the threshold of womanhood often drift into a decline in spite of all care and attention. Even strong and lively girls become weak, depressed, irritable and listless. It is the dawn of womanhood—a crisis in the life of every girl—and prompt measures should be taken to keep the blood pure and rich vcith the red tint of health.</p>
        <p>If the blood is not in a healthy condition at this critical stage, the body becomes ill-nour- ished and grave disorders may result. If the health of the body is not maintained by the flow of pure, strong blood, all sorts of evils and weaknesses follow, and these may lead to fatal disease. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills have saved thousands of young girls from what might have been life-long invalidism or early death. They are a blood-builder of unequalled rich- ness, strengthening weak nerves and producing the liberal supply of red, healthy blood which a girl needs to sustain her strength. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills have proved their value over and over again to young women whose health was failing. Any dealer can supply Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People; if preferred, Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, will send them direct for 2s. 9d. a box (six boxes for 13s. 9d.). A. book entitled "Plain Talks to Women" will be sent free to any woman who writes to Hints Dept., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, for a copy; it contains much valuable information.</p>
        <p>Funeral of the Late Mr. W. H. Mayers. The funeral of the late Mr. W. H. Mayers, of Priestley-street, took place on Monday after- noon at the Cemetery. The Rev. J. H. G. Hol- lingworth, vicar of St. Barnabas, conducted the service. The mourners were: Mrs. Mayers (widow), Miss Ada Mayers (daughter), and Mrs. and—Mr. F. Dimelow (daughter and son-in-law), Mrs. L. Mayers (aunt), Mr. and Mrs. Billinge. Mr. and Mrs. Chadwick (brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law), Mr. and Mrs. Sharpies (brother- in-law and sister-in-law), Mrs. A. Baxter (sister- in-law), Mr. and Mrs. Grantham, Mrs. France (aunt), Miss Goodison (of Stockport), Mr. A. Downing, Mr. W. E. Stout, Messrs. T. Eccleston, B. Dimelow, and W. Pickstock. The cortege passed through the Cemetery gates between lines of representatives of the "Tom Sangster" Lodge of the R.A.0.8. in their regalia, each wearing an ivy leaf. The members were, as follows: Brothers J, Walsh, K.0.M., K.G.11.; J. W. Tay- lor, K.0.M., K.G.H.; T. Davies, R.0.H., K.G.H.; W. Bruce, K.0.M., K.G.H.; J. A. Woodhead, C.P., C.G.H.; R. Poole, C.G.H.; A. E. Denham, T. Ince, jun., H. Edwards, son., H. Edwards, jun., S. Allen, J. Chrimes, C.G.H.; T. E. Sang- ster, K.G.H.; W. Aspinall, A. W. Turpin (HiP- podrome), P. Lawless, W. Longmore, and W. i Shaw.</p>
      </div>
