        <p>WEST RIDING ASYLUM, WAKEFIELD.— An Active Assistant-Cook required, an experi- enced Cook,General suitable; wages £3O, annual increase 1;1. to £35, with board, lodging, washing, uniform, and boots, valued at £32 for Superannua- tion deductions; no insurance contribution.—Send application, stating age, height, and references to ability and character, to Medical Director. WARRINGTON UNION. Applications are invited for the position Of • PROBATIONER NURSES at the Union Infirmary, Warrington. Salary, ..t9 per annum, rising .t 0 per annum to .215 per annum, together with rations, residence, washing, use of uniform, and attendance in sick- ness whilst in the Infirmary. The Infirmary is recognised by the Local Gc•vernment Board as a training school, and there is a Resident Medical Officer. Fcrms of application may be obtained- POW the undersigned, and the same must be returned not later than 10 a.m. on Wednesday, the 15th July, 1914. Bnieown By order, seoyffi Chambers, BOTTOMLEY, Clerk. Union Offices, Warrington, let July, 1914.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN UNION. ASSISTANT NURSE. An Assistant Nurse (to take alternate day and night duty) is required at the Workhouse at Dutton, near Warrington. Salary: £25 per annum, with uniform, board, washing, and lodgings in the Nurses' Home within the Workhouse precincts. Candidates must have received some training, in general nursing, and the appointment will be subject to the approval of the Local Govern- ment Board. Applications, stating age, experience, and whether single, or widow, accompanied by copies of not more than two recent testimonials, must reach my office, 71, High-street, Runcorn, not later than 10 o'clock on Wednesday morring, the 22nd day of. July, 1914. Any further information may be obtained from the Matron of the Workhouse. GEORGE F. ASHTON, Clerk to the Guardians. Union Offices, Runcorn, 7th July, 1914.</p>
        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. TRAMWAYS DEPARTMENT. The Electricity and Tramways Committee of the County Borough of Warrington invite TENDERS for STEEL TRAMWAY POLES. ' Specification and Form of Tender can be obtained from the undersigned on payment of One Guinea, which will be returned on receipt of a bona-fide tender. Five shillings ner copy will be charged for extra copies of specification. Tenders, addressed to the Chairman of the Electricity and Tramways Committee, Town Hall, Warrington, must be sealed with wax and endorsed "Tender for Steel Tramway Poles," and delivered not later than 12 o'clock noon on Tuesday, July, 21st, 1911. The lowest or any tender will not necessarily be accepted. F. V. L. MATHIAS, Borough Electrical and Tramways Engineer Howley, Warrington. _</p>
        <p>MR. C. MADELEY HONOURED Presides Over the Museum Associa- tion Conference at Swansea. OBJECT OF MUSEUMS. Warrington figured conspicuously at the 25th annual conference of the Museums' Association held at Swansea, South Wales, this week. The President of the association was a Warrington man, Mr. Charles Madeley, the Director of our local Municipal Museum, and a prominent dele- gate was Alderman Bennett, chairman of the Museum Committee. The Mayor of Swansea extended a civil wel- come to the delegates at the opening of the con- ' ference on Tuesday, and spoke of the great ' service the association was rendering to the cause of art. Mr. Madeley, in reply, thanked the Mayor most heartily for the very agreeable manner in which he had welcomed the 'conference to Swansea. They had been looking forward with a good deal of pleasure to the visit to. Swansea, and what they had just heard would only whet their appetites for what they were going to see in the art 'galleries, loan collection, and on the excursion which had been arranged to some , of the ancient castles for which the district is famed. He expressed the particular and extreme gratitude of the delegates to the local commit- tee for the trouble which it had taken to pre- pare—for the meeting, and he thanked them most heartily. (Hear, hear.) THE PRESIDENT'S ADDRESS. s sMoand eltehye theneodtelioNf-e delivered "What is the presidenttrivael The asswiation, he said, had been content athdledlorrry of a Municipal Museum?" e. to take museums as they are, and whilst wel-</p>
