        <p>(fljurrljf/titts. WYCLIFFE P.S.A. At Wycliffe P.S.A. meeting on Sunday Mr. H. Osborne presided. Speaking about "Excuses," the chairman remarked that they were very commonly made, even by Adam, our first parent. He recalled the parable of Christ about the rich man, who invited the people to his feast, and was rewarded by excuses such as having bought land, or oxen, or having taken a wife. People should never make excuses that would not stand the test. In delivering his monthly address the Rev. T. T. James said that the recent walking day reminded one of the important subject of "Child Life," which was now engaging the attention of Governments, Town Councils, and Education Committees. They should take the deepest interest in any scheme which tended to improve the child's surroundings, since it would make for present welfare and future happiness. They should see that everything was done" for he good of the Child; but if it went wrong let them not blame anyone else. There was a mis- taken idea that the child's defects could be attributed to everything around it. At the close of his life, Thomas Carlyle said that although his ideals differed greatly from those of his father, yet his father's life had moulded him rightly. The whole duty of man, he said, was to serve God, and the child under good teach- ings and influences would go aright. Messrs. C. Cotton and W. Beswick also took part in the meeting.</p>
