        <p>indicated the route, the procession crossed Buttermarket-street directly, marched along Naylor-street, round by Cockhedge and out at the Central Station, along Bewsey-street, Gol- borne-street, Bold-street, Barbauld-street, Arpley Cannons, Mersey-street, with several diversions; back into Buttermarket-street, and into church. Many admiring spectators lined the streets as they passed,and applauded As particular sections of the procession pleased them. The Catholic Boys' Brigade, otherwise known as</p>
        <p>the thague of the Cross Cadets, nearly 200 strong, and fully equipped, attracted much attention as they marched along in charge of Father Prim- avesi, their chaplain and promoter, looking very proud of them, and of Captain Price, who is to be complimented upon their efficiency. The corps is "recognised" by the West Lanca- shire Territorial Association, and on the pre. vious Thursday had, at the official inspection in a field near Orford Hall, gained high praisf from Captain Izod, the inspecting officer. In addition to the clergy already mentioned, Father Mawson was present. ST. BENEDICT'S. To the tune of bagpipes the St. Benedict's scholars and teachers slowly wended their way in a semi-circular tour round the neighbour- hood of the church and provided a gorgeous procession. The band, that of the St. Joseph's Industrial School, Longsight, Manchester, looked very attractive in their curious divers-</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CORPORATION. About 600 of the employes in the various departments, of the Corporation spent a very pleasant day at Southport on Saturday. . men were given a free ticket and an allowance and enjoyed the various entertainments at SOuthport. ROBERTSON'S EMPLOYES' OUTING. Down the Canal to New Brighton. There was an element of novelty in the trip, of the employes of Messrs. William Robertson, Ltd., Morris Brook Steel Works, who had their annual outing on Saturday. The s.s. Flying Breeze had been chartered for the occasion and sailed from Latchford Locks down the Manches- ter Ship Canal with about 400 trippers to New Brighton. The usual places of amusement were well patronised, and after a very enjoyable day the return journey was made, the party arriving home about half-pfist ten. The arrangements were in the hands of Messrs. R. W. Monk and H. Bairstow. he outing was so enjoyable that the organ- isers are considering the running of another trip to the same place on August Bank Holi- day Monday. Providing sufficient inducement offers, it is hoped to charter one of the New Brighton ferry steamers, with a seating accom- modation of upwards of 1,100. MONKS, HALL EMPLOYES. Nine Hundred Make Merry at Southport About 900 of the employes from all depart- ments of _Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co.'s Works cJourneyedentrzt stat annualot otSiziniupat.bhop ou trTt7h ean. md piaci d:en idaal tmhtero trains naenjoy- ableys sea- lef</p>
        <p>"A BIT MERRIN." Basket Maker Drowned in the Canal After Drinking. Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest at War- rington on Wednesday on the body of Patrick Quinn (30), basket maker, 558. Knutsford-road, whose body was found in the Bridgewater Canal at Black Bear Bridge on Mondey. James Quinn, brother of deceased, said Pat- rick was a single man and left home with his little niece on Friday to buy her some choco- lates. He left her at the shop, and he (wit- ness) did not see him alive afterwards His brother's cap was found on the canal path. In answer to the Coroner, witness said his brother was used to coming home the worse for liquor, but slept it oil outside somewhere. He was sober when witness saw him on Friday. Arthur Ford, general .labourer, 15, Rock., road, Latchford, said he was in the Brook I Inn on Friday night and noticed deceased drinking with other men at the other side of the room. When the men went Quinn came over to him and, they drank together. Quinn.said : "We are going to Blackpool to-morrow, me</p>
        <p>• and you Arthur," and. he (witness) replied: , "We are not. lam going to New Brighton 1 • with the kiddies." After closing time they , , J talked Tor half an hour. They had all had drink at the time, and he could not remember • nroperly what happened then. Quinn was . about " half-and-half," or what' they called "a • . little merry." , • Margaret Shaw, wife of Joseph Shaw, lock keeper at Manor Lock, Latchford, said Ehe found the cap about 200 yards from where the. • body was recovered. - Joseph Houghton, lahourer, 343, Liverpool- -1 road, captain of the Warrington GrlipPling Corps, spoke to the recovery of the i It was stated that the public-house wh°da,so3.l; 300 yards from where the body was deceased's 1- r It was in the opposite direction .to , . home. low, been known 1 James Quinn said his brother " body was \ . here . . led.rownedt; show how to have slept near the spot w to take his eblre dne! found. He never threatenaed home. i. and was quite comfortab t was A verdict of , • ,; Jt .. 'A , turned, there being --water. deceased got, into ,th-e . ' " Fonuondevidence</p>
