        <p>OUR OCCUPATIONS. Agriculture Still Employs Most Men. CLERGYMEN DECREASE; PRIESTS INCREASE. A Blue Book of over 1,000 pages, which was issued on Monday in connection with the 1911 census returns, gives much information regard- ing the occupation and industries of England and Wales, and shows that exclusive of domestic service, agriculture affords employment to more persons than does any other single industry or service. Domestic indoor service accounts for 1,302,438 persons, of whom only 41,765 are males, and agriculture for 1,229,555, of whom 1,134,714, are males. , The next highest is coal mining with 971,236, of whom 968,051 are males; followed by building, 817,942 (814,989 males). The next is cotton manufacture, in which the figures are Males Females Total 623,82.5 No fewer than 83.2 of the total persons engaged in cotton manufacture, it is stated, ate in Lancashire. Other statistics are :- Total. Males. Females Railway com- panies' service. 542,969 ... 535,799 ... 7,170 Engineering and machine - mak- • ing, ironfound- ing, and boiler- making !Dressmaking Teaching Inn and hotel service Grocery Tailoring Printing, book- binding, and stationery National govern- ment 'Wool and worsted manufacture . ! Boot, shoe, etc , making Drapery In the general, or local,. government of the country, 299,599 persons, comprising 248,624 males and 50,975 females, are employed. The Post Office workers, excluding artisans and labourers (125,698) comprise nearly three- quarters of the total classified to the Civil Ser- vice and nine-tenths of the females. Of the 46,654 Civil servants not employed by the Post Office 61.6 per cent, reside in London or the adjoining counties. There were also another 74,296 males and 2.551 females in Government employment in ship- building works, engineering works, etc. The police numbered 53,160, or 18.4 per cent. more than in 1901, there being one policeman to every 679 of the population as against one to 724 in 1901. Municipal and Poor-law officers numbered 74,087, against 36,870 in 1901, and 24,930 in 1891, the increase of 100 per cent, since the last census being due largely to the development of the activities of local authorities. The total number of employes of local authorities, however, was 588,951, including 1.347 physicians and surgeons, 175,856 teachers, 2,901 charwomen, 31,317 tram- way servants, and 12,697 carmen, etc. Professional occupations and their subordinate services accounted for 714,621 t, or 108,361 more than in 1901. -,There was a decline in clergymen of the Es- tablished Church from 25,235 to 24,350, but Roman Catholic priests increased from 2,849 to 3.302, or 15.9 per cent., largely due to the inva- sion of foreign-born priests. Ministers and Priests of other religious bodies increasea froin 11,572 to 11,981. • • . Roman. Catholics were strongest in Lancashire and Nonconformists in Monmouthshire, while the Established Church was relatively at its best in Rutland, Oxford, and Hereford. Professional Statistics. The comparative totals for the principal Pro- fessions were :- 1901. 1911. Barristers and solicitors ... 20,998 ~. 21,380 Law clerks Physicians and surgeons 22,698 ... 25,048 Dentists Veterinary surgeons • 2,911 ... 2,612 Midwives Teachers (excluding music) 230,345 ... 251,968 Art, music, and drama 102,305 ... 125,006 Authors, editors, journalists, and reporters included 12.030 males and 1,756 females. In 1911 there were 11,619 painters, sculptors, and artists; 5,110 engravers, and 8,921 archi- tects; 16,915 photographers, 47,116 musicians, 9.076 actors (increase 3,028), and 9,171 actresses (increase 2,728), while largely owing to the pic- ture palaces, those engaged in subordinate ser- vices increased from 6.840 to 17,078. SHIP CANAL AND COTTON. The position of the Port of Manchester in regard to the cotton season, 1913-14, which is now coming to a close shows a slight improve- ment. The arrivals of American cotton at the docks from September 1 to June 30 amount to 501,000 bales, as against 500,000 for the corresponding period last year. "These figures," states a writer in the current number of the Port of Manchester official sailing list, "are encourag- ing, for it must not be overlooked that Man- chester's imports are mainly made up of c.i.f. purchases, and that the present season has ex- ceptional difficulties for this style of business owing to the low quality of the American cotton crop." A puffin or "sea parrot," which found a home in. Clifton Park; Rotherham, was killed by a</p>
