        <p>FOOTBALL. • INTERMEDIATE N.U. LEAGUE. The Scheme Now Takes a Definite Form. PLAYERS ASKED TO ENROL. BY "SCRUM." The suggestion thrown out by Councillor Osborne and other speakers at the annual meeting of the Warrington N.U. Football Club that an Intermediate League should be formed for boys who had left school, until they reach the age of 17 years, has been taken up enthu- siastically. Such a League has now come into existence, and it ought to prove a great suc- cess. We used to hear a great deal about teaching the young idea how to shoot ; now we ought to hear more, locally at least, about teaching the young idea how to score tries and drop goals. No time has been wasted in bringing the scheme of an Intermediate League to fruition, and I am officially informed by the secretary pro tem., Mr. E. Jackson Mee, that at a joint meeting of the Warrington F.C. Committee and the Committee of the Schools' Rugby League it was decided to form' such a League. The rapid way the arrangements have been made , indicates that the new body will be hustled into success. THE CONDITIONS. Simplicity marks the conditions under which the players can join teams who will participate in Intermediate League engagements. Some of the points are:— AU boys who are under 17 on the first day of September are eligible to play for the whole of the following season. Players must be members of the team at- tached to their old school, if an old boys' team is established; but where no such team is organised, or when a player is not an ex- scholar of a Warrington school, he will be allocated by the committee to any team in the district of his residence. Jerseys, pants and playing fields will be pro- vided, and mementoes will be given to tile League leaders. The League will be governed by a committee composed of two representatives of the War. rington R.F.C., two ilapresentatives of the Schools' Rugby League, and one representative from , each competing club. HINTS FOR THE BOYS. All boys who are desirous of joining an old boys' team should immediately get to know if his old school is forming an old boys' team. Where a boy has attended more than t. 19 school he is deemed eligible for the school he last attended. During next week several teachers 'sill he calling meetings of old boys, with s view to Joining the League. All boys who are unable to become members of their old school team should write imme- diately to Mr. E. Jackson Mee, 11, Enville- street, the hon. secretary pro. tern., who will be pleased to give information to all who are interested in the League. A referees' list is to be compiled, and the committee would be pleased to receive the names of those who are desirous of refereeing in the League matches. .; ROLL IN YOUR NAMES. It is to be hoped the young idea will rally round their old schools and thus enable many of them to put strong teams into the field. There must be hundreds of ex-scholars in War- rington who love the game, and if they come up to the scratch there should be absolutely no question of the new League proving a great success. I would join my appeal with that of the secretary and urge every boy anxious to play to send in his name. The earlier' he does So the better, and the more who send in the merrier. Many arrangements have yet to be made, and the sooner the committee know the likely num- ber of teams the better can they arrange fix- tures. Personally, lam anxious to see a grand start made with the opening of the season in September. WARRINGTON 'A' TEAM FIXTURES A Well-arranged Programme. The Warrington "A " team fixtures in the Combination Competition have now been isAied. They show a welbarranged list, and should be productive of some good games. The matches as arranged are Dale. Opponents. Ground. 1914. Sept. 5 Leigh 12 Broughton Rangers 19 Rochdale Hornets 26 Oldham ....... Oct.. 3 Salford 10 Runcorn 17 Huddersfield 24 Millorn 31 Nov. 7 Wigan 14 Wigan Highfield 21 Widnes home 28 St. Helens ............. • Dec. 5 Swinton ...... . 12 Pemberton 19 (Xmas Day)2s Leigh ........ ' . 26 St. Helens Recs. 1915. - Jan. 1 2 St. Helens 9 Mihlorn away 16 St.- Helens flees 23 Salford 30 Oldham Feb. 6 Pemberton • 13 Broughton Rangers 20 27 Mar. 6 Swinton 13 Wigan Higlifield 20 Widnes 27 Ap.GF2' Runcorn • 3 Rochdale Hornets E.M: 5 10 Wigan 17 Huddersfield 24 A NORTHERN UNION TRIUMPH. The Tourists Win the Rubber. The N.U. Team won the third test match at Sydney on Saturday by 14 points to 6. There was a gate of 40,000, and the Englishmen, although handicapped through so many men on the injured list, played a dashing game. They have thus won the rubber, winning the first and third matches and losing the second. The tourists in the first half almost com- pletely hemmed in the home team. Wood scored a penalty goal. He failed with four other penalty kicks. In the second half Clarke, and Williams had to retire injured, yet with: 11 men the tourists played splendidly and ran out easy winners.</p>
        <p>LAWN TENNIS. • CROSFIELD'S V. GRAPPENHALL. Played at Grappenhall on Saturday. Re- sults:— • Mixed Doubles.—Mr. Evans and Miss C. Jenks beat Mr. Hesketh and Mrs. Sumner, 6-3, 1-6, 6-4; Mr. Sandy and Miss Jones beat Mr. H. Shakeshaft and Miss Howson, 6-0, 4-6, 6-1; • Mr. Hayes and Miss Antrobus beat Mr. W. Shakeshaft and Miss Thompson, 5-6, 6-3, 6-4; Mr. Barlow and Miss White lost to Mr. Hart- ley and Mrs. Turton, 3-6, 0-6; Mr. Snelling and Miss Burnett beat Mr. Bennion and Miss Warburton, 6-5, 6-5; Mr. Pervical and Miss Dean beat Mr. Holt and Miss White, 6-1, 1-6, 8-6. Ladies' Doubfes.—Miss Jenks and Miss Jones lost to Mrs. Sumner and Miss Warburton, 3-6, 5-6; Miss Antrobus and Miss Burnett lost to Miss Howson and Miss Thompson, 3-6, 3-6; Miss White and Miss Dean lost to Mrs. Turton and Miss White, 6-2, 0-6, 3-6. Gentlemen's Doubles.—Mr. Pereiial and Mr. Barlow beat Mr. Hesketh and Mr. Shakeshaft, 6-2, 6-5; Mr. Evans and Mr. SandY beat Mr. Bolt and Mr. Hartley, 6-2, 61; Mr. Hayes and Mr. Snelling beat Mr. Bennion and Mr. W. Shakeshaft, 6-2, 6-9. Result: Crosfield's won by 8 events to. 4; 17 sets to 12; 135 games to 120.</p>
        <p>BATHS AND GOLOSHES.. An Amusing Discussion.. Two items raised in connection with the minutes of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee submitted to the Tostre Council meet- ing on Tuesday aroused an amusing discussion. Alderman BOLTON, submitting. the minutes, made this cryptic remark: I may tell you that the baths are near completion. Councillor PEMBERTON : Never! (Laughter.) Alderman BOLTON: Yes, they are: (Laughter.) Councillor PEMBERTON : You say getting finished. When are they really going to be finished? (Laughter.) Alderman*BOLTON : They are now really nearly finished. (Renewed laughter.) Dr. Fox said there was really too great a depth of water at the shallow end of the ladies' baths, and it was difficult for young children who went there. He suggested that the depth of water should be reduced. Alderman BOLTON said it was a question of management, and Mr. Barlow would see that no child was in danger. THOSE GOLOSHES ONCE MORE. Councillor ROBERTS asked if it was not desir- able to reduce the charge for the use of the goloshes on the bowlizfg greens. It would be a great benefit to abolish the charge altogether. Alderman BOLTON: If you make a present of the goloshes, Councillor Roberts, we shall be , glad to do away with the charge. (Laughter.) Councillor Ronal's: If you make me a present of the income received from them. (More' laughter.) Alderman BOLTON:' I expected something of that kind. (Laughter.) It cost a lot of money, to pay for the goloshes, and the Committee make a charge at the wish of the men themselves,,but that charge is not so high as that made in other towns. We have to consider the interest of the ratepayers, and if we can get any revenue from the goloshes we are entitled to it. If Councillor Roberts makes a definite offer the Committee will consider it. Everyone now seems to think they ought to have everything for nothing, but I can't get anything for nothing. (Laughter.) • GOLF. WARRINGTON CLUB. ' The captain's medal competition took place on Saturday, when Mr. E. L. Robinson quali- fied in the first division, with 79, net, and Mr. W. Fletcher in the second division, also with 79 net, and the tie for the memento has, there- fore, to be played off. The other scores were as follows:— First Division. Gross. H'cap. Net. Fairclough J. W. Bibby W. Greenhouse T. B. Handley R. Williams Second Division. Gross. H'cap. Net. B. S. Newcombe S. Baxter J. IL Clarke J. Bainbridge E. A. White A. Warburton 0. Lof tho use J. Chalmers THE LADIES' COMPETITION. The qualifying round for the club prize was played on Wednesday. Details : Gross. Weep. Net. Miss Sloane Mrs. Hepherd Miss D. Picton Mrs. E. A. White Miss R. Picton - Miss E. Grimshaw Mrs. Baxter Five cards were not returned. A series of towers each armed with two quick- firing guns for defence against aircraft are to be provided around Portsmouth harbour and elsewhere on the coast.</p>
